7592186045585 true] [17592186220955 1081 "(1)/((s + 2)^{2}) + \\frac{2}{s - 2}" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220955 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220955 1084 #uuid "882e2401-4a01-5cf5-bb9c-e138532e0dd3" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220956 1081 "(2)/((s + 2)^{2}) + \\frac{1}{s - 2}" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220956 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220956 1084 #uuid "8ec9e882-7755-5220-be1e-3c0532391c1f" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220957 1081 "\\frac{1}{s + 2} + (2)/((s - 2)^{2})" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220957 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220957 1084 #uuid "fb0770f1-3bc7-5faa-8b6b-3608ed791e4d" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220958 1081 "(2)/((s + 2)(s - 2)^{2})" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220958 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220958 1084 #uuid "04c6f21a-d149-5481-af6e-b6e5e372daf9" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220959 1081 "(2)/((s + 2)^{2}(s - 2))" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220959 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220959 1084 #uuid "32921f3f-bac8-5fb3-8878-0d99ce52fc52" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220960 1069 "e-19288" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220960 1071 "A triangle has a base length of $12$ feet and a height of $90$ feet. What is the area, in square feet, of the triangle?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220960 1072 #uuid "58b3eb29-e189-52d9-b10d-e94171937d2f" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220960 1074 "To find the area of a triangle, we use the formula\n\n$$\nA = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot b \\cdot h\n$$\n\nwhere $b$ is the base length and $h$ is the height.\n\nWe substitute the given values into the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 12 \\cdot 90 \\\\\n&= 6 \\cdot 90 \\\\\n&= 540ft^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220961 1069 "e-19291" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220961 1071 "The perimeter of triangle $JKL$ is $36$ meters. The length of side $JK$ is $11$ meters, and the length of side $JL$ is $10$ meters. What is the length, in meters, of side $KL$?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220961 1072 #uuid "3d9e0da1-52bb-57e4-a862-c2ad1c290a66" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220961 1074 "To find the missing side of a triangle, we use the perimeter formula, which is the sum of the lengths of its sides. In this case\n\n$$\nP = JK + JL + KL\n$$\n\nWe substitute the known values into the formula and solve for $KL{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n36 &= 11 + 10 + KL \\\\\n36 &= 21 + KL \\\\\n36 - 21 &= KL \\\\\n15 &= KL \\\\\nKL &= 15\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the length of side $KL$ is $15\\,\\text{m}$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220962 1069 "e-19293" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220962 1071 "What is the perimeter, in feet, of a rectangle with a length of $15$ feet and a width of $6$ feet?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220962 1072 #uuid "670c4446-65a9-50e8-8b4b-8acea1f540a9" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220962 1074 "To find the perimeter of a rectangle, we use the formula\n\n$$\nP = 2\\ell + 2w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\ell$ is the length and $w$ is the width.\n\nWe substitute the given values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP &= 2(15) + 2(6) \\\\\n&= 30 + 12 \\\\\n&= 42\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the perimeter is $42$ feet." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220963 1069 "e-19294" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220963 1071 "Each side of a square has a length of $40$ units. What is the perimeter of this square?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220963 1072 #uuid "1ac2d1db-3e8d-5dbb-a7cd-58f4646dde71" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220963 1074 "To find the perimeter of a square, we use the formula\n$P = 4s$,\nwhere $s$ is the length of one side.\n\nWe substitute the given side length into the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP &= 4 \\cdot 40 \\\\\n&= 160units\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220964 1069 "e-19306" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220964 1071 "The side length of square $P$ is $8$ meters. The perimeter of square $Q$ is $5$ times the perimeter of square $P$. What is the side length, in meters, of square $Q$?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220964 1072 #uuid "78258c07-885e-5254-a3ff-e81e19e19b4a" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220964 1074 "We are told that square $P$ has a side length of $8\\,\\text{m}$.\n\nTo find the perimeter of square $P$, we use the formula for the perimeter of a square\n$P = 4s$.\nSubstituting $s = 8$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP &= 4 \\cdot 8 \\\\\n&= 32m\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $P_Q$ be the perimeter of square $Q$. We use the fact that it is $5$ times the perimeter of square $P{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_{Q} &= 5 \\cdot 32 \\\\\n&= 160m\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the side length of square $Q$, we divide its perimeter by $4{:}$\n\n$$\ns = \\frac{160}{4} = 40\\, \\text{m}\n$$\n\nSo, the side length of square $Q$ is $40\\,\\text{m}$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220965 1069 "e-19307" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220965 1071 "![](images/80/80faeaf27df1b53e7abacd1003250c45eb6324c4ad0b594cda006b12ac6b17ab.png)\n\nNote: Figure not drawn to scale.\n\nThe figure shows the lengths, in meters, of two sides of a right triangle. What is the area of the triangle, in square meters?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220965 1072 #uuid "8e5f88cb-17a7-566c-97d8-ba5643b11cd0" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220965 1074 "In a right triangle, we can use the two legs as the base and the height because they are perpendicular.\n\nWe are given that the legs have lengths $10$ meters and $4$ meters.\n\nWe use the area formula for a triangle:\n\n$$\nA = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\text{base} \\cdot \\text{height}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 10 \\cdot 4 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 40 \\\\\n&= 20m^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the area of the triangle is $20 \\, \\textrm{m}^2$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220966 1069 "e-19374" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220966 1071 "A room in the shape of a cube has an edge length of $13$ feet. What is the volume, in cubic feet, of the room?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220966 1072 #uuid "cd7a685a-829d-5a55-af32-12b72c914b42" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220966 1074 "To find the volume of a cube, we use the formula\n$V = s^3$,\nwhere $s$ is the edge length of the cube.\n\nHere, the edge length is $13$ feet. Substituting into the formula, we get\n$V = 13^3$.\n\nNow, we compute the cube:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n13^{3} &= 13 \\cdot 13 \\cdot 13 \\\\\n&= 169 \\cdot 13 \\\\\n&= 2,197\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the cube is $2{,}197$ cubic feet." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220967 1069 "e-19380" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220967 1071 "A cube-shaped toy chest has one open face. Each edge of the chest measures $30$ centimeters. What is the exterior surface area, in square centimeters, of this open-front chest?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220967 1072 #uuid "3c720b40-0333-557c-98c2-8d5cbae35562" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220967 1074 "The chest is shaped like a cube, meaning all its faces are squares of equal size. Since the front is open, it has only $5$ square faces: $4$ sides and $1$ back.\n\nTo find the total exterior surface area $S$, we multiply the area of one square face by $5$. So,\n\n$$\nS = 5 \\cdot s^2\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the edge length of the cube.\n\nNext, we substitute $s = 30$, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS &= 5 \\cdot s^{2} \\\\\n&= 5 \\cdot 30^{2} \\\\\n&= 5 \\cdot 900 \\\\\n&= 4,500\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area of the chest is $4{,}500$ square centimeters." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220968 1069 "e-19383" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220968 1071 "What is the volume, in cubic meters, of a sphere with a radius of $\\dfrac{9}{2}$ meters?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220968 1072 #uuid "f0d8a828-410f-5a1f-8830-4ab0a0f55319" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220968 1074 "We are given the radius of a sphere and asked to find its volume.\n\nThe formula for the volume of a sphere is\n\n$$\nV = \\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi r^3\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the radius.\n\nLet's substitute\n\n$$\nr = \\dfrac{9}{2}\n$$\n\ninto the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= \\frac{4}{3}π(\\frac{9}{2})^{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{3}π \\cdot \\frac{729}{8} \\\\\n&= \\frac{243π}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the volume is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{243\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\ncubic meters." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220969 1069 "e-19390" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220969 1071 "A cube has a volume of $97\\,336$ cubic centimeters. What is the surface area $A$, in square centimeters, of the cube?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220969 1072 #uuid "6c0f6451-987f-5422-9c2e-d91b7ba3d94c" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220969 1074 "We are given the volume of the cube. The formula for the volume of a cube is\n$V = s^3$,\nwhere $s$ is the side length.\n\nLet's set up the equation and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ns^{3} &= 97336 \\\\\ns &= \\sqrt[3]{97336} \\\\\n&= 46\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the surface area using the formula $A = 6s^2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= 6 \\cdot 46^{2} \\\\\n&= 6 \\cdot 2116 \\\\\n&= 12 696\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220970 1069 "e-19391" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220970 1071 "A cube has a surface area of $294$ square centimeters. What is the volume, in cubic centimeters, of the cube?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220970 1072 #uuid "b1c3fa85-3eb5-5006-8548-4f6f3c044eeb" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220970 1074 "We are given the cube's surface a. The formula for the surface area of a cube is\n$A = 6s^2$,\nwhere $s$ is the side length.\n\nWe set up the equation and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6s^{2} &= 294 \\\\\ns^{2} &= 49 \\\\\ns &= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the volume using the formula $V = s^3{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= 7^{3} \\\\\n&= 343\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the volume of the cube is $343$ cubic centimeters." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220971 1069 "e-19392" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220971 1071 "A right circular cone has a volume of $19{,}200\\pi$ cubic inches, and the area of its base is $1{,}200\\pi$ square inches. What is the slant height, in inches, of this cone?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220971 1072 #uuid "3727f8d6-0185-5381-9238-7de85ca1013c" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220971 1074 "We are given the volume and the base area of the cone and are asked to find the slant height. To do this, we first need to determine the height $h$ and the radius $r$, and then use the Pythagorean Theorem.\n\nThe volume formula for a cone is\n\n$$\nV = \\dfrac{1}{3} \\pi r^2 h\n$$\n\nSince the area of the base is $1,200\\pi$, we know that\n\n$$\n\\pi r^2 = 1{,}200\\pi\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into the volume formula and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot πr^{2} \\cdot h \\\\\n19, 200π &= \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot 1,200π \\cdot h \\\\\n19, 200 &= 400 \\cdot h \\\\\n48 &= h \\\\\nh &= 48\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for the slant height using the Pythagorean Theorem\n\n$$\nl = \\sqrt{r^2 + h^2}\n$$\n\nWe already know that\n\n$$\n\\pi r^2 = 1{,}200\\pi\n$$\n\n, so\n\n$$\nr^2 = 1{,}200\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nl &= \\sqrt{r^{2} + h^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{1,200 + 48^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{1,200 + 2,304} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{3,504} \\\\\n&\\approx 59.19\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\nSo, the slant height is $59.19$ inches." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220972 1069 "e-19393" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220972 1071 "A right circular cone has a volume of $\\dfrac{5}{4}\\pi$ cubic meters and a height of $2$ meters. What is the radius, in meters, of the base of the cone?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220972 1072 #uuid "8645581d-394c-59bd-a19c-c4305776c7de" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220972 1074 "We are given the volume of the cone and its height. The formula for the volume of a cone is\n\n$$\nV = \\dfrac{1}{3} \\pi r^2 h\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute\n\n$$\nV = \\dfrac{5}{4}\\pi\n$$\n\nand $h = 2$ into the formula and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{5}{4}π &= \\frac{1}{3}πr^{2}(2) \\\\\n\\frac{5}{4}π &= \\frac{2}{3}πr^{2} \\\\\n\\frac{5}{4} &= \\frac{2}{3}r^{2} \\\\\n\\frac{15}{8} &= r^{2} \\\\\nr &= \\sqrt{\\frac{15}{8}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr &= \\sqrt{\\frac{15}{8}} \\\\\nr &= \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2\\sqrt{2}} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\\nr &= \\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the radius of the base is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\sqrt{30}}{4}\n$$\n\nmeters." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220973 1069 "e-19394" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220973 1071 "A right circular cone has a height of $27$ centimeters $(\\mathrm{cm})$ and a base with a diameter of $8~\\mathrm{cm}$. The volume of this cone is $n\\pi~\\mathrm{cm}^3$. What is the value of $n$?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220973 1072 #uuid "20dba2ad-2be5-55c7-b388-e0aa6eefdb19" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220973 1074 "We are given the height and the diameter of the cone. The formula for the volume of a cone is\n\n$$\nV = \\dfrac{1}{3} \\pi r^2 h\n$$\n\nSince the diameter is $8~\\mathrm{cm}$, the radius is $4~\\mathrm{cm}$.\n\nWe substitute the known values into the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= \\frac{1}{3}π(4)^{2}(27) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}π \\cdot 16 \\cdot 27 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}π \\cdot 432 \\\\\n&= 144πcm^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the value of $n$ is $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{144}$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220974 1069 "e-19395" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220974 1071 "A large pipe is shaped like a right circular cylinder with radius $r$ and height $h$.\n\n![](images/e9/e990ef66ccc992a8d7b1b65e3b3408fc0fc6ba8f5dc6b2e518a26281feeb0125.png)\n\nAnother pipe has a volume that is $162$ times greater than the volume of the first pipe. Find the expressions representing the radius $R$, in terms of $r$, and the height $H$, in terms of $h$, of the second pipe." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220974 1072 #uuid "26f618a5-7f2e-5ae9-9254-3d04b655b7b3" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220974 1074 "We compare the volumes of two cylinders using their formulas, given that the second pipe’s volume is $162$ times greater than the first. Recall that the volume of a cylinder is given by\n\n$$\nV = \\pi r^2 h\n$$\n\nLet the volume of the smaller pipe be $V_1{:}$\n\n$$\nV_1 = \\pi r^2 h\n$$\n\nWe are told the second pipe has $162$ times the volume of the first pipe, so its volume, $V_2$, is\n\n$$\nV_2 = 162 \\cdot \\pi r^2 h\n$$\n\nSuppose the second pipe has radius $R$ and height $H$. Its volume is\n\n$$\nV_2 = \\pi R^2 H\n$$\n\nNow, we set the two expressions for $V_2$ equal and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nπR^{2}H &= 162 \\cdot πr^{2}h \\\\\nR^{2}H &= 162r^{2}h\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we test the choices to find which pair of values for $R$ and $H$ makes this equation true.\n\n- For $R = 36r$ and $H = \\dfrac{1}{2}h{:}$\n$R^{2}H|= (36r)^{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}h; = 1296r^{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}h; = 648r^{2}h\\cdot$\nThis doesn't match the given equation.\n- For $R = 6r$ and $H = 4h{:}$\n$R^{2}H|= (6r)^{2} \\cdot 4h; = 36r^{2} \\cdot 4h; = 144r^{2}h\\cdot$\nThis doesn't match the given equation.\n- For $R = 12r$ and $H = \\dfrac{9}{5}h{:}$\n$R^{2}H|= (12r)^{2} \\cdot \\frac{9}{5}h; = 144r^{2} \\cdot \\frac{9}{5}h; = 259.2r^{2}h\\cdot$\nThis doesn't match the given equation.\n- For $R = 6r$ and $H = 18h{:}$\n$R^{2}H|= (6r)^{2} \\cdot 18h; = 36r^{2} \\cdot 18h; = 648r^{2}h\\cdot$\nThis doesn't match the given equation.\n\n- For $R = 9r$ and $H = 2h{:}$\n$R^{2}H|= (9r)^{2} \\cdot 2h; = 81r^{2} \\cdot 2h; = 162r^{2}h✓$\nThis matches the given equation.\n\nSo, the correct answer is $R = 9r$ and $H = 2h$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220975 1069 "e-19396" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220975 1071 "The volume of a right circular cylinder $M$ is $90$ cubic inches. What is the volume, in cubic inches, of a right circular cylinder with five times the radius and one-fifth the height of cylinder $M$?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220975 1072 #uuid "f7ead7a6-52d1-59df-807f-c31d0f1ab400" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220975 1074 "We are given that the volume of cylinder $M$ is $90$ cubic inches.\n\nThe volume of a cylinder is given by the formula\n\n$$\nV = \\pi r^2 h\n$$\n\nLet's suppose the radius and height of cylinder $M$ are $r$ and $h$, respectively. Then its volume is\n\n$$\nV_M = \\pi r^2 h = 90\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider a new cylinder with radius $5r$ and height\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{5}h\n$$\n\nWe compute its volume as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV_{new} &= π(5r)^{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{5}h \\\\\n&= π(25r^{2}) \\cdot \\frac{1}{5}h \\\\\n&= πr^{2}h \\cdot 5 \\\\\n&= 90 \\cdot 5 \\\\\n&= 450\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the volume of the new cylinder is $450$ cubic inches." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220976 1069 "e-19397" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220976 1071 "A company manufactures cylindrical drums whose height is $1$ inch less than five times the radius. Find the expression representing the volume $V$, in cubic inches, of one of these drums in terms of its radius $r$, in inches." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220976 1072 #uuid "c5cf0109-e59a-58aa-8ff5-5f4a8d047ab5" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220976 1074 "We are given that the drum is a right circular cylinder. The formula for the volume of a cylinder is\n\n$$\nV = \\pi r^2 h\n$$\n\nWe are told that the height $h$ is $1$ inch less than five times the radius. In other words,\n$h = 5r - 1$.\n\nNow, we substitute this expression into the volume formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= πr^{2}h \\\\\n&= πr^{2}(5r - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the final answer is\n\n$$\nV = \\pi r^2 (5r - 1)\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220977 1069 "e-19398" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220977 1071 "A cylindrical food container holds soup and is topped off with broth. The container has a base area of $80\\,\\mathrm{cm}^2$ and a height of $9\\,\\mathrm{cm}$. If $240\\,\\mathrm{cm}^3$ of broth is added to fill the container to the top, what is the volume of soup in the container?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220977 1072 #uuid "f7059d37-acdb-5205-969b-60f641789347" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220977 1074 "We are given the base area and height of a cylindrical container, and the volume of broth used to top it off. We are asked to find the volume of soup.\n\nFirst, let's calculate the total volume of the container using the formula:\n$V = B h$,\nwhere $B$ is the base area and $h$ is the height.\n\nSubstituting $B = 80$ and $h = 9{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= 80 \\cdot 9 \\\\\n&= 720\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the total volume of the container is $720\\,\\mathrm{cm}^3$. Since $240\\,\\mathrm{cm}^3$ of broth was added, the remaining space must be soup.\n\nLet $V_s$ be this volume. Then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV_{s} &= 720 - 240 \\\\\n&= 480\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of soup in the container is approximately $480\\,\\mathrm{cm}^3$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220978 1069 "e-19417" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220978 1071 "In the $xy$-plane, the graph of the equation\n$(x + 3)^2 + (y - 1)^2 = 36$\nis a circle. Point $M$ lies on the circle and has coordinates $(-3, 7)$. If $\\overline{MN}$ is a diameter of the circle, what are the coordinates of point $N$?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220978 1072 #uuid "3db798b5-5ea3-5ef0-8c05-486d39e8679f" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220978 1074 "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^2 + (y-b)^2 = r^2\n$$\n\nWe are given the equation\n\n$$\n(x + 3)^2 + (y - 1)^2 = 36\n$$\n\nwhich can be rewritten as\n\n$$\n\\big(x - (-3)\\big)^2 + (y - 1)^2 = 6^2\n$$\n\nComparing the last equation with the general equation of a circle, we obtain that the coordinates of the center of our circle are $(-3,1)$.\n\nWe are told that $\\overline{MN}$ is a diameter of the circle. Therefore, the midpoint of $\\overline{MN}$ coincides with the center of the circle; that is, the midpoint of $\\overline{MN}$ has coordinates $(-3,1)$.\n\nNow, let $(x_1,y_1)$ be the coordinates of point $N$. Since $(-3,1)$ is the midpoint of the segment with endpoints $M(-3,7)$ and $N(x_1,y_1)$, we have\n\n$$\n(-3,1) = \\left(\\dfrac{-3 + x_1}{2}, \\dfrac{7 + y_1}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nSolving for $x_1$ in the equation\n\n$$\n-3 = \\dfrac{-3 + x_1}{2}\n$$\n\nand solving for $y_1$ in the equation\n\n$$\n1 = \\dfrac{7 + y_1}{2}\n$$\n\nwe get $x_1 = -3$ and $y_1 = -5$.\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of point $N$ are $(-3,-5)$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220979 1069 "e-19418" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220979 1071 "Which of the following equations represents a circle in the $xy$-plane that intersects the $x$-axis at exactly one point?\n\n1. $(x - 6)^2 + (y + 4)^2 = 16$\n2. $(x - 6)^2 + (y + 5)^2 = 16$\n3. $(x - 6)^2 + (y + 4)^2 = 25$\n4. $(x - 6)^2 + (y - 4)^2 = 9$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220979 1072 #uuid "e4a175d8-f683-587f-a621-f1bce46c23c4" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220979 1074 "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^2 + (y-b)^2 = r^2\n$$\n\nThe radius of a circle is the distance from the center of the circle to any point on the circle. If a circle in the $xy$-plane intersects the $x$-axis at exactly one point, then the distance from the center of the circle to this point on the $x$-axis must be equal to the radius of the circle.\n\nIn our case, if\n\n$$\n(x-a)^2 + (y-b)^2 = r^2\n$$\n\nrepresents a circle that intersects the $x$-axis at exactly one point, then\n\n$$\n\\mid b \\mid = r\n$$\n\nWith this in mind, we proceed to analyze each of the equations given.\n\n- For the equation $(x - 6)^2 + (y + 4)^2 = 16$, we have $b = -4$ and $r = 4$. Then $\\mid b \\mid = r$.\n- For the equation $(x - 6)^2 + (y + 5)^2 = 16$, we have $b = -5$ and $r = 4$. Then $\\mid b \\mid \\neq r$.\n- For the equation $(x - 6)^2 + (y + 4)^2 = 25$, we have $b = -4$ and $r = 5$. Then $\\mid b \\mid \\neq r$.\n- For the equation $(x - 6)^2 + (y - 4)^2 = 9$, we have $b = 4$ and $r = 3$. Then $\\mid b \\mid \\neq r$.\n\nTherefore, the circle that touches the $x$-axis at exactly one point is\n\n$$\n(x - 6)^2 + (y + 4)^2 = 16\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"IV\" only." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220980 1069 "e-19419" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220980 1071 "The graph of the equation $(x + 4)^2 + (y - 3)^2 = 100$ is a circle in the $xy$-plane. The point $(h, k)$ lies on the circle. Which of the following is a possible value for $k$?\n\n1. $10$\n2. $13$\n3. $6$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220980 1072 #uuid "91f8da9f-c298-55f8-823b-50ff5b06cae5" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220980 1074 "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^2 + (y-b)^2 = r^2\n$$\n\nWe are given the equation\n\n$$\n(x + 4)^2 + (y - 3)^2 = 100\n$$\n\nwhich can be rewritten as\n\n$$\n(x - (-4))^2 + (y - 3)^2 = 10^2\n$$\n\nSo, the center of the circle is $(-4, 3)$ and its radius is $10$.\n\nNext, we draw our circle in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/7d/7d42807d0e85aa3b95a95d51b1b946099f36f98d54b8ebea4e67c60fd0c331df.png)\n\nSince the $y$-coordinate of the center is $3$ and the radius is $10$, the least possible $y$-coordinate for any point on the circle is\n\n$$\n3 - 10 = -7\n$$\n\nSimilarly, the greatest possible $y$-coordinate for any point on the circle is\n\n$$\n3 + 10 = 13\n$$\n\nSince $(h, k)$ lies on the circle, we must have\n\n$$\n-7 \\leq k \\leq 13\n$$\n\nTherefore, among the given options, the only value that satisfies the above inequality is $13$. So, the correct answer is \"II\" only." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220981 1069 "e-19420" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220981 1071 "A circle in the $xy$-plane has equation $(x - 7)^2 + (y + 4)^2 = 16$. Which of the following points does **NOT** lie in the interior of the circle?\n\n1. $(7,-4)$\n2. $(12,-4)$\n3. $(9,-4)$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220981 1072 #uuid "f0db98a1-1e86-50b7-b420-5de9a6d8e192" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220981 1074 "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^2 + (y-b)^2 = r^2\n$$\n\nWe are given the equation\n\n$$\n(x - 7)^2 + (y + 4)^2 = 16\n$$\n\nwhich can be rewritten as\n\n$$\n(x - 7)^2 + (y - (-4))^2 = 4^2\n$$\n\nComparing the last equation with the general equation of a circle, we obtain that the center of our circle has coordinates $(7,-4)$, and its radius is $4$.\n\nNow, for a point to be inside a circle, the distance from that point to the center of the circle must be less than the radius.\n\nWith this in mind, we proceed to find which of the given points is such that its distance to $(7,-4)$ is less than $4$ (the radius of the circle).\n\n- For $(7,-4)$, this is the center of the circle, and therefore must lie in its interior.\n- For $(12,-4)$, we get\n$\\sqrt{(12 - 7)^{2} + (-4 - (-4))^{2}}|= \\sqrt{5^{2} + 0}; = \\sqrt{25}; = 5; ≮4$.\n- For $(9,-4)$, we obtain\n$\\sqrt{(9 - 7)^{2} + (-4 - (-4))^{2}}|= \\sqrt{2^{2} + 0}; = \\sqrt{4}; = 2; < 4$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220982 1069 "e-19421" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220982 1071 "![](images/15/15eac62c7d7a5ea61495f9307d22594ccf8a6c979b8797abc7c7bea0e0906527.png)\n\nCircle $M$ (shown) is defined by the equation $(x + 1)^2 + (y + 1)^2 = 4$. Circle $N$ (not shown) is the result of shifting circle $M$ right $8$ units and up $3$ units and increasing the radius so that the radius of circle $N$ is $5$ times the radius of circle $M$. Find the equation defining circle $N$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220982 1072 #uuid "6e67ae40-19d7-5a95-b587-f0b3be4b1170" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220982 1074 "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^2 + (y-b)^2 = r^2\n$$\n\nThe equation representing circle $M$ can be rewritten as\n\n$$\n(x - (-1))^2 + (y - (-1))^2 = 2^2\n$$\n\nComparing the last equation with the general equation of a circle, we obtain that the coordinates of the center of the circle $M$ are $(-1,-1)$ and its radius is $2$.\n\nSince the circle $N$ is the result of shifting circle $M$ right $8$ units and up $3$ units and increasing the radius so that its radius is $5$ times the radius of circle $M$, we get\n\n- the center of $N$ has coordinates $(-1+8,\\,-1+3) = (7,2)$, and\n- the radius of $N$ is $r = 5(2) = 10$.\n\nTherefore, the equation of circle $N$ is\n\n$$\n(x - 7)^2 + (y - 2)^2 = 10^2\n$$\n\nor equivalently,\n\n$$\n(x - 7)^2 + (y - 2)^2 = 100\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220983 1069 "e-19423" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220983 1071 "The equation $(x - 3)^2 + (y + 7)^2 = 100$ represents circle $M$. Circle $N$ is obtained by shifting circle $M$ left $4$ units in the $xy$-plane. Find the equation that represents circle $N$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220983 1072 #uuid "662f643e-3fbb-52b5-a9dd-f5517c49ccde" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220983 1074 "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^2 + (y-b)^2 = r^2\n$$\n\nThe equation representing circle $M$ can be rewritten as\n\n$$\n(x - 3)^2 + (y - (-7))^2 = 10^2\n$$\n\nComparing the last equation with the general equation of a circle, we obtain that the coordinates of the center of the circle $M$ are $(3,-7)$ and its radius is $10$.\n\nSince circle $N$ is obtained by shifting circle $M$ left $4$ units, we obtain\n\n- $N$ has the same radius as circle $M$, and\n- the center of $N$ has coordinates $(3 - 4, -7) = (-1,-7)$.\n\nTherefore, the equation of circle $N$ is\n\n$$\n(x - (-1))^2 + (y - (-7))^2 = 10^2\n$$\n\nor equivalently,\n\n$$\n(x + 1)^2 + (y + 7)^2 = 100\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220984 1069 "e-19424" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220984 1071 "The circle $R$ in the $xy$-plane has the equation\n$(x - 2)^2 + (y + 3)^2 = 25$.\nThe circle $S$ has the same center as circle $R$. The radius of circle $S$ is three times the radius of circle $R$. The equation defining circle $S$ in the $xy$-plane is\n$(x - 2)^2 + (y + 3)^2 = k$,\nwhere $k$ is a constant. What is the value of $k$?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220984 1072 #uuid "3b40435c-337b-53e7-9085-b32b4dd8a4d4" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220984 1074 "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^2 + (y-b)^2 = r^2\n$$\n\nThe equation of the circle $R$ can be rewritten as\n\n$$\n(x - 2)^2 + (y - (-3))^2 = 5^2\n$$\n\nTherefore, its center has coordinates $(2,-3)$ and its radius is $r_{R} = 5$.\n\nWe are given that the radius of circle $S$ is three times the radius of circle $R$. So, the radius of circle $S$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr_{S} &= 3 \\cdot r_{R} \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot 5 \\\\\n&= 15\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $S$ and $R$ have the same center, the general equation of the circle $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x - 2)^2 + (y + 3)^2 = 15^2\n$$\n\nor equivalently,\n\n$$\n(x - 2)^2 + (y + 3)^2 = 225\n$$\n\nFinally, comparing the last equation with\n\n$$\n(x - 2)^2 + (y + 3)^2 = k\n$$\n\nwe can conclude that $k = 225$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220985 1069 "e-19426" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220985 1071 "In the $xy$-plane, the graph of $7x^{2} + 14x + 7y^{2} - 28y = 0$ is a circle. What is the radius of the circle?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220985 1072 #uuid "fc11fbec-0ef1-5625-bc54-90666cef5aa9" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220985 1074 "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^2 + (y-b)^2 = r^2\n$$\n\nTherefore, to find the radius of our circle, we must first find its general equation.\n\nWe begin by simplifying the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n7x^{2} + 14x + 7y^{2} - 28y &= 0 \\\\\n7(x^{2} + 2x + y^{2} - 4y) &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} + 2x + y^{2} - 4y &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, grouping the $x$ and $y$ terms and completing the squares, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + 2x + y^{2} - 4y &= 0 \\\\\n(x^{2} + 2x) + (y^{2} - 4y) &= 0 \\\\\n(x^{2} + 2 \\cdot 1x + 1^{2}) + (y^{2} - 2 \\cdot 2y + 2^{2}) &= 0 + 1^{2} + 2^{2} \\\\\n(x^{2} + 2 \\cdot 1x + 1^{2}) + (y^{2} - 2 \\cdot 2y + 2^{2}) &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can factor the perfect squares and get\n\n$$\n(x + 1)^2 + (y - 2)^2 = 5\n$$\n\nor equivalently,\n\n$$\n(x - (-1))^2 + (y - 2)^2 = \\big(\\sqrt{5}\\big)^2\n$$\n\nComparing the last equation with the general equation of a circle, we obtain that the radius of our circle is\n\n$$\nr = \\sqrt{5}\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220986 1069 "e-19428" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220986 1071 "The graph of $x^2 - 14x + y^2 + 2y = 20$ in the $xy$-plane is a circle. What is the radius of the circle?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220986 1072 #uuid "cb67cb05-1109-508d-ac7d-fe7f1fa2590e" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220986 1074 "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^2 + (y-b)^2 = r^2\n$$\n\nTherefore, to find the radius of our circle, we must first find its general equation.\n\nGrouping the $x$ and $y$ terms and completing the squares, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 14x + y^{2} + 2y &= 20 \\\\\n(x^{2} - 14x) + (y^{2} + 2y) &= 20 \\\\\n(x^{2} - 2 \\cdot 7x + 7^{2}) + (y^{2} + 2 \\cdot 1y + 1^{2}) &= 20 + 7^{2} + 1^{2} \\\\\n&= 20 + 49 + 1 \\\\\n&= 70\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can factor the perfect squares and get\n\n$$\n(x - 7)^2 + (y + 1)^2 = 70\n$$\n\nor equivalently,\n\n$$\n(x - 7)^2 + (y + 1)^2 = (\\sqrt{70})^2\n$$\n\nComparing the last equation with the general equation of a circle, we obtain that the radius of our circle is\n\n$$\nr = \\sqrt{70}\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220987 1069 "e-19429" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220987 1071 "A circle in the $xy$-plane has its center at $(-8,5)$ and has a radius of $6$. An equation of this circle is $x^2 + y^2 + a x + b y + c = 0$, where $a$, $b$, and $c$ are constants. What is the value of $c$?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220987 1072 #uuid "2324dec1-d85d-5f67-8345-73521c4402bb" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220987 1074 "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^2 + (y-b)^2 = r^2\n$$\n\nWe are given that the radius of the circle is $6$ and it's centered at the point $(-8,5)$. So, $r = 6$, $a = -8$, and $b = 5$. Substituting these values into the equation above, we obtain\n\n$$\n(x - (-8))^2 + (y - 5)^2 = 6^2\n$$\n\nor equivalently,\n\n$$\n(x + 8)^2 + (y - 5)^2 = 36\n$$\n\nExpanding and grouping like terms, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x + 8)^{2} + (y - 5)^{2} &= 36 \\\\\nx^{2} + 16x + 64 + y^{2} - 10y + 25 &= 36 \\\\\nx^{2} + y^{2} + 16x - 10y + 53 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the last equation with\n\n$$\nx^2 + y^2 + ax + by + c = 0\n$$\n\nwe get $c =53$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220988 1069 "e-20288" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220988 1071 "In a triangle $\\triangle{AED}$, the angle $\\angle{D}$ is a right angle. The points $B$ and $C$ lie on $\\overline{AE}$ and $\\overline{ED}$, respectively, such that $\\overline{BC}$ is parallel to $\\overline{AD}$. If the length of $\\overline{AD}$ is $49$ units, the length of $\\overline{BC}$ is $14$ units, and the area of $\\triangle{AED}$ is $735$ square units, what is the length of $\\overline{CD}$, in units?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220988 1072 #uuid "41164f22-c855-5607-9334-45ac1dad526a" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220988 1074 "We proceed to represent the given information in a diagram.\n\n![](images/2a/2a3d476da03e790f01dafb1a7a737af7ab5792b00b5bdbe9067d0548bcc25e16.png)\n\nThe formula for the area of a triangle is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = \\dfrac{b \\cdot h}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere $b$ is a base and $h$ is the corresponding height.\n\nIn the case of $\\triangle{AED}$, from the diagram we can see that its base is\n$b = 49$,\nand its corresponding height is\n$h = ED$.\n\nTherefore, the area of $\\triangle{AED}$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = \\dfrac{49 \\cdot ED}{2} = 24.5\\,ED\n$$\n\nWe are given that this area is equal to $735$ square units. Therefore, we can establish the equality\n\n$$\n24.5\\,ED = 735\n$$\n\nSolving for $ED$, we obtain\n\n$$\nED = \\dfrac{735}{24.5} = 30\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, since $\\overline{BC}$ is parallel to $\\overline{AD}$ and $\\angle{D}$ is a right angle, $\\angle{BCE}$ is also a right angle.\n\nSince $\\triangle{BCE}$ and $\\triangle{ADE}$ each have a right angle and share angle $\\angle{E}$, we have $\\triangle{BCE} \\sim \\triangle{ADE}$ by the AA similarity criterion. As a consequence of the similarity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{BC}{AD}=\\dfrac{EC}{ED}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $BC = 14$, $AD = 49$, and $ED=30$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{14}{49} &= \\frac{EC}{30} \\\\\n\\frac{2}{7} &= \\frac{EC}{30} \\\\\nEC &= \\frac{60}{7}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\nCD = ED - EC\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nCD &= 30 - \\frac{60}{7} \\\\\n&= \\frac{210}{7} - \\frac{60}{7} \\\\\n&= \\frac{150}{7}units\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220989 1069 "e-20290" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220989 1071 "![](images/b1/b1796d046b5d71b7a57b576c221da6a86a2d2b711bb947925a76a57a33a8b7c9.png)\n\nIn the figure above, $TX=8$ and $RX=15$. What is the length of $\\overline{XY}$?\n\n*Note: Figure not drawn to scale.*" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220989 1072 #uuid "48b2ffd3-ff3f-59c1-9dfc-dec680b0fdf5" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220989 1074 "Applying the Pythagorean Theorem to $\\triangle{RXT}$, we can obtain $RT$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nRT^{2} &= TX^{2} + RX^{2} \\\\\nRT^{2} &= 8^{2} + 15^{2} \\\\\nRT^{2} &= 64 + 225 \\\\\nRT^{2} &= 289 \\\\\nRT &= 17\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we can see that $\\triangle{RYT}$ and $\\triangle{RXT}$ are right triangles that have the non-right angle $\\angle{R}$ as a common angle. Therefore, $\\triangle{RYT} \\sim \\triangle{RXT}$ by the AA Similarity Criterion. As a consequence of the similarity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{RY}{RT} = \\dfrac{RT}{RX}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the values for $RT$ and $RX$ into the equality above, and solving for $RY$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{RY}{17} &= \\frac{17}{15} \\\\\n\\frac{RY}{17} &= \\frac{17}{15} \\\\\nRY &= 17 \\cdot \\frac{17}{15} \\\\\nRY &= \\frac{289}{15}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, note that\n\n$$\nXY = RY - RX\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nXY &= \\frac{289}{15} - 15 \\\\\n&= \\frac{289}{15} - \\frac{225}{15} \\\\\n&= \\frac{289 - 225}{15} \\\\\n&= \\frac{64}{15}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the length of $\\overline{XY}$ is\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\dfrac{64}{15}}\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220990 1069 "e-20295" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220990 1071 "In a set of three consecutive even integers, where the integers are ordered from least to greatest, the first integer is represented by $s$. The product of $11$ and the second even integer is no more than $22$ less than the sum of the first and third even integers. Find the inequality that represents this situation." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220990 1072 #uuid "afd02330-5569-59f6-b155-9ac9d86afbc3" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220990 1074 "Let the three consecutive even integers be\n\n$$\ns,\\quad s + 2,\\quad s + 4\n$$\n\nThe second even integer is $s + 2$, so $11$ times that is\n\n$$\n11(s + 2)\n$$\n\nThe sum of the first and third even integers is\n\n$$\ns + (s + 4)=2s + 4\n$$\n\nWe are told that the product is *no more than* $22$ less than this sum, so we subtract $22$ from the sum and write the inequality:\n\n$$\n11(s + 2) \\leq 2s + 4 - 22\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220991 1069 "e-20296" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220991 1071 "An aquarium builder restricts tank dimensions for a standard shelf. For tanks shaped like rectangular prisms, the sum of the perimeter of the base and the height must not exceed $160$ inches. The perimeter of the base is calculated using the width and length. If a tank has a height of $40$ inches and its length is $1.5$ times its width, find the inequality that represents the possible values of the width $w$, in inches." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220991 1072 #uuid "547237aa-614f-5903-a8a8-b9138d6f2bf9" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220991 1074 "Let $w$ be the width of the tank. Since the length is $1.5w$, the perimeter of the base is\n\n$$\n2(w + 1.5w) = 2(2.5w) = 5w\n$$\n\nThe sum of the perimeter and the height must be no more than $160$ inches. Therefore,\n\n$$\n5w + 40 \\leq 160\n$$\n\nNext, we subtract $40$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n5w \\leq 120\n$$\n\nNow, we divide both sides by $5$:\n\n$$\nw \\leq 24\n$$\n\nSince width must be positive, the inequality is\n\n$$\n0 < w \\leq 24\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220992 1069 "e-20297" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220992 1071 "In an electric circuit, Ohm's law is given by the formula\n\n$I = \\dfrac{V}{R}$,\n\nwhere $I$ is the current in amperes, $V$ is the potential difference in volts, and $R$ is the resistance in ohms.\n\nSuppose a circuit has a resistance of $720$ ohms, and its potential difference comes from $n$ ten-volt batteries, producing a total of $10n$ volts. If the current in the circuit must not exceed $0.9$ amperes, what is the greatest number $n$ of batteries that can be used?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220992 1072 #uuid "7a7325d1-bfb9-5097-8045-bc74327d27e5" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220992 1074 "We are given the formula:\n\n$$\nI = \\dfrac{V}{R}\n$$\n\nand told that $V = 10n$ and $R = 720$. The current must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{10n}{720} \\leq 0.9\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply both sides by $720{:}$\n\n$$\n10n \\leq 648\n$$\n\nNow, we divide both sides by $10{:}$\n\n$$\nn \\leq \\dfrac{648}{10} = 64.8\n$$\n\nSince $n$ must be an integer, the greatest possible value is $64$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220993 1069 "e-20298" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220993 1071 "Jamal works part-time building websites. He earns $\\[math]20$ per hour for the first $4$ hours each week and $\\[/math]26$ per hour for any additional hours. He saves $65\\%$ of his weekly earnings. What is the minimum number of additional hours he must work to save at least $\\[math]325$?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220993 1072 #uuid "5d70d383-bb95-54bf-828b-b8a12deb37e8" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220993 1074 "Let $x$ be the number of additional hours Jamal works beyond the first $4$ hours.\n\nHe earns $\\[math]20$ per hour for the first $4$ hours:\n\n$$\n20 \\cdot 4 = 80\n$$\n\nHe earns $\\[/math]26$ for each additional hour:\n$26x$\n\nHis total earnings are\n$80 + 26x$.\n\nHe saves $65\\%$ of his earnings:\n$0.65(80 + 26x)$.\n\nHe wants to save *at least* $\\[math]325$. We write this as the following inequality:\n\n$$\n0.65(80 + 26x) \\geq 325\n$$\n\nThen, we divide both sides by $0.65$:\n\n$$\n80 + 26x \\geq \\dfrac{325}{0.65} = 500\n$$\n\nNow, we subtract $80$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n26x \\geq 420\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides by $26$:\n\n$$\nx \\geq \\dfrac{420}{26} \\approx 16.15\n$$\n\nSince he cannot work a fraction of an hour, he must work *at least $17$ additional hours*." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220994 1069 "e-20299" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220994 1071 "A robotics club is buying identical toolkits for each of the $50$ members. The total budget for the purchase is $\\[math]7,000$, which includes a $6\\%$ sales tax. What is the maximum possible price per toolkit, before sales tax, that the club can afford within the budget, rounded to two decimal places?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220994 1072 #uuid "31e8f2e6-8e33-5bea-9362-e737097f48f1" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220994 1074 "Let $x$ represent the pre-tax price of one toolkit. The cost for $50$ toolkits before tax is $50x$. With $6\\%$ sales tax, the total cost becomes\n\n$$\n50x \\cdot 1.06\n$$\n\nThis amount must be less than or equal to the budget:\n\n$$\n50x \\cdot 1.06 \\leq 7,000\n$$\n\nNow, we divide both sides by $1.06{:}$\n\n$$\n50x \\leq \\dfrac{7,000}{1.06} \\approx 6,603.77\n$$\n\nFinally, dividing both sides by $50$ yields:\n\n$$\nx \\leq \\dfrac{6,603.77}{50} \\approx 132.08\n$$\n\nSo, the maximum possible price per toolkit, before sales tax, is approximately $\\[math]132.08$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220995 1069 "e-20300" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220995 1071 "To determine the popularity of a video game, a developer surveyed $350$ players. Among the first $210$ players, $75$ rated the game as excellent. Among the remaining $140$ players, $g$ rated it as excellent. If more than $40\\%$ of all players rated the game as excellent, find the inequality that describes the possible values of $g$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220995 1072 #uuid "87a657b5-afdc-52ba-bb99-032808c0e6b3" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220995 1074 "The total number of players surveyed is $350$.\n\nThe number of players who rated the game as excellent is $g + 75$, where $g$ is the number from the second group.\n\nWe are told that *more than* $40\\%$ of players rated it as excellent. That means\n\n$$\ng + 75 > 0.40(350)\n$$\n\nTherefore, this inequality must be satisfied, and since $g$ represents a count of people, it must also satisfy\n\n$$\ng \\leq 140\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220996 1069 "e-20301" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220996 1071 "A dance studio offers a monthly unlimited class pass for $\\[math]135$. Individual classes cost $\\[/math]7.00$, $\\[math]8.75$, or $\\[/math]9.50$, depending on the class type. What is the minimum number of classes someone must attend for the unlimited pass to be more cost-effective than paying per class?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220996 1072 #uuid "24e60bc0-622d-57cb-ba37-4c046bf2121f" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220996 1074 "To find the fewest number of classes that makes the unlimited pass worthwhile, we assume each class costs the highest price of $\\[math]9.50$.\n\nLet $x$ represent the number of classes attended.\n\nWithout the pass, the total cost is $\\[/math]9.50$ per class:\n$9.50x$\n\nWith the pass, the cost is a flat $\\[math]135$.\n\nWe want the pass to cost less than paying individually. This gives the inequality:\n\n$$\n135 < 9.50x\n$$\n\nNow, solve the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}135 & < 9.50x \\\\ \\frac{135}{9.50} & < x \\\\ 14.210… & < x\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSince $x$ must be a whole number, the smallest value that satisfies the inequality is $15$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220997 1069 "e-20311" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220997 1071 "A tutor is planning a weekend workshop. The venue rental is a one-time cost of $\\[math]35$, and each participant receives materials costing $\\[/math]18.25$. If the tutor’s total budget is $\\[math]200$, what is the greatest number of participants that can attend without exceeding the budget?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220997 1072 #uuid "88d51355-bee2-522e-8883-edb30d0341d1" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220997 1074 "We are given a scenario with a fixed cost and a per-participant cost. We are told the total cost must not exceed $\\[math]200$, and we are asked to find the maximum number of participants the tutor can afford.\n\nLet $x$ be the number of participants.\n\nThe total cost is the fixed fee plus the per-participant fee:\n\n$$\n\\text{Total cost} = 35 + 18.25x\n$$\n\nWe are told this total must not exceed $\\[/math]200$, so we write the inequality:\n\n$$\n35 + 18.25x \\leq 200\n$$\n\nWe now solve the inequality for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}35 + 18.25x & \\le 200 \\\\ 18.25x & \\le 200 - 35 \\\\ 18.25x & \\le 165 \\\\ x & \\le \\frac{165}{18.25} \\\\ x & \\le 9.0410…\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSince $x$ must be a whole number, the greatest possible number of participants is $9$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220998 1069 "e-20313" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220998 1071 "Noah is buying supply kits for a robotics challenge. There is a one-time handling fee of $\\[math]17$, and each kit costs $\\[/math]16$. Noah must spend *at least* $\\[math]113$ in total. If he can only purchase a whole number of kits, what is the minimum number of kits he must order?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220998 1072 #uuid "30fed334-e52d-5f62-91dc-b9dade98936b" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220998 1074 "Let $k$ represent the number of kits Noah orders.\n\nThe total cost is given by the cost per kit times the number of kits plus the handling fee:\n\n$$\n16k + 17 \\geq 113\n$$\n\nWe solve the inequality step by step:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}16k + 17 & \\ge 113 \\\\ 16k & \\ge 113 - 17 \\\\ 16k & \\ge 96 \\\\ k & \\ge 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSince the number of kits must be a whole number, the minimum number of kits Noah must order is $6$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220999 1069 "e-20314" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220999 1071 "Chloe is reading a novel and wants to finish at least $18\\,000$ words this evening. She reads at a pace of $300$ words per minute. What is the fewest number of minutes she must read to meet her goal?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220999 1072 #uuid "ad3ddb47-4d92-5239-9169-33ed1ea8fc0f" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186220999 1074 "This is a rate problem involving reading speed and time. We use the relationship\n\n$$\n\\text{words} = \\text{rate} \\times \\text{time}\n$$\n\nLet $t$ be the number of minutes Chloe reads. Since she reads at a pace of $300$ words per minute, the total number of words read is\n\n$$\n300t\n$$\n\nShe wants to read *at least* $18\\,000$ words, so we write this as the following inequality:\n\n$$\n300t \\geq 18\\,000\n$$\n\nNow, we divide both sides by $3\\,000$ to obtain\n\n$$\nt \\geq 60\n$$\n\nSo, Chloe must read for *at least* $60$ minutes. Since the question asks for the fewest number of minutes, the answer is $60$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221000 1069 "e-20316" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221000 1071 "A camera's memory card can store at most $2\\,000$ photos. It currently has $1\\,345$ photos stored. What is the maximum **additional number of photos** that can be taken without exceeding the memory limit?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221000 1072 #uuid "88c0c429-925d-53ae-ad64-3a269597096b" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221000 1074 "Let $p$ represent the additional number of photos that can be taken. Since the memory card currently has $1\\,345$ photos stored, the total becomes\n\n$$\n1345 + p\n$$\n\nBecause the memory card can hold *no more than* $2\\,000$ photos, we write the inequality\n\n$$\n1345 + p \\leq 2000\n$$\n\nSolving this, we get\n\n$$\np \\leq 655\n$$\n\nSo, the memory card can store *at most* $655$ additional photos." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221001 1069 "e-20325" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221001 1071 "A value $m$ is not greater than $9$ less than $5$ times the value of $n$. If the value of $n$ is $4$, what is the maximum possible value of $m$?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221001 1072 #uuid "81a94511-8e43-5226-894a-0f3d990f7c6f" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221001 1074 "The phrase “$m$ is not greater than $9$ less than $5$ times the value of $n$” can be unpacked in parts to understand how it translates to an inequality.\n\n- First, the expression “$5$ times the value of $n$” means we compute $5n$.\n- Next, the phrase “$9$ less than $5n$” tells us to **subtract $9$** from that result, giving $5n - 9$.\n- Then, the phrase *“not greater than”* means that $m$ must be *less than or equal to* this amount.\n\nPutting these pieces together, we get the inequality:\n\n$$\nm \\leq 5n - 9\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n = 4$ into the inequality yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}m & \\le 5(4) - 9 \\\\ m & \\le 20 - 9 \\\\ m & \\le 11\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the maximum possible value of $m$ is $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{11}$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221002 1069 "e-20326" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221002 1071 "The greatest possible value of $u$ is $12$ less than $5$ times another number $v$. Which inequality represents the possible values of $u$?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221002 1072 #uuid "4071549b-133c-5649-90bc-435584293a42" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221002 1074 "The phrase “the greatest possible value of $u$ is $12$ less than $5$ times the value of $v$” can be unpacked in parts to understand how it translates to an inequality.\n\n- First, the expression “$5$ times the value of $v$” means we compute $5v$.\n- Next, the phrase “$12$ less than $5v$” tells us to *subtract $12$* from that result, giving $5v - 12$.\n- Then, the phrase *“greatest possible value”* means that $u$ must be *less than or equal to* this amount.\n\nPutting these pieces together, we get the inequality:\n\n$$\nu \\leq 5v - 12\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221003 1069 "e-20327" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221003 1071 "An email system allows attachments totaling at most $25$ MB. A required system log of $3$ MB is automatically attached. Images are $2$ MB each and video clips are $7$ MB each. Find the inequality that represents the possible combinations of images, $i$, and clips, $c$, that can be sent." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221003 1072 #uuid "b21e6567-f853-58a1-adf1-67626940d1ee" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221003 1074 "We are told that\n\n- The total attachment size must not exceed $25$ MB.\n- The system log already uses $3$ MB.\n\nThis means the remaining size available is:\n$25 - 3 = 22$\n\nEach image uses $2$ MB, and each clip uses $7$ MB. If $i$ is the number of images and $c$ is the number of clips, the variable attachment size is:\n$2i + 7c$\n\nSince the variable attachments must not exceed $22$ MB, we write the inequality:\n\n$$\n2i + 7c \\leq 22\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221004 1069 "e-20337" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221004 1071 "A factory worker is assembling products. Each widget of type $\\textrm{X}$ requires $\\[math]15$ in parts, and each widget of type $\\textrm{Y}$ requires $\\[/math]12$. The total value of parts used must be no less than $\\[math]360$. If $x$ represents the number of type $\\textrm{X}$ widgets and $y$ represents the number of type $\\textrm{Y}$ widgets, find the inequality that represents this situation." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221004 1072 #uuid "4fd82b8b-a4b2-5db2-a3cc-dcd724aa19dc" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221004 1074 "The worker is building $x$ type $\\textrm{X}$ widgets at $\\[math]15$ each and $y$ type $\\textrm{Y}$ widgets at $\\[/math]12$ each.\n\nSo, the total cost is\n$15x + 12y$.\n\nWe are told the value must be *no less than* $\\[math]360$, and so we have\n\n$$\n15x + 12y \\geq 360\n$$\n\nThus, the correct inequality is\n\n$$\n15x + 12y \\geq 360\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221005 1069 "e-20338" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221005 1071 "At an airport, two luggage scanners are used. Scanner $\\textrm{A}$ processes $90$ bags per hour, while Scanner $\\textrm{B}$ processes $120$ bags per hour. If Scanner $\\textrm{A}$ is used for $a$ hours and Scanner $\\textrm{B}$ is used for $b$ hours, and more than $1,\\!800$ bags must be scanned, find the inequality that represents this situation." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221005 1072 #uuid "72a9c426-786f-55e4-b16c-21545cdac914" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221005 1074 "The total number of bags scanned is the sum from both scanners:\n\n- Scanner $\\textrm{A}$ processes $90$ bags per hour for $a$ hours, so the number of bags scanned is $90a$.\n- Scanner $\\textrm{B}$ processes $120$ bags per hour for $b$ hours, so the number of bags scanned is $120b$.\n\nThus, the total number of bags scanned is\n$90a + 120b$.\n\nSince the total must be *more than* $1,\\!800$ bags, we have the inequality\n$90a + 120b > 1{,}800$.\n\nHence, the inequality is\n$90a + 120b > 1{,}800$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221006 1069 "e-20339" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221006 1071 "Two fitness trainers are running workout stations. Each trainer completes a number of rounds with clients. They must complete fewer than $40$ rounds in total. Find the inequality that represents this situation, where $r_1$ is the number of rounds completed by Trainer $1$ and $r_2$ is the number completed by Trainer $2$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221006 1072 #uuid "b6539581-a879-5daa-8758-628e4a781933" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221006 1074 "Let $r_1$ represent the number of rounds completed by Trainer $1$ and $r_2$ the number completed by Trainer $2$. The total number of rounds is:\n$r_1 + r_2$\n\nWe are told the trainers must complete *fewer than* $40$ rounds.\n\nSo, the inequality representing this scenario is\n$r_1 + r_2 < 40$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221007 1069 "e-20341" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221007 1071 "A company is packing laptops and monitors into shipping crates. Each crate can carry no more than $180$ pounds. Each laptop weighs $5.2$ pounds, and each monitor weighs $12.3$ pounds. The number of monitors must be no more than twice the number of laptops. Let $l$ represent the number of laptops and $m$ represent the number of monitors, where $l$ and $m$ are nonnegative integers. Which of the following systems of inequalities best represents this situation?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221007 1072 #uuid "5fd16140-b587-5bb5-9269-5cf43b98fab6" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221007 1074 "We translate each condition into an inequality:\n\n- The total weight cannot exceed $180$ pounds. Since each laptop weighs $5.2$ pounds and each monitor weighs $12.3$ pounds, we have\n$5.2l + 12.3m \\leq 180$.\n- The number of monitors must be no more than twice the number of laptops, so we write:\n$m \\leq 2l$.\n\nPutting both constraints together, the system of inequalities is\n\n$$\n{5.2l + 12.3m \\le 180; m \\le 2l\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221008 1069 "e-20345" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221008 1071 "A farmer is purchasing $x$ bags of grain and $y$ water tanks for livestock. The farmer must purchase at least $25$ items in total, but wants to spend no more than $\\[math]7,000$. Each bag of grain costs $\\[/math]120$ and each water tank costs $\\[math]350$, and the farmer must buy at least $10$ bags of grain and at least $5$ water tanks. What is the system of inequalities representing this situation?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221008 1072 #uuid "e2f55794-bcb3-5846-9c79-6494d924de45" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221008 1074 "First, we translate each condition into an inequality:\n\n- We're told that the total cost cannot exceed $\\[math]7,000$. Since each bag of grain costs $\\[/math]120$ and each water tank costs $\\[math]350$, we have\n$120x + 350y \\leq 7,000$.\n- The total number of items must be at least $25$, so we have\n$x + y \\geq 25$.\n- The farmer must buy at least $10$ bags of grain. Then, we can write\n$x \\geq 10$.\n- The farmer must also buy at least $5$ water tanks, so we have\n$y \\geq 5$.\n\nThus, putting it all together, the system of inequalities that represents this situation is\n\n$$\n{120x + 350y \\le 7, 000; x + y \\ge 25; x \\ge 10; y \\ge 5\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221009 1069 "e-20346" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221009 1071 "A science teacher is buying $x$ microscopes and $y$ lab kits for student experiments. The teacher needs to purchase at least $16$ items in total, but wants to spend no more than $\\[math]600$. If each microscope costs $\\[/math]50$ and each lab kit costs $\\[math]25$, what is the system of inequalities that best models this situation?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221009 1072 #uuid "78d093f7-9689-5326-bfab-6cec8b19e157" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221009 1074 "First, we translate each part of the problem into an inequality:\n\n- There must be at least $16$ in total, so we have\n$x + y \\geq 16$.\n- We're told that the total cost cannot exceed $\\[math]600$. Since each microscope costs $\\[/math]50$ and each lab kit costs $\\[math]25$, we have\n$50x + 25y \\leq 600$.\n\nTherefore, putting it all together, the system of inequalities that models this situation is\n\n$$\n{x + y \\ge 16; 50x + 25y \\le 600\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221010 1069 "e-20355" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221010 1071 "A car’s fuel efficiency includes a base rate of $e$ km/L plus an improvement of $7\\%$ based on engine tuning hours $h$. The goal is for the total efficiency to be at least $1.2$ times and at most $1.8$ times the base rate. Find the inequality that shows all possible values of $h$ that meet this goal." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221010 1072 #uuid "ac4a2d61-4017-507e-bf33-4d903f04a09d" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221010 1074 "We are told that the total efficiency is $e + 0.07h$.\n\nTo meet the goal, it must be at least $1.2e$ and at most $1.8e$. So, we have\n\n$$\n1.2e \\leq e + 0.07h \\leq 1.8e\n$$\n\nThen, we subtract $e$ throughout:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1.2e - e & \\le 0.07h \\le 1.8e - e \\\\ 0.2e & \\le 0.07h \\le 0.8e\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide by $0.07{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{0.2e}{0.07} \\leq h \\leq \\dfrac{0.8e}{0.07}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inequality is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{0.2e}{0.07} \\leq h \\leq \\dfrac{0.8e}{0.07}\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221011 1069 "e-20359" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221011 1071 "A volunteer estimates that she delivers between $5$ and $8$ gallons of water per trip. Based on this estimate, find the inequality that represents the total number of gallons, $g$, she could deliver in $15$ trips." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221011 1072 #uuid "5072919f-8877-54c9-a57e-62150e9bdecd" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221011 1074 "We are told that the volunteer delivers between $5$ and $8$ gallons per trip.\n\nTo find the total number of gallons delivered in $15$ trips, we multiply both the minimum $(5)$ and maximum $(8)$ gallons by $15$. This gives\n$5 \\times 15 \\[5pt]$\nand\n$8 \\times 15$.\n\nSo, the total number of gallons $g$ must satisfy\n\n$$\n5 \\cdot 15 \\leq g \\leq 8 \\cdot 15\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inequality is\n\n$$\n5 \\cdot 15 \\leq g \\leq 8 \\cdot 15\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221012 1069 "e-20365" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221012 1071 "${x + 3y = 12; 4(x + 3y) + y = 55$\n\nIf $(x, y)$ is the solution to the given system of equations, what is the value of $y$?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221012 1072 #uuid "a142d1f2-0507-5919-ab0b-86e303129f7d" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221012 1074 "We can substitute\n\n$$\nx + 3y = 12\n$$\n\nfrom the first equation into the second equation to solve for $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4(x + 3y) + y &= 55 \\\\\n4(12) + y &= 55 \\\\\n48 + y &= 55 \\\\\ny &= 55 - 48 \\\\\ny &= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $y$ is $7$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221013 1069 "e-20366" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221013 1071 "${\\frac{1}{4}y = 12; x - \\frac{1}{4}y = 2$\n\nThe system of equations above has solution $(x, y)$. What is the value of $x$?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221013 1072 #uuid "f15e8e8f-a4f4-5880-879a-9a05b7700f15" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221013 1074 "First, we solve the first equation for $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{4}y &= 12 \\\\\ny &= 12 \\cdot 4 \\\\\ny &= 48\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute this value into the second equation to solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - \\frac{1}{4}y &= 2 \\\\\nx - \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot 48 &= 2 \\\\\nx - 12 &= 2 \\\\\nx &= 2 + 12 \\\\\nx &= 14\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $x$ is $14$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221014 1069 "e-20390" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221014 1071 "${\\frac{8}{5}x - 3y = 6; \\frac{2}{5}x - 3y =-6$\n\nThe solution to the given system of equations is $(x, y)$. What is the value of $\\dfrac{3}{5}x - y$?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221014 1072 #uuid "ba74c335-95ae-54e3-b518-083168dfc8e8" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221014 1074 "We will solve the system using the elimination method.\n\nNotice that if we multiply the second equation by $-1$, then we will get a $+3y$ term that can cancel with the $-3y$ term in the first equation:\n\n$$\n{\\frac{8}{5}x - 3y = 6; \\frac{2}{5}x - 3y =-6 \\mid \\mid \\cdot (-1)\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{8}{5}x- 3y &= 6 \\\\\n- \\frac{2}{5}x+ 3y &= 6 \\\\\n(\\frac{8}{5}x - \\frac{2}{5}x) &= 12 \\\\\n\\frac{6}{5}x &= 12 \\\\\nx &= 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substitute $x = 10$ into one of the original equations to solve for $y$. We'll use the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{2}{5}x - 3y &= -6 \\\\\n\\frac{2}{5}(10) - 3y &= -6 \\\\\n4 - 3y &= -6 \\\\\n- 3y &= -10 \\\\\ny &= \\frac{10}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{5}x - y{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3}{5}x - y &= \\frac{3}{5}(10) - \\frac{10}{3} \\\\\n&= 6 - \\frac{10}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{18 - 10}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{8}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the value of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{5}x - y\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{8}{3}\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221015 1069 "e-20392" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221015 1071 "${3x + 2y = 8; 4x - 3y = 5$\n\nIf $(x, y)$ is the solution to the system of equations above, what is the value of $2x + 3y$?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221015 1072 #uuid "427c5311-5c8f-5636-bc3b-59125cc20438" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221015 1074 "We will solve the system using elimination.\n\nNotice that if we multiply the first equation by $3$ and the second equation by $2$, then we will get a $6y$ term that can cancel with the $-6y$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n{3x + 2y = 8 \\mid \\mid \\cdot (3); 4x - 3y = 5 \\mid \\mid \\cdot (2)\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n9x + 6y &= 24 \\\\\n8x- 6y &= 10 \\\\\n(9x + 8x) &= (24 + 10) \\\\\n17x &= 34 \\\\\nx &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substitute $x = 2$ into one of the original equations to solve for $y$. We'll use the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x + 2y &= 8 \\\\\n3(2) + 2y &= 8 \\\\\n6 + 2y &= 8 \\\\\n2y &= 2 \\\\\ny &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $2x + 3y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x + 3y &= 2(2) + 3(1) \\\\\n&= 4 + 3 \\\\\n&= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the value of $2x + 3y$ is $7$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221016 1069 "e-20401" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221016 1071 "${6p - 7q = 2; - 3p + 4q = 5$\n\nThe solution to the given system of equations is $(p, q)$. What is the value of $pq$?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221016 1072 #uuid "a13c0ae2-efb9-5375-81fd-63dd3a4a76de" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221016 1074 "We will solve the system using the elimination method.\n\nNotice that if we multiply the second equation by $2$, then we will get a $-6p$ term that can cancel with the $6p$ term in the first equation:\n\n$$\n{6p - 7q = 2; - 3p + 4q = 5 \\mid \\mid \\cdot 2\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6p - 7q &= 2 \\\\\n- 6p + 8q &= 10 \\\\\n(-7q + 8q) &= (2 + 10) \\\\\nq &= 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substitute $q = 12$ into one of the original equations to solve for $p$. We'll use the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6p - 7q &= 2 \\\\\n6p - 7(12) &= 2 \\\\\n6p - 84 &= 2 \\\\\n6p &= 86 \\\\\np &= \\frac{43}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get\n\n$$\npq = \\dfrac{43}{3} \\cdot 12 = 172\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221017 1069 "e-20409" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221017 1071 "During a bus ride through hilly terrain, a driver covered $d$ kilometers downhill at $25\\,\\text{km/h}$ and $u$ kilometers uphill at $10\\,\\text{km/h}$. The total distance of the trip was $55$ kilometers, and the bus spent three times as many hours going uphill as it did going downhill. How many kilometers did the bus travel uphill?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221017 1072 #uuid "28088664-ec27-5106-a26b-b76ca7c903aa" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221017 1074 "Let's establish the linear equations that model the given situation.\n\n- The total distance of the trip was $55$ kilometers, so\n$d + u = 55$.\n- Since $\\text{time} = \\dfrac{\\text{distance}}{\\text{speed}}$, the bus spent $\\dfrac{d}{25}$ hours going downhill and $\\dfrac{u}{10}$ hours going uphill. Because the bus spent three times as many hours uphill as downhill, we have\n$\\dfrac{u}{10} = 3 \\cdot \\dfrac{d}{25}$.\n\nPutting these equations together, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{d + u = 55; \\frac{u}{10} = 3 \\cdot \\frac{d}{25}\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using the substitution method. We solve for $d$ in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd + u &= 55 \\\\\nd &= 55 - u\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can substitute $d = 55 - u$ into the second equation and solve for $u{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{u}{10} &= 3 \\cdot \\frac{d}{25} \\\\\n\\frac{u}{10} &= 3 \\cdot \\frac{55 - u}{25} \\\\\nu &= 30 \\cdot \\frac{55 - u}{25} \\\\\nu &= (6(55 - u))/(5) \\\\\n5u &= 6(55 - u) \\\\\n5u &= 330 - 6u \\\\\n11u &= 330 \\\\\nu &= \\frac{330}{11} \\\\\nu &= 30\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the bus traveled $30$ kilometers uphill." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221018 1069 "e-20411" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221018 1071 "A garden path that is $46\\,\\text{m}$ long is divided into two segments. One segment has a length of $x\\,\\text{m}$ and the other has a length of $y\\,\\text{m}$. The value of $x$ is $4$ more than $2$ times the value of $y$. What is the value of $x$?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221018 1072 #uuid "50c169f5-f080-5027-9f08-78bc5f4fcd62" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221018 1074 "Let's establish the linear equations that model the given situation.\n\n- The total length of the path is $46$ meters. Therefore, we have\n$x + y = 46$.\n- Since the value of $x$ is $4$ more than $2$ times the value of $y$, we have\n$x = 2y + 4$.\n\nPutting these equations together, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{x + y = 46; x = 2y + 4\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using the substitution method. The second equation\n\n$$\nx = 2y + 4\n$$\n\nis already solved for $x$, so we can substitute it into the first equation and solve for $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + y &= 46 \\\\\n(2y + 4) + y &= 46 \\\\\n3y + 4 &= 46 \\\\\n3y &= 42 \\\\\ny &= \\frac{42}{3} \\\\\ny &= 14\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $y = 14$ back into the equation\n\n$$\nx = 2y + 4\n$$\n\nto solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= 2y + 4 \\\\\n&= 2(14) + 4 \\\\\n&= 28 + 4 \\\\\n&= 32\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221019 1069 "e-20412" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221019 1071 "A market sold $25$ baskets of vegetables containing either carrots or onions. Each basket of carrots costs $\\[math]3$, and each basket of onions costs $\\[/math]4$. If the total revenue from all sales was $\\[math]82$, how much, in dollars, was collected from selling the onion baskets?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221019 1072 #uuid "5c34fab4-d2e9-5a26-a5dd-a4a7fcda5968" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221019 1074 "Let $c$ and $n$ be the number of carrot and onion baskets sold, respectively.\n\nLet's establish the linear equations that model the given situation.\n\n- The market sold $25$ baskets in total. Therefore,\n$c + n = 25$.\n- The total money collected was $\\[math]82$. Since a carrot basket costs $\\[/math]3$ and an onion basket costs $\\[math]4$, we have\n$3c + 4n = 82$.\n\nPutting these equations together, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{c + n = 25; 3c + 4n = 82\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using the substitution method. We solve for $c$ in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nc + n &= 25 \\\\\nc &= 25 - n\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can substitute $c = 25 - n$ into the second equation and solve for $n{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3c + 4n &= 82 \\\\\n3(25 - n) + 4n &= 82 \\\\\n75 - 3n + 4n &= 82 \\\\\nn + 75 &= 82 \\\\\nn &= 82 - 75 \\\\\nn &= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since each onion basket costs $\\[/math]4$, the total amount collected from the sale of onion baskets is\n\n$$\n\\[math]4 \\times 7 = 28\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221020 1069 "e-20414" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221020 1071 "A chemist prepared a $90.0\\,\\textrm{mL}$ salt solution that is $25.0\\%$ salt by volume. This solution was created by mixing two other salt solutions. The first solution was $10.0\\%$ salt, and the second was $40.0\\%$ salt.\n\nWhat was the volume, in milliliters, of the salt contained in the first solution used to create the final mixture?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221020 1072 #uuid "12253190-da6b-52bd-9459-82851efe8642" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221020 1074 "Let $x$ and $y$ be the volume (in milliliters) of the first and second solutions, respectively.\n\nLet's establish the linear equations that model the given situation.\n\n- The total volume was $90.0\\,\\text{mL}$, so\n$x + y = 90.0$.\n- We are given that the total salt content is $25.0\\%$ of $90.0\\,\\text{mL}$. Therefore,\n$25.0% \\cdot 90.0mL|= 0.25 \\cdot 90.0mL; = 22.50mL$.\nWe are also told that the first solution was $10.0\\%$ salt and the second was $40.0\\%$ salt. Therefore, we get the equation\n$0.10x + 0.40y = 22.50$.\n\nPutting the equations together, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{x + y = 90.0; 0.10x + 0.40y = 22.50\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using the substitution method. We solve for $y$ in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + y &= 90.0 \\\\\ny &= 90.0 - x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can substitute\n\n$$\ny = 90.0 - x\n$$\n\ninto the second equation and solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.10x + 0.40y &= 22.50 \\\\\n0.10x + 0.40(90.0 - x) &= 22.50 \\\\\n0.10x + 36.0 - 0.40x &= 22.50 \\\\\n- 0.30x &= -13.50 \\\\\nx &= \\frac{-13.50}{-0.30} \\\\\nx &= 45.0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the first solution was $10.0\\%$ salt by volume, the volume of salt in the first solution used was\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.10x &= 0.10(45.0) \\\\\n&= 4.5mL\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221021 1069 "e-20415" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221021 1071 "In a school reading poll, four times as many students preferred fiction over nonfiction. It was also reported that $120$ more students preferred fiction than nonfiction. According to this data, how many students preferred nonfiction?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221021 1072 #uuid "c98e80f7-2be6-53f2-a30a-234bdb0ff2b5" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221021 1074 "Let $f$ be the number of students who preferred fiction, and $n$ be the number who preferred nonfiction.\n\nLet's establish the linear equations that model the given situation.\n\n- The poll states that four times as many students preferred fiction over nonfiction. Therefore, we have\n$f = 4n$.\n- It was also reported that $120$ more students preferred fiction than nonfiction. So, we have\n$f = n + 120$.\n\nPutting these equations together, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{f = 4n; f = n + 120\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using the substitution method. The first equation $f = 4n$ is already solved for $f$, so we can substitute it into the second equation and solve for $n{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf &= n + 120 \\\\\n4n &= n + 120 \\\\\n4n - n &= 120 \\\\\n3n &= 120 \\\\\nn &= \\frac{120}{3} \\\\\nn &= 40\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $40$ students preferred nonfiction." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221022 1069 "e-20418" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221022 1071 "At a restaurant, one diner paid $\\[math]155.50$ for $6$ main courses and $5$ drinks. Another diner paid $\\[/math]72.50$ for $3$ main courses and $2$ drinks.\n\nWhat is the cost, in dollars, of one drink?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221022 1072 #uuid "c3f07c8c-efed-556d-885e-6b8e2a3f46f4" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221022 1074 "Let $m$ and $d$ be the cost of one main course and the cost of one drink, respectively.\n\nLet's establish the linear equations that model the given situation.\n\n- The first diner paid $\\[math]155.50$ for $6$ mains and $5$ drinks, so we have\n$6m + 5d = 155.50$.\n- The second diner paid $\\[/math]72.50$ for $3$ mains and $2$ drinks, so we have\n$3m + 2d = 72.50$.\n\nPutting these equations together, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{6m + 5d = 155.50; 3m + 2d = 72.50\n$$\n\nWe will solve the above system using the elimination method. Notice that the $m$-coefficient in the first equation is ${\\color{red}{6}}$, while in the second equation it is ${\\color{blue}{3}}$.\n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $-{\\color{red}{2}}$, then the $m$-coefficient in the first and second equations will be $6$ and $-6$, respectively, and they will cancel when we add the equations.\n\n$$\n{6m + 5d = 155.50; (3m + 2d = 72.50) \\cdot (-2)\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $m$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6m + 5d &= 155.50 \\\\\n- 6m - 4d &= -145.00 \\\\\n(5d - 4d) &= (155.50 - 145.00) \\\\\nd &= 10.50\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the cost of one drink is $\\[math]10.50$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221023 1069 "e-20423" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221023 1071 "A school organized $40$ student teams for a math competition. Some teams had $3$ members, and the rest had $6$ members. If every team was full and $156$ students participated, how many of the teams had $3$ members?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221023 1072 #uuid "74309431-1979-5d30-a771-fb260c3defb5" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221023 1074 "Let $x$ and $y$ be the number of $3$-member and $6$-member teams, respectively.\n\nLet's establish the linear equations that model the given situation.\n\n- The total number of teams was $40$, so\n$x + y = 40$.\n- The total number of students was $156$. Since $3$ students fill each $3$-member team and $6$ fill each $6$-member team, we have\n$3x + 6y = 156$.\n\nPutting these equations together, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{x + y = 40; 3x + 6y = 156\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using the substitution method. We solve for $y$ in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + y &= 40 \\\\\ny &= 40 - x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can substitute $y = 40 - x$ into the second equation and solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x + 6y &= 156 \\\\\n3x + 6(40 - x) &= 156 \\\\\n3x + 240 - 6x &= 156 \\\\\n- 3x &= -84 \\\\\nx &= ((-84))/((-3)) \\\\\nx &= 28\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the number of $3$-member teams was $28$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221024 1069 "e-20431" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221024 1071 "![](images/6f/6f9f13b5becabd1cdc61e4409ab8301fc73f1dbf25e044afc1a9246691e88a3a.png)\n\nWhat system of linear equations represents the graph shown above?\n\n${-2x + y =-5; x + 2y = 0$\n\n${2x - y = 5; x - 2y = 0$\n\n${x + 2y = 5; 2x + y = 0$\n\n${2x - y =-5; x - 2y = 0$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221024 1072 #uuid "dd927003-15eb-59a6-8dc4-b464e8239d5c" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221024 1074 "Recall that each equation of a linear system defines a straight line. The solution to the system corresponds to the point where the two lines intersect.\n\nLet’s find the equations of the lines.\n\n- For the first (orange) line, we pick two points on the line.\nFor example, this line passes through $(0, -5)$ and $(1, -3)$.Now, we compute the slope:\n$m = \\frac{-3 - (-5)}{1 - 0} = \\frac{2}{1} = 2$\nUsing the slope and the point $(0, -5)$, we can write the equation in slope-point form:\n$y + 5|= 2(x - 0); y|= 2x - 5$\nand rewrite it in standard form:\n$y|= 2x - 5; - 2x + y|=-5$\n- For the second (blue) line, we pick two points on the line.\nThis line passes through $(0, 0)$ and $(2, -1)$.Now, we compute the slope:\n$m = \\frac{-1 - 0}{2 - 0} = -\\frac{1}{2}$\nUsing the slope and the point $(0, 0)$, we can write the equation in slope-point form:\n$y|=-\\frac{1}{2}x$\nand rewrite it in standard form:\n$y|=-\\frac{1}{2}x; 2y|=-x; x + 2y|= 0$\n\nSo, the system that matches the graph is the following:\n\n$$\n{-2x + y =-5; x + 2y = 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is **only I.**" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221025 1069 "e-20432" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221025 1071 "![](images/be/be55795a3af8da6c91c4a21b813a438622f49801992bbd9855597bb7e56b88b2.png)\n\nWhich of the following systems of equations has the same solution as the system of equations graphed above?\n\n${x =-4; y = 1$\n\n${x = 4; y =-1$\n\n${x = 1; y =-4$\n\n${x =-1; y = 4$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221025 1072 #uuid "34a9957b-d428-5b7e-897d-d7dd1eb21e27" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221025 1074 "The solution to a system of two linear equations corresponds to the point where the two lines intersect.\n\nBy inspecting the graph, we can see that the lines intersect at the point $(1, -4)$.\n\nSo, the solution can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n{x = 1; y =-4\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is **only III.**" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221026 1069 "e-20433" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221026 1071 "![](images/57/5747249bf065187e832b2ebef180ddfa91e4c4c0adb9ca11907ae9d26e8cf903.png)\n\nWhich of the graphs above represents the following system of linear equations?\n${x + 3y =-3; 2x - 5y = 10$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221026 1072 #uuid "6ed9ed3f-c2e7-52a1-ba42-2e4203ae6942" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221026 1074 "To determine which graph represents this system, we first rewrite each equation in slope-intercept form.\n\n- For the first equation, we have the following:\n$x + 3y|=-3; 3y|=-x - 3; y|=-\\frac{1}{3}x - 1$\nThis line has a slope of $-\\dfrac{1}{3}$ and a $y$-intercept of $-1$.\n- For the second equation, we have the following:\n$2x - 5y|= 10; - 5y|=-2x + 10; y|= \\frac{2}{5}x - 2$\nThis line has a slope of $\\dfrac{2}{5}$ and a $y$-intercept of $-2$.\n\nNow, we draw both lines on the same graph.\n\n- For the first line, $y = -\\dfrac{1}{3}x - 1{:}$If $x = 0$, then $y = -1$.If $y = 0$, then $x = -3$.So, the line passes through points $(0, -1)$ and $(-3, 0)$.\n- For the second line, $y = \\dfrac{2}{5}x - 2{:}$If $x = 0$, then $y = -2$.If $y = 0$, then $x = 5$.So, the line passes through points $(0, -2)$ and $(5, 0)$.\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following graph, where the intersection of the lines represents the solution to our system.\n\n![](images/2c/2ca4fd9da55dfbe61ead9f6b933d2a85a5a72cacbd59fadb9b6c1fafbb84bbdd.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is graph II." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221027 1069 "e-20441" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221027 1071 "![](images/0c/0c6b28dfffaacdd0a44c7a7af75902cfd9fcc39eefaefa52f9b70a04e9db1ddb.png)\n\nIn the figure above, $\\overline{LN}$ and $\\overline{MO}$ intersect at point $P$, $MP = NP$, and $LP = PO$. What is the measure, in degrees, of $\\angle NLO$?\n\nNote: Figure not drawn to scale." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221027 1072 #uuid "277b20ad-4979-5083-9dc5-09abbb92f370" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221027 1074 "First, since $\\angle NLP$ is a straight angle and\n\n$$\nm\\angle NPO=58^\\circ\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠LPO + m∠NPO &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nm∠LPO + 58^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nm∠LPO &= 122^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, consider $\\triangle LPO$.\n\nSince $LP=PO$, it is an isosceles triangle with the base $\\overline{LO}$.\nSo, the base angles are equal:\n\n$$\nm\\angle PLO = m\\angle OLP\n$$\n\nThe sum of the measures of the interior angles of a triangle is $180^\\circ$. For $\\triangle LPO$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠PLO + m∠OLP + m∠LPO &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n2m∠PLO + 122^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n2m∠PLO &= 58^{∘} \\\\\nm∠PLO &= 29^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $P$ lies on $\\overline{LN}$, the angle between $\\overline{NL}$ and $\\overline{LO}$ is the same as the angle between $\\overline{PL}$ and $\\overline{LO}$. Therefore,\n\n$$\nm\\angle NLO = m\\angle PLO = 29^\\circ\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221028 1069 "e-20442" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221028 1071 "![](images/ea/ea8be1c273198a8493bd07f723d4002e1e172b03cb38c19dc1885d2d6cce260b.png)\n\nIn the figure above, points $B$, $C$, $D$, and $E$ lie on line segment $\\overline{AF}$, line segment $\\overline{CZ}$ intersects line segment $\\overline{DY}$ at point $M$, $m\\angle DBY = 57^\\circ$, $m\\angle DYB = 71^\\circ$, $m\\angle DMZ = 88^\\circ$, and $m\\angle FEZ = 148^\\circ$. What is the measure, in degrees, of $\\angle EZC$?\n\nNote: *Figure not drawn to scale.*" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221028 1072 #uuid "ec995f34-4ce4-5414-992b-d7399c82bc71" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221028 1074 "First, let's represent all the given angles on the graph.\n\n![](images/d7/d7145517674249fc9abdbe2f9925dc13806ffd634e58832a83b54607fcc5041b.png)\n\nConsider $\\triangle DBY$.\n\nSince the sum of the measures of the interior angles of a triangle is $180^\\circ$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠DBY + m∠BYD + m∠DYB &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n57^{∘} + m∠BYD + 71^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n128^{∘} + m∠BYD &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nm∠BYD &= 52^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following (we disregard the angles that we already used):\n\n![](images/7d/7d94eab2f7c6d0cb1d7b4945bcad33de71a357a3e1d20bc793a4e8bf115c8c95.png)\n\nSince $\\angle CMZ$ is a straight angle and\n\n$$\nm\\angle DMZ=88^\\circ\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠DMC + m∠DMZ &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nm∠DMC + 88^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nm∠DMC &= 92^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider now $\\triangle CDM$.\n\nUsing again that the sum of the measures of the interior angles of a triangle is $180^\\circ$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠CDM + m∠DCM + m∠DMC &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n52^{∘} + m∠DCM + 92^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n144^{∘} + m∠DCM &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nm∠DCM &= 36^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the following (we disregard the angles that we already used):\n\n![](images/d3/d3ea2c62f3ea13a1713a04737207ada68749035580a768370bb5d2051b13f0de.png)\n\nSince $\\angle AEF$ is straight, it has a measure of $180^\\circ$. At $E$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠ZEC + m∠FEZ &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nm∠ZEC + 148^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nm∠ZEC &= 32^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in $\\triangle EZC$, the sum of the measures of the interior angles is $180^\\circ$. This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠ZEC + m∠EZC + m∠ECZ &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n32^{∘} + m∠EZC + 36^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n68^{∘} + m∠EZC &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nm∠EZC &= 112^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221029 1069 "e-20444" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221029 1071 "![](images/30/30cfa9a498f6d49947aeabdb3d044621e50cc1c31d277be1c3fbdc67836892fa.png)\n\nIn the figure, $RS = SP$, $m\\angle TQS = 21^\\circ$, and $m\\angle RQT = 74^\\circ$. What is the value of $x$?\n\nNote: Figure not drawn to scale." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221029 1072 #uuid "386748fd-b371-5979-bd24-3af0ddac90cd" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221029 1074 "First, let's represent all the given angles on the graph.\n\n![](images/be/bec60842e41a3c10bc161c5617fc5fe9513c6bd336d814f9aecc40c0c5989362.png)\n\nFirst, since $\\angle PQR$ is a straight angle and\n\n$$\nm\\angle RQT=74^\\circ\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠PQT + m∠RQT &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nm∠PQT + 74^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nm∠PQT &= 106^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, consider $\\triangle PQT$.\n\nSince the sum of the measures of the interior angles of a triangle is $180^\\circ$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠PQT + m∠QTS + m∠P &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n106^{∘} + 21^{∘} + m∠P &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n127^{∘} + m∠P &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nm∠P &= 53^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following:\n\n![](images/fd/fdaa98730825598272c7ee09517c7b5d19f42e071e970b9dfc8155a864b0a1c9.png)\n\nConsider $\\triangle RSP$.\n\nSince $RS = SP$, it is an isosceles triangle with the base $\\overline{RP}$.\nSo, the base angles are equal:\n\n$$\nm\\angle R = m\\angle P = 53^\\circ\n$$\n\nThe sum of the measures of the interior angles of a triangle is $180^\\circ$. For $\\triangle RSP$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠P + m∠R + m∠RSP &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n2m∠P + m∠RSP &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n2(53^{∘}) + x^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n106^{∘} + x^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nx^{∘} &= 74^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $x$ is $74$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221030 1069 "e-20445" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221030 1071 "![](images/f1/f199dda1267b921d4c9cd5f5a65f55346dd6abce81f5ae100357f7f0aee58d43.png)\n\nIn the figure above, $PM = PO$. What is the value of $x$?\n\nNote: Figure not drawn to scale." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221030 1072 #uuid "75a95c5c-2d82-52c2-8ea6-66bfa5376832" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221030 1074 "Consider $\\triangle MPO$.\n\nSince $PM=PO$, it is an isosceles triangle with the base $\\overline{MO}$.\nSo, the base angles are equal:\n\n$$\nm\\angle M = m\\angle O\n$$\n\nThe sum of the measures of the interior angles of a triangle is $180^\\circ$. For $\\triangle MPO$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠MPO + m∠M + m∠O &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n132^{∘} + m∠O + m∠O &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n132^{∘} + 2m∠O &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n2m∠O &= 48^{∘} \\\\\nm∠O &= 24^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, consider $\\triangle ONQ$.\n\nUsing again that the sum of the measures of the interior angles of a triangle is $180^\\circ$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠ONQ + m∠O + m∠Q &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nm∠ONQ + 24^{∘} + 19^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nm∠ONQ + 43^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nm∠ONQ &= 137^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $\\angle MON$ is a straight angle and\n\n$$\nm\\angle ONQ=137^\\circ\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠QNM + m∠ONQ &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nx^{∘} + 137^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nx^{∘} &= 43^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $x$ is $43$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221031 1069 "e-20446" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221031 1071 "![](images/0e/0e25438d7740bed734491e36d1099afc060dade73ff6d6864e7b0369ff6a9ca1.png)\n\nIntersecting lines $r$, $s$, and $t$ are shown. What is the value of $x$?\n\nNote: Figure not drawn to scale." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221031 1072 #uuid "307f72eb-c6fe-5892-8772-9e7266ab9093" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221031 1074 "First, let the measure of the acute angle formed at the intersection of lines $s$ and $t$ be $y^\\circ$, and the measure of the acute angle formed at the intersection of lines $s$ and $r$ be $z^\\circ$.\n\n![](images/b5/b5e8227d69bd987774806bda3bb5ad56d5bc8c07d3d67851dfeec1f9c58a2988.png)\n\nNow, since the straight angle formed on line $s$ has a measure of $180^\\circ$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n113^{∘} + y^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\ny^{∘} &= 67^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe sum of the measures of the interior angles of a triangle is $180^\\circ$. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{∘} + z^{∘} + 36^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n67^{∘} + z^{∘} + 36^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n103^{∘} + z^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nz^{∘} &= 77^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the straight angle formed on line $r$ also has a measure of $180^\\circ$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{∘} + z^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nx^{∘} + 77^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nx^{∘} &= 103^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $x$ is $103$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221032 1069 "e-20448" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221032 1071 "In $\\triangle A B C$, the measure of $\\angle A$ is $70^{\\circ}$. If $\\triangle ABC$ is isosceles, which of the following is **not** a possible measure of $\\angle B$?\n\n1. $62^\\circ$\n2. $70^\\circ$\n3. $55^\\circ$\n4. $40^\\circ$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221032 1072 #uuid "35c88a5f-ab3b-52f8-9695-8e318df75d54" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221032 1074 "In $\\triangle ABC$, the sum of the measures of the interior angles is $180^\\circ{:}$\n\n$$\nm\\angle A + m\\angle B + m\\angle C = 180^\\circ\n$$\n\nSince $\\triangle ABC$ is isosceles, it must have at least two equal angles. Let's consider the distinct possibilities.\n\n- If $m\\angle B = m\\angle A = 70^\\circ$, then $70^\\circ$ is a possible measure of $\\angle B$.\n- If $m\\angle B = m\\angle C$, then we have\n$m∠A + m∠B + m∠C|= 180^{∘}; m∠A + m∠B + m∠B|= 180^{∘}; m∠A + 2m∠B|= 180^{∘}; 70^{∘} + 2m∠B|= 180^{∘}; 2m∠B|= 110^{∘}; m∠B|= 55^{∘}$.\nSo, $55^\\circ$ is a possible measure of $\\angle B$.\n- If $m\\angle C = m\\angle A = 70^\\circ$, then we have\n$m∠A + m∠B + m∠C|= 180^{∘}; 70^{∘} + m∠B + 70^{∘}|= 180^{∘}; 140^{∘} + m∠B|= 180^{∘}; m∠B|= 40^{∘}$.\nSo, $40^\\circ$ is a possible measure of $\\angle B$.\n\nTherefore, among the choices, only $62^\\circ$ is not a possible measure of $\\angle B$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221033 1069 "e-20449" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221033 1071 "In $\\triangle A U V$, the measures of $\\angle A$ and $\\angle V$ are each $80^{\\circ}$. What is the measure of $\\angle U$, in degrees?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221033 1072 #uuid "39f0c9e6-0d17-5ad5-8bfb-e7b398e5c4bd" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221033 1074 "The sum of the measures of the interior angles of a triangle is $180^\\circ$. For the given $\\triangle AUV$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠A + m∠U + m∠V &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n80^{∘} + m∠U + 80^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n160^{∘} + m∠U &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nm∠U &= 180^{∘} - 160^{∘} \\\\\nm∠U &= 20^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221034 1069 "e-20450" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221034 1071 "![](images/e1/e16dfe26a54dc2695b0234ff0f8401a006509401edbf6d3b50307839acf42026.png)\n\nIn the figure above, two sides of a triangle are extended. What is the value of $x$?\n\nNote: Figure not drawn to scale." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221034 1072 #uuid "2d643610-fe73-5e98-beb7-cf6a89fb5214" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221034 1074 "Let the measure of the unknown interior angle of the given triangle be $y{:}$\n\n![](images/35/3582549e1242eada4ca2ca6a98f041c4e661512cf19ca06560d9e45079b6de6b.png)\n\nIn any triangle, the sum of the measures of the interior angles is always $180^\\circ$.\nSo, in the given triangle, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n45^{∘} + 43^{∘} + y &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n88^{∘} + y &= 180^{∘} \\\\\ny &= 180^{∘} - 88^{∘} \\\\\ny &= 92^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that the interior and exterior angles are supplementary, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + y &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nx + 92^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nx &= 180^{∘} - 92^{∘} \\\\\nx &= 88^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $x$ is $88^\\circ$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221035 1069 "e-20451" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221035 1071 "![](images/19/19459938c47c917b44cbf696162e711d1249da5917b6183e510ef8463030a982.png)\n\nIn the figure, $\\overline{UW}$ extends to point $X$. If $m\\angle UVW = m\\angle VWU$, what is the value of $x$?\n\nNote: Figure not drawn to scale." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221035 1072 #uuid "9751ccc8-7eba-53ad-bcb3-a779b7065655" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221035 1074 "Since $\\angle WUX$ is a straight angle and\n\n$$\nm\\angle VWX=124^\\circ\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠VWU + m∠VWX &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nm∠VWU + 124^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nm∠VWU &= 56^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nm\\angle UVW = m\\angle VWU = 56^\\circ\n$$\n\nIn any triangle, the sum of the measures of the interior angles is always $180^\\circ$. For $\\triangle UVW$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠UVW + m∠VWU + m∠WUV &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nx^{∘} + 56^{∘} + 56^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nx^{∘} + 112^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nx^{∘} &= 68^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $x$ is $68$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221036 1069 "e-20453" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221036 1071 "![](images/c2/c21ba6d4e5143143fe2175eb8942c22a802f795ab03699620ce580ff08271012.png)\n\nIn the right triangle shown above, what is the value of $x$?\n\nNote: Figure not drawn to scale." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221036 1072 #uuid "8bfe4d51-7d30-542c-bf36-361adfde0718" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221036 1074 "In any triangle, the sum of the measures of the interior angles is always $180^\\circ$.\nSo, in the given right triangle, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n90^{∘} + 44^{∘} + x^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n134^{∘} + x^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nx^{∘} &= 180^{∘} - 134^{∘} \\\\\nx^{∘} &= 46^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $x$ is $46$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221037 1069 "e-20454" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221037 1071 "![](images/50/5007cfc030792b3c8438961cd95fe42c36881aef205a295053321eec1926cfea.png)\n\nIn the figure above, $\\overline{RU}$ intersects $\\overline{SV}$ at $T$. If $x = 105^\\circ$, what is the value of $y$?\n\nNote: Figure not drawn to scale." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221037 1072 #uuid "afb0bbe3-b615-5edf-87e9-e293ee341479" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221037 1074 "In $\\triangle RST$, the sum of the interior angles is always $180^\\circ{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠RST + m∠SRT + m∠STR &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nx + 58^{∘} + m∠STR &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n105^{∘} + 58^{∘} + m∠STR &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n163^{∘} + m∠STR &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nm∠STR &= 17^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$\\angle STR$ equals its vertical angle $\\angle V T U{:}$\n\n![](images/41/41f92d7fcf3b98aa17ed4e009d1d382145babbd668e6e09a44b960edcd4f219f.png)\n\nIn $\\triangle U V T$, the sum of the interior angles is always $180^\\circ{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠VTU + m∠TUV + m∠TVU &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n17^{∘} + y + 63^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\ny + 80^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\ny &= 100^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $y$ is $100^\\circ$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221038 1069 "e-20456" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221038 1071 "![](images/55/551a6ed492277818d889b877179df96aa354b957b0d2862591c54d875b4d2743.png)\n\nIn the given triangle, $RS = ST$ and $\\angle SRT$ has a measure of $47^{\\circ}$. What is the value of $x$?\n\nNote: Figure not drawn to scale." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221038 1072 #uuid "48f3ce06-c815-5b9f-8f3e-cdac3bd2b51e" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221038 1074 "Consider $\\triangle RST$. Since $RS=ST$, it is an isosceles triangle with the base $\\overline{RT}$.\n\nSo, the base angles are equal:\n\n$$\nm\\angle SRT = m\\angle RTS = 47^\\circ\n$$\n\nSince the sum of the measures of the interior angles of a triangle is $180^\\circ$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠SRT + m∠RTS + m∠RST &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n47^{∘} + 47^{∘} + x^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n94^{∘} + x^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nx^{∘} &= 86^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $x$ is $86$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221039 1069 "e-20469" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221039 1071 "![](images/d4/d497350ae8def3e2179b469a2c770be994fc4c266b989fc0122c1ead6582d907.png)\n\nTriangles $J K L$ and $U V W$ are shown above. Which of the following is equal to the ratio $\\dfrac{K L}{J K}$?\n\n1. $\\dfrac{V W}{U V}$\n2. $\\dfrac{U V}{V W}$\n3. $\\dfrac{U W}{V W}$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221039 1072 #uuid "cccb3e8f-b407-539c-ab9d-45817d005e99" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221039 1074 "To solve this problem, we use angle measures to show that triangles $JKL$ and $UVW$ are similar by the AA criterion. This lets us identify corresponding sides and determine the ratio\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{KL}{JK}\n$$\n\nWe are given that $\\angle J$ is a right angle and\n\n$$\nm\\angle L = 33^\\circ\n$$\n\nso we can find $m\\angle K$ using the fact that the sum of the interior angles in a triangle is $180^\\circ{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠K &= 180^{∘} - (m∠J + m∠L) \\\\\n&= 180^{∘} - (90^{∘} + 33^{∘}) \\\\\n&= 180^{∘} - 123^{∘} \\\\\n&= 57^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, in the triangles $\\triangle JKL$ and $\\triangle UVW$, there are two pairs of congruent angles:\n\n- $m\\angle J = m\\angle U = 90^\\circ$, and\n- $m\\angle K = m\\angle V = 57^\\circ$.\n\nThus, $\\triangle JKL \\sim \\triangle UVW$ by the *AA similarity criterion*.\n\nTherefore, the ratio\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{KL}{JK}\n$$\n\ncorresponds to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{V W}{U V}\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221040 1069 "e-20470" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221040 1071 "Triangles $EFH$ and $JKM$ are similar, with $\\overline{EF}$ corresponding to $\\overline{JK}$. The perimeter of triangle $EFH$ is $12$ and $JK = 4 EF$. Find the perimeter of triangle $JKM$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221040 1072 #uuid "1cc67475-d2a7-536c-8fb4-0eb09a16990c" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221040 1074 "Recall that similar triangles have proportional corresponding side lengths.\n\nWe are told that\n$JK = 4 EF$.\n\nThus, $\\triangle JKM$ is an enlargement of $\\triangle EFH$ by a scale factor of $4$.\n\nWhen a triangle is scaled by a factor of $k$, the perimeter also scales by the same factor.\n\nTherefore, the perimeter of $\\triangle JKM$ is\n\n$$\np = 4 \\cdot 12 = 48\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221041 1069 "e-20471" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221041 1071 "Triangle $J K L$ is similar to triangle $R S T$, where $J$, $K$, and $L$ correspond to $R$, $S$, and $T$, respectively. In triangle $J K L$, the length of $\\overline{J K}$ is $56$ and the length of $\\overline{K L}$ is $84$. In triangle $R S T$, the length of $\\overline{R S}$ is $28$. What is the length of $\\overline{S T}$?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221041 1072 #uuid "4cf04519-ea44-5678-9b8b-0902f91df60e" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221041 1074 "Since $\\triangle JKL \\sim \\triangle RST$, it follows that\n\n$$\n\\frac{KL}{JK} = \\frac{ST}{RS}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $JK = 56$, $KL = 84$, and $RS = 28$, we substitute these values and solve for $ST{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{84}{56} &= \\frac{ST}{28} \\\\\n\\frac{3}{2} &= \\frac{ST}{28} \\\\\nST &= \\frac{3}{2} \\cdot 28 \\\\\n&= 42\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221042 1069 "e-20477" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221042 1071 "Consider the equations $4x - y = 1$ and $mx + ny = 1$, where $m$ and $n$ are constants. The graphs of these equations in the $xy$-plane represent perpendicular lines. Which of the following pairs of equations also represents a pair of perpendicular lines?\n\n1. $4x + y = 1$ and $mx + 2ny = 1$\n2. $4x + y = 1$ and $mx - ny = 1$\n3. $8x - 2y = 1$ and $2mx + ny = 1$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221042 1072 #uuid "00e98380-579b-55b2-b404-3d3e449b5909" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221042 1074 "To find the slope of the line $4x - y = 1$, we rewrite the equation in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n-y = -4x + 1 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad y = 4x - 1\n$$\n\nSo, the slope is $4$.\n\nNow, a line is perpendicular to another line when its slope is the negative reciprocal of that other line's slope. That means the second line must have slope\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nFrom the line equation\n\n$$\nmx + ny = 1\n$$\n\nit follows that the slope of the second line is\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{m}{n}\n$$\n\nSo we set\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{m}{n} = -\\dfrac{1}{4} \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\dfrac{m}{n} = \\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nNow, let's utilise this information to check which pair of lines is perpendicular:\n\n- First, we consider the following lines:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4x + y = 1 & ⇒y =-4x + 1 \\\\ mx + 2ny = 1 & ⇒y =-\\frac{m}{2n}x + \\frac{1}{2n}\\end{bmatrix}$\nThe slope of the first line is $-4$.\nThe slope of the second line is\n$-\\frac{m}{2n}|=-\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{m}{n}; =-\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{4}; =-\\frac{1}{8}$.\nSince the product of slopes does not equal $-1$, this pair of lines is NOT perpendicular.\n- Next, we consider the following lines:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4x + y = 1 & ⇒y =-4x + 1 \\\\ mx - ny = 1 & ⇒y = \\frac{m}{n}x - \\frac{1}{n}\\end{bmatrix}$\nThe slope of the first line is $-4$.\nThe slope of the second line is\n$\\dfrac m n = \\dfrac 1 4$.\nSince the product of the slopes equals $-1$, this pair of lines is perpendicular.\n- Finally, we consider the following lines:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}8x - 2y = 1 & ⇒y = 4x - \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 2mx + ny = 1 & ⇒y =-\\frac{2m}{n}x + \\frac{1}{n}\\end{bmatrix}$\nThe slope of the first line is $4$.\nThe slope of the second line is\n$-\\frac{2m}{n}|=-2 \\cdot \\frac{m}{n}; =-2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{4}; =-\\frac{1}{2}$\nSince the product of slopes does not equal $-1$, this pair of lines is NOT perpendicular.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221043 1069 "e-20478" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221043 1071 "In the $xy$-plane, line $p$ is defined by $-3x + y = 5$. Line $q$ is perpendicular to line $p$ and has a $y$-intercept at $(0, -4)$. Find the equation of line $q$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221043 1072 #uuid "67dc8d4e-9cc9-558c-8c46-72e4388bed05" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221043 1074 "First, we rewrite the given line in slope-intercept form to find its slope:\n\n$$\n-3x + y = 5 \\quad \\implies \\quad y = 3x + 5\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of line $p$ is $3$.\n\nA line perpendicular to this line will have slope\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nsince the slopes of perpendicular lines are negative reciprocals.\n\nThe perpendicular line must pass through $(0, -4)$. So its equation in slope-intercept form is\n\n$$\ny = -\\frac{1}{3}x - 4\n$$\n\nTo write this in standard form, we multiply both sides by 3 and rearrange:\n\n$$\nx + 3y = -12\n$$\n\nTherefore, an equation of line $q$ is\n\n$$\nx + 3y = -12\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221044 1069 "e-20479" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221044 1071 "Line $b$ in the $xy$-plane is perpendicular to the line with equation $x = -8$. What is the slope of line $b$?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221044 1072 #uuid "b0776464-75c0-5220-955e-3ed39ff80818" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221044 1074 "The line given by $x = -8$ is a vertical line, which has an undefined slope.\n\nA line perpendicular to a vertical line is horizontal, and the slope of any horizontal line is $0$. Therefore, the slope of line $b$ is $0$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221045 1069 "e-20482" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221045 1071 "Line $f$ is defined by $\\dfrac{4}{7}x - \\dfrac{2}{3}y + 6 = 0$. Line $g$ is perpendicular to line $f$ in the $xy$-plane. What is the slope of line $g$?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221045 1072 #uuid "b547dcc3-1732-5449-a00d-661340172d1e" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221045 1074 "First, we rewrite the equation in slope-intercept form to find the slope of line $f{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{4}{7}x - \\frac{2}{3}y + 6 &= 0 \\\\\n- \\frac{2}{3}y &= -\\frac{4}{7}x - 6 \\\\\ny &= \\frac{6}{7}x + 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of line $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6}{7}\n$$\n\nA line perpendicular to this line has a slope that is the negative reciprocal. That means we flip the fraction and change the sign.\n\nThe reciprocal of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6}{7}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7}{6}\n$$\n\nand taking the opposite sign gives\n\n$$\nm_g = -\\dfrac{7}{6}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of line $g$ is\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{7}{6}\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221046 1069 "e-20489" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221046 1071 "Line $p$ is defined by $y = -\\dfrac{7}{12}x + 5$. Line $q$ is perpendicular to line $p$ in the $xy$-plane. What is the slope of line $q$?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221046 1072 #uuid "04f675cd-2731-54ed-8370-5c2be8a55efe" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221046 1074 "The slope of line $p$ is\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{7}{12}\n$$\n\nSince the slopes of perpendicular lines are negative reciprocals of each other, we start by taking the reciprocal of\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{7}{12}\n$$\n\nwhich is\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{12}{7}\n$$\n\nand then change the sign. This gives us the slope of line $q{:}$\n\n$$\nm_q = \\dfrac{12}{7}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of line $q$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{12}{7}\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221047 1069 "e-20750" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221047 1071 "![](images/79/79d056ab1a6587e2ce9ab61e7567832d1bbd1608b6529da875e98c2848e1cb3a.png)\n\nTo fund a class trip, Leo sells boxes of pencils and pens. He earns $\\[math]7$ for each box of pencils sold and $\\[/math]14$ for each box of pens sold. The graph above shows all possible combinations of boxes Leo could sell to earn $t$ dollars. What is the value of $t$?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221047 1072 #uuid "aceaffd8-7ee6-50c0-b76f-7ac8b84c3390" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221047 1074 "To find Leo’s total earnings $t$, we use the earnings per box and apply the coordinates from the graph to evaluate $t$ using the linear equation that models the situation.\n\nLet $x$ be the number of boxes of pencils and $y$ be the number of boxes of pens.\n\nThus, the equation that translates this situation is\n\n$$\n7x + 14y = t\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we see:\n\n- The line crosses the $y$-axis at $(0,10)$, this means if he sold $10$ boxes of pens and $0$ of pencils, he earned\n$t|= 7x + 14y; = 7(0) + 14(10); = 140$.\n- The line crosses the $x$-axis at $(20,0)$, this means if he sold $20$ boxes of pencils and $0$ of pens, he earned\n$t|= 7x + 14y; = 7(20) + 14(0); = 140$.\n\nTherefore, in either case, Leo would have earned a total of\n\n$$\nt = \\[math]140\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221048 1069 "e-20751" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221048 1071 "The functions $u$ and $v$ are defined as\n$u(x) = \\dfrac{4}{5}x - 8 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad v(x) = \\dfrac{2}{5}x + 6$.\nIf the function $w$ is defined as\n$w(x) = u(x) + v(x)$,\nwhat is the $x$-coordinate of the $x$-intercept of the graph of $y = w(x)$ in the $xy$-plane?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221048 1072 #uuid "397c3c58-b56f-5655-8444-a285f0e17e96" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221048 1074 "First, we find $w(x)$ by adding $u(x)$ and $v(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nw(x) &= u(x) + v(x) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{4}{5}x - 8) + (\\frac{2}{5}x + 6) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{4}{5}x + \\frac{2}{5}x) + (-8 + 6) \\\\\n&= \\frac{6}{5}x - 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the $x$-intercept of $w(x)$, we set $w(x) = 0$ and solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nw(x) &= \\frac{6}{5}x - 2 \\\\\n0 &= \\frac{6}{5}x - 2 \\\\\n\\frac{6}{5}x &= 2 \\\\\nx &= \\frac{5}{6} \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= \\frac{10}{6} \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-coordinate of the $x$-intercept of $w(x)$ is\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\dfrac{5}{3}}\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221049 1069 "e-20752" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221049 1071 "![](images/0c/0cb80d583a23ede4807a4f1066a4396f3f02b7d6db2fba6baa7f51c460b5d54e.png)\n\nThe graph of the linear function $y = h(x) - 6$ is shown. If $c$ and $d$ are positive constants, which equation could define $h$?\n\n1. $h(x) = -d - c x$\n2. $h(x) = -d + c x$\n3. $h(x) = d - c x$\n4. $h(x) = d + c x$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221049 1072 #uuid "78fa33ba-fa0a-589a-ad3d-25d2fe478311" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221049 1074 "To determine which equation could define $h(x)$, we analyze the graph of the function\n\n$$\ny = h(x) - 6\n$$\n\nand deduce the slope and $y$-intercept of $h(x)$ based on the vertical shift.\n\nFrom the graph, we observe the following:\n\n- The slope of the line is negative, so the slope of $h(x)$ must also be negative.\n- The $y$-intercept of $h(x) - 6$ is positive, which means the $y$-intercept of $h(x)$ must be even more positive, since the graph of $h(x) - 6$ lies $6$ units below the graph of $h(x)$.\n\nGiven that $h(x)$ has a negative slope and a positive $y$-intercept, and that $c$ and $d$ are positive constants, the only equation among the options that satisfies these conditions is:\n\n$$\nh(x) = d - c x\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221050 1069 "e-20755" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221050 1071 "The line with the equation $\\dfrac{3}{4}x + \\dfrac{5}{6}y = 2$ is graphed in the $xy$-plane. What is the $x$-coordinate of the $x$-intercept of the line?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221050 1072 #uuid "c649ee7c-0cd0-5e57-a312-0337fb5b827a" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221050 1074 "Since the $x$-intercept occurs when $y = 0$, we set $y = 0$ and solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3}{4}x + \\frac{5}{6}y &= 2 \\\\\n\\frac{3}{4}x + \\frac{5}{6}(0) &= 2 \\\\\n\\frac{3}{4}x &= 2 \\\\\nx &= \\frac{4}{3} \\cdot 2 \\\\\nx &= \\frac{8}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-coordinate of the $x$-intercept is\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\dfrac{8}{3}}\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221051 1069 "e-20756" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221051 1071 "In the $xy$-plane, line $p$ has a slope of $\\dfrac{3}{2}$ and an $x$-intercept of $(4, 0)$. What is the $y$-coordinate of the $y$-intercept of line $p$?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221051 1072 #uuid "fc6854c4-e165-5681-b807-13593b9e72f7" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221051 1074 "First, we find the equation of the line using point-slope form.\n\nWe know the line passes through\n\n$$\n(x_1, y_1) = (4, 0)\n$$\n\nand has slope\n\n$$\nm = \\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nso the equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - y_{1} &= m(x - x_{1}) \\\\\ny - 0 &= \\frac{3}{2}(x - 4) \\\\\ny &= \\frac{3}{2}x - \\frac{3}{2} \\cdot 4 \\\\\ny &= \\frac{3}{2}x - 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the $y$-intercept, we plug in $x=0$ and solve for $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= \\frac{3}{2}x - 6 \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2}(0) - 6 \\\\\n&=-6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-coordinate of the $y$-intercept is $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{-6}$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221052 1069 "e-20757" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221052 1071 "In the $xy$-plane, line $r$ intersects the $y$-axis at the point $(0, -3)$ and passes through the point $(6, 3)$. If the point $(12, v)$ lies on line $r$, what is the value of $v$?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221052 1072 #uuid "783f5275-154c-56ba-8720-bfd2b97963f6" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221052 1074 "We want to find the equation of the line that passes through the points\n\n$$\n(x_1, y_1) = (0, -3)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n(x_2, y_2) = (6, 3)\n$$\n\nin the form\n\n$$\ny = mx + c\n$$\n\nwhere $m$ is the slope and $c$ is the $y$-intercept.\n\nFirst, we use the two given points to find the slope $m{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\frac{y_{2} - y_{1}}{x_{2} - x_{1}} \\\\\n&= (3 - (-3))/(6 - 0) \\\\\n&= \\frac{6}{6} \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the line passes through the point $(0, -3)$, it crosses the $y$-axis at $-3$, so the $y$-intercept is\n$c = -3$.\n\nSo, the equation of this line is\n$y = x - 3$.\n\nTherefore, to find the value of $v$ for the point $(12, v)$ that lies on the line, we substitute $x = 12$ and solve for $y=v{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= x - 3 \\\\\nv &= 12 - 3 \\\\\n&= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221053 1069 "e-20758" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221053 1071 "The graph of the equation $lx + my = 30$ is a line in the $xy$-plane, where $l$ and $m$ are constants. If the line contains the points $(1, 6)$ and $(5, 8)$, what is the value of $m$?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221053 1072 #uuid "1c6a8fc5-1e2b-5544-a124-4ee36ddd642d" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221053 1074 "We are given that the points $(1, 6)$ and $(5, 8)$ lie on the line defined by the equation\n\n$$\nlx + my = 30\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $m$, we substitute each point into the equation to create a system of two equations and then eliminate $l$ to solve for $m$.\n\nFirst, we plug in the points $(1,6)$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlx + my &= 30 \\\\\nl(1) + m(6) &= 30 \\\\\nl + 6m &= 30\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we plug in the points $(5,8)$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlx + my &= 30 \\\\\nl(5) + m(8) &= 30 \\\\\n5l + 8m &= 30\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{l + 6m = 30; 5l + 8m = 30\n$$\n\nTherefore, multiplying the first equation by $5$ and subtracting the second, we find that the value of $m$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5(l + 6m) - (5l + 8m) &= 5 \\cdot 30 - 30 \\\\\n5l + 30m - 5l - 8m &= 150 - 30 \\\\\n22m &= 120 \\\\\nm &= \\frac{120}{22} \\\\\nm &= \\frac{60}{11}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221054 1069 "e-20759" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221054 1071 "![](images/5d/5d552ff20b0a86a8df0cc6c24b56b7ffa7d350c11462c14c4a0320ec8cc67d94.png)\n\nThe graph shows a linear relationship between $x$ and $y$. Find the equation of the line in standard form, ensuring the coefficient of $x$ is a positive constant $R$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221054 1072 #uuid "9f1ea9a5-058f-5842-b07b-db45675adcb2" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221054 1074 "First, we want to determine the equation of the line in the form\n\n$$\ny = mx + b\n$$\n\nwhere $m$ is the slope and $b$ is the $y$-intercept.\n\nLooking at the graph, we see that the line crosses the $y$-axis at $(0,4)$, so the $y$-intercept is\n$b = 4$.\n\nNext, we determine the slope. Looking at the graph, we see that the line passes through the points\n\n$$\n(x_1, y_1) = (0,4)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n(x_2, y_2) = (1,2)\n$$\n\nThus, the slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\frac{y_{2} - y_{1}}{x_{2} - x_{1}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2 - 4}{1 - 0} \\\\\n&= \\frac{-2}{1} \\\\\n&=-2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the line equation is\n\n$$\ny = -2x + 4\n$$\n\nWe can rewrite this in standard form as follows:\n\n$$\n2x + y = 4\n$$\n\nTherefore, an equivalent equation that represents this relationship with positive $R$ is\n\n$$\nR x + y = 4\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221055 1069 "e-20760" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221055 1071 "![](images/c9/c9652d0de930cb99c1e94f3fc25835ca36457c17010c0fc33787ab71f1913077.png)\n\nThe point $(d, 4)$ lies on the line shown. What is the value of $d$?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221055 1072 #uuid "72a6ddc1-fef9-5f17-84d8-b2c52ec04c52" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221055 1074 "We want to find the value of $d$ such that the point $(d,4)$ lies on the line.\n\nFirst, we determine the equation of the line in the form\n\n$$\ny = mx + b\n$$\n\nwhere $m$ is the slope and $b$ is the $y$-intercept.\n\nLooking at the graph, we see that the line crosses the $y$-axis at\n\n$$\n(x_1, y_1) = (0,10)\n$$\n\nSo the $y$-intercept is\n$b = 10$.\n\nThe line also crosses the $x$-axis at\n\n$$\n(x_2, y_2) = (12,0)\n$$\n\nThus, the slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\frac{y_{2} - y_{1}}{x_{2} - x_{1}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{0 - 10}{12 - 0} \\\\\n&= \\frac{-10}{12} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{5}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the line is\n\n$$\ny = -\\dfrac{5}{6}x + 10\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the point $(d, 4)$ lies on the line, we plug in $y = 4$ and solve for $x = d{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4 &= -\\frac{5}{6}d + 10 \\\\\n4 - 10 &= -\\frac{5}{6}d \\\\\n- 6 &= -\\frac{5}{6}d \\\\\n\\frac{5}{6}d &= 6 \\\\\nd &= 6 \\cdot \\frac{6}{5} \\\\\n&= \\frac{36}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221056 1069 "e-20761" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221056 1071 "The graph of $7x + 3y = 5$ is translated down $5$ units in the $xy$-plane. What is the $x$-coordinate of the $x$-intercept of the resulting graph?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221056 1072 #uuid "3fd338db-89b9-5618-a889-849508703497" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221056 1074 "Translating the graph down by $5$ units means replacing $y$ with $y_n + 5$, where $y_n$ is the new $y$-coordinate:\n$y_n = y - 5$\n\nSolving for $y{:}$\n\n$$\ny = y_n + 5\n$$\n\nThus, to rewrite the given equation in terms of $y_n$, we replace $y$ by $y_n + 5$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n7x + 3y &= 5 \\\\\n7x + 3(y_{n} + 5) &= 5 \\\\\n7x + 3y_{n} + 15 &= 5 \\\\\n7x + 3y_{n} &= -10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the $x$-intercept occurs when $y_n = 0$, we set $y_n = 0$ and solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n7x + 3y_{n} &= -10 \\\\\n7x + 3(0) &= -10 \\\\\n7x &= -10 \\\\\nx &= -\\frac{10}{7}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-coordinate of the $x$-intercept is\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{10}{7}\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221057 1069 "e-20762" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221057 1071 "![](images/33/33b1b856856015c5141e525089f806e9bf602d9dba227fda85eac2c033f19c60.png)\n\nThe equation $rx + sy = -s$ is given, where $r$ and $s$ are constants such that $0 < r < s$. Which of the graphs above represents the equation in the $xy$-plane?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221057 1072 #uuid "76a5266e-e1a7-52c8-bced-4ead6cbcbde8" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221057 1074 "We want to determine which graph corresponds to the equation\n\n$$\nrx + sy = -s\n$$\n\nunder the condition $0 < r < s$.\n\nFirst, we rewrite this in slope-intercept form,\n\n$$\ny = mx + b\n$$\n\nby solving for $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nrx + sy &= -s \\\\\nsy &= -rx - s \\\\\ny &= -\\frac{r}{s}x - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we find the slope and $y$-intercept are\n\n$$\nm = -\\dfrac{r}{s} \\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad b = -1\n$$\n\nSo, the line crosses the $y$-axis at\n$(0,-1)$.\n\nNow, since $0 < r < s$, we have\n\n$$\n0 < \\dfrac{r}{s} < 1\n$$\n\nMultiplying by $-1$, we get\n\n$$\n0 > -\\dfrac{r}{s} > -1 \\qquad \\text{or} \\qquad -1 < -\\dfrac{r}{s} < 0\n$$\n\nSo the slope\n\n$$\nm = -\\dfrac{r}{s}\n$$\n\nis negative with an absolute value less than $1$, meaning the line slopes downward but is relatively flat.\n\nTherefore, the only graph that crosses the $y$-axis at $(0,-1)$, slopes downward, and is relatively flat is\n\n![](images/09/0942c501522fb759a35ba331e1022f05f205183c3b7dc7d120f99dc2651edb98.png)" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221058 1069 "e-20766" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221058 1071 "The function $v$ is defined by $v(x) = -x - 5$. The graph of $y = v(x)$ in the $xy$-plane has an $x$-intercept at $(g, 0)$ and a $y$-intercept at $(0, h)$, where $g$ and $h$ are constants. What is the value of $g + h$?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221058 1072 #uuid "065bf348-2b50-5cd9-a3a6-a700ad51d9b8" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221058 1074 "We are asked to find the sum of the $x$-intercept $g$ and the $y$-intercept $h$ of the function\n\n$$\nv(x) = -x - 5\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute the point $(g, 0)$ into the equation to solve for $g{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(x) &= -x - 5 \\\\\n0 &= - (g) - 5 \\\\\ng &= -5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the point $(0, h)$ into the equation to solve for $h{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(x) &= -x - 5 \\\\\nh &= -0 - 5 \\\\\nh &= -5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng + h &= -5 + (-5) \\\\\n&=-10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221059 1069 "e-20769" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221059 1071 "![](images/ff/ff93bd7add42723842f7efe4e45ab12f452d1b581b3d937a6edeb497b32b4663.png)\n\nThe graph of $y = g(x) + 5$ is shown above. Find the equation that defines the linear function $g$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221059 1072 #uuid "745fec95-19a0-5fb8-839b-b81270c9962b" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221059 1074 "We are given the graph of the function\n\n$$\ny = g(x) + 5\n$$\n\nand asked to find the function $g(x)$.\n\nWe first want to determine the equation of the shown line in the form\n\n$$\ny = mx + b\n$$\n\nwhere $m$ is the slope and $b$ is the $y$-intercept.\n\nLooking at the graph, we see that it crosses the $y$-axis at\n\n$$\n(x_1, y_1) = (0,3)\n$$\n\nThus, the $y$-intercept is\n$b = 3$.\n\nThe line also crosses the $x$-axis at\n\n$$\n(x_2, y_2) = (-3,0)\n$$\n\nThus, the slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\frac{y_{2} - y_{1}}{x_{2} - x_{1}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{0 - 3}{-3 - 0} \\\\\n&= \\frac{-3}{-3} \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the equation of the line is\n\n$$\ny = x + 3\n$$\n\nSince this represents\n\n$$\ny = g(x) + 5\n$$\n\nwe set up the equation and solve for $g(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(x) + 5 &= x + 3 \\\\\ng(x) &= x + 3 - 5 \\\\\ng(x) &= x - 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation for $g$ is\n\n$$\ng(x) = x - 2\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221060 1069 "e-20773" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221060 1071 "What is the slope of the graph of $2x - 6y = 18$ in the $xy$-plane?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221060 1072 #uuid "c9d35229-a7d3-5cd2-9fb0-3e5e861f4d9f" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221060 1074 "First, we rewrite the given equation in slope-intercept form,\n\n$$\ny = mx + b\n$$\n\nwhere $m$ is the slope:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x - 6y &= 18 \\\\\n- 6y &= -2x + 18 \\\\\ny &= \\frac{1}{3}x - 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the line is\n\n$$\nm = \\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221061 1069 "e-20780" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221061 1071 "In the $xy$-plane, the graph of the linear function $q$ contains the points $(0, -4)$ and $(8, 0)$. Find the equation defining $q$, where $y = q(x)$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221061 1072 #uuid "f940c6a2-9472-5723-9277-1a1ccaf9212b" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221061 1074 "We want to find which equation correctly represents the line that passes through the points\n\n$$\n(x_1, y_1) = (0, -4)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n(x_2, y_2) = (8, 0)\n$$\n\nin the form\n\n$$\ny = mx + b\n$$\n\nwhere $m$ is the slope and $b$ is the $y$-intercept.\n\nFirst, we use the two given points to find the slope $m{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= (0 - (-4))/(8 - 0) \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{8} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the line passes through the point $(0, -4)$, it crosses the $y$-axis at $-4$, so the $y$-intercept is\n$b = -4$.\n\nSo, the equation of this line is\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{1}{2}x - 4\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $y = q(x)$, the equation that defines $q$ is\n\n$$\nq(x) = \\dfrac{1}{2}x - 4\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221062 1069 "e-20781" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221062 1071 "When line $u$ is graphed in the $xy$-plane, it has an $x$-intercept of $(3, 0)$ and a $y$-intercept of $\\left(0, -\\dfrac{9}{2}\\right)$. What is the slope of line $u$?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221062 1072 #uuid "abb6c4f9-f548-5bbf-bbe3-ee454b51fbfe" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221062 1074 "We want to find the slope of the line that passes through the two given intercepts.\n\nThus, we use the two given points,\n\n$$\n(x_1, y_1) = (3, 0)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n(x_2, y_2) = \\left(0, -\\dfrac{9}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nto find the slope $m{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\frac{y_{2} - y_{1}}{x_{2} - x_{1}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{-9/2 - 0}{0 - 3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{-9/2}{-3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{9}{2} \\div 3 \\\\\n&= \\frac{9}{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the line is\n\n$$\nm = \\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221063 1069 "e-20783" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221063 1071 "The relationship between two variables, $x$ and $y$, is linear. For every increase in the value of $x$ by $1$, the value of $y$ decreases by $2$. When the value of $x$ is $5$, the value of $y$ is $-1$. Find the equation representing this relationship." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221063 1072 #uuid "845d31c4-7200-5c06-b181-e7344b5f6766" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221063 1074 "We want to find the equation of the line in the form\n\n$$\ny = mx + b\n$$\n\nwhere $m$ is the slope and $b$ is the $y$-intercept.\n\nFirst, since the relationship is linear and $y$ decreases by $2$ for every increase of $1$ in $x$, the slope of the line is\n$m = -2$.\n\nNow, we substitute the point $(5, -1)$ into the equation to solve for $b{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= mx + b \\\\\n- 1 &= -2(5) + b \\\\\n- 1 &= -10 + b \\\\\nb &= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation representing this relationship is\n\n$$\ny = -2x + 9\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221064 1069 "e-20785" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221064 1071 "For the linear function $t$, the graph of $y = t(x)$ in the $xy$-plane has a slope of $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ and passes through the point $(6, -4)$. Find the equation defining $t$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221064 1072 #uuid "a8180461-66ab-5dea-a1c3-0319bb34489f" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221064 1074 "We want to find the equation of the line in the form\n\n$$\ny = mx + b\n$$\n\nwhere $m$ is the slope and $b$ is the $y$-intercept.\n\nWe are told the line has a slope\n\n$$\nm = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the point $(6, -4)$ into the equation to solve for $b{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= mx + b \\\\\n- 4 &= \\frac{1}{2}(6) + b \\\\\n- 4 &= 3 + b \\\\\nb &= -7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of this line is\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{1}{2}x - 7\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $y = t(x)$, the equation that defines $t$ is\n\n$$\nt(x) = \\dfrac{1}{2}x - 7\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221065 1069 "e-20786" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221065 1071 "If the graph of $9x + 18y = 72$ is shifted down $6$ units in the $xy$-plane, what is the $y$-intercept of the resulting graph?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221065 1072 #uuid "1f068ec4-e0a3-56aa-8bc7-75ac3a683060" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221065 1074 "We begin by finding the $y$-intercept of the original graph. To do this, we set $x = 0$ and solve for $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n9(0) + 18y &= 72 \\\\\n18y &= 72 \\\\\ny &= \\frac{72}{18} \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, shifting the graph down by $6$ units decreases all $y$-coordinates by $6$. Therefore, the new $y$-intercept becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - 6 &= 4 - 6 \\\\\n&=-2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercept of the shifted graph is\n$(0, -2)$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221066 1069 "e-20787" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221066 1071 "![](images/f6/f60c95a8e0bb48a093fafa3754351a715572fcea7c1dd4740c61ecc0ca8f4621.png)\n\nThe graph shows the possible combinations of the number of notebooks and pens that could be purchased for $\\[math]21$ at a school supply store. If Mia purchased pens and $6$ notebooks for a total of $\\[/math]21$, how many pens did she buy?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221066 1072 #uuid "070f24b1-f7fc-5181-82fd-04907e6e6b71" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221066 1074 "Since the line graph above represents all $\\[math]21$ combinations of notebooks and pens, each point on the line shows a possible purchase:\n\n- the $x$-coordinate gives the number of notebooks, and\n- the $y$-coordinate gives the number of pens.\n\nWe need to find how many pens Mia bought when she purchased $6$ notebooks. Looking at the graph, for $x=6$, we have $y=12$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/80/80b77e84527bdc3dbf0589466f4ada57b5e415dcf6257bfaef613c738138ef39.png)\n\nTherefore, Mia bought $12$ pens." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221067 1069 "e-20788" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221067 1071 "Which of the following is the graph of the equation $y = -\\dfrac{4}{3}x + 5$ in the $xy$-plane?\n\n![](images/ed/ed9487fd00024672b65151c250ba152475bde73e4d0a995c34e22a3e54ec8068.png)" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221067 1072 #uuid "0c3f3c55-7da9-5a41-b601-4a7f4059dc8e" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221067 1074 "The given equation is already in the slope-intercept form\n\n$$\ny = mx + b\n$$\n\nwhere $m$ is the slope and $b$ is the $y$-intercept.\n\nFrom the equation\n\n$$\ny = -\\dfrac{4}{3}x + 5\n$$\n\n, we can identify:\n\n$$\nm = -\\dfrac{4}{3} \\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad b = 5\n$$\n\nWhen looking at the options, we expect:\n\n- The line intersects the $y$-axis at $(0, 5)$.\n- The line slopes downward from left to right, decreasing by $4$ units in $y$ for every $3$ unit increase in $x$.\n\nTherefore, the graph that represents the equation\n\n$$\ny = -\\dfrac{4}{3}x + 5\n$$\n\nis:\n\n![](images/a9/a9a1a147439ff1745647f59182afc42479a5aedba3032b8510774d389317e480.png)" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221068 1069 "e-20793" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221068 1071 "![](images/0f/0f6bccd78fecc9da35ea778f988fc7bdf41057eeee9e22a152bec35f466b497a.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the line shown in the $xy$-plane above?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221068 1072 #uuid "39093e08-3708-5f5f-9dd4-8eed1ebfe14f" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221068 1074 "We want to determine the equation of the line in the form\n\n$$\ny = mx + b\n$$\n\nwhere $m$ is the slope and $b$ is the $y$-intercept.\n\nLooking at the graph, we see that the line crosses the $y$-axis at $-3$. Thus, the $y$-intercept is\n$b = -3$.\n\nNext, we determine the slope. Looking at the graph, we see that the line passes through the points\n\n$$\n(x_1, y_1) = (0, -3)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n(x_2, y_2) = (2, -4)\n$$\n\nThus, the slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\frac{y_{2} - y_{1}}{x_{2} - x_{1}} \\\\\n&= (-4 - (-3))/(2 - 0) \\\\\n&= \\frac{-1}{2} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is\n\n$$\ny = -\\dfrac{1}{2}x - 3\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221069 1069 "e-20818" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221069 1071 "![](images/41/41caff15d8fb7c2710194f7d6bb5b913e83aa1f41d019427f95d2c6a6a16dabe.png)\n\nThe line above represents the solutions to a system of two linear equations. If a new system of three equations is created using the system of equations represented by the graph above and the equation $3x+3y=3$, how many solutions will the resulting system of three equations have?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221069 1072 #uuid "56e9d1f3-07c6-5160-8aca-052acbc07cb6" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221069 1074 "First, let's determine the equation of the given line.\n\nFirst, we calculate the slope. Notice that the line passes through the points $(0,1)$ and $(1,0)$. Therefore, its slope is\n\n$$\nm = \\dfrac{0-1}{1-0} = -1\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of our line is\n\n$$\ny = -x + c\n$$\n\nand since the line passes through $(0,1)$, the $y$-intercept is $c=1$. This gives the line\n\n$$\ny = -x + 1\n$$\n\nRecall that a system of linear equations has infinitely many solutions when both equations represent the *same line.*\n\nWe're given the line\n\n$$\n3x + 3y = 3\n$$\n\nLet's compare this against the first line by writing it in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x + 3y &= 3 \\\\\n3y &= -3x + 3 \\\\\n\\frac{3y}{3} &= \\frac{-3x + 3}{3} \\\\\ny &= -x + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the line shown in the diagram and the line represented by\n\n$$\n3x+3y = 3\n$$\n\nare the same.\n\nThis means that the system of equations has infinitely many solutions." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221070 1069 "e-20825" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221070 1071 "Consider the system of equations below, where $q$ is a constant.\n${15x + 10y =-25; y - 8qy = \\frac{7}{5} + 6x$\nGiven that the system has no solution, what is the value of $q$?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221070 1072 #uuid "1b961fef-2207-581d-aeb8-711b260c0a42" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221070 1074 "A system of linear equations has no solution if the equations represent parallel lines that never intersect. This occurs when both equations have the same slope but different $y$-intercepts.\n\nLet’s start by rewriting the equations in slope-intercept form,\n\n$$\ny = mx + b\n$$\n\nwhere $m$ is the slope and $b$ is the $y$-intercept.\n\n- First equation:\n$15x + 10y|=-25; 10y|=-15x - 25; y|= \\frac{-15}{10}x + \\frac{-25}{10}; y|=-\\frac{3}{2}x - \\frac{5}{2}$\n- Second equation:\n$y - 8qy|= \\frac{7}{5} + 6x; (1 - 8q)y|= 6x + \\frac{7}{5}; y|= (6)/((1 - 8q))x + (7)/(5(1 - 8q))$\n\nTherefore, for this equation to have the same slope as the first equation, we match the slopes and solve for $q{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(6)/((1 - 8q)) &= -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\\n6 \\cdot 2 &= -3(1 - 8q) \\\\\n12 &= -3 + 24q \\\\\n24q &= 15 \\\\\nq &= \\frac{5}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the system has no solution for\n\n$$\nq = \\dfrac{5}{8}\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221071 1069 "e-20826" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221071 1071 "Consider the system of equations below, where $a$ is a constant.\n${7 - 9m = aw; - 9m + 6w =-\\frac{3}{2}$\nIf the system has no solution, what is the value of $a$?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221071 1072 #uuid "caec3c10-eff6-5843-9f58-c40e54ef732d" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221071 1074 "A system of linear equations has no solution if the equations represent parallel lines that never intersect. This occurs when both equations have the same slope but different $y$-intercepts.\n\nLet’s start by rewriting the equations in slope-intercept form,\n\n$$\nm = kw + b\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the slope and $b$ is the $y$-intercept:\n\n- First equation:\n$7 - 9m|= aw; - 9m|= aw - 7; m|=-\\frac{a}{9}w + \\frac{7}{9}$\n- Second equation:\n$-9m + 6w|=-\\frac{3}{2}; - 9m|=-6w - \\frac{3}{2}; m|= \\frac{2}{3}w + \\frac{1}{6}$\n\nSince the equations have different $y$-intercepts\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{7}{9}\\right\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{1}{6}\\right)\n$$\n\nthe slopes must be equal for the system to have no solution.\n\nTherefore, we match the slopes and solve for $a{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-\\frac{a}{9} &= \\frac{2}{3} \\\\\n- a &= 6 \\\\\na &= -6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221072 1069 "e-20828" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221072 1071 "${y = \\frac{1}{4}x - 5; y = dx + 9$\n\nIn the system of equations above, $d$ is a constant. If the system has no solution, what is the value of $d$?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221072 1072 #uuid "1326c415-c5e0-53aa-8b8d-df73d4085068" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221072 1074 "A system of linear equations has no solution if the equations represent parallel lines that never intersect. This occurs when both equations have the same slope but different $y$-intercepts.\n\nBoth equations are in slope-intercept form,\n\n$$\ny = mx + b\n$$\n\nwhere $m$ is the slope and $b$ is the $y$-intercept.\n\n- The first equation $y = \\dfrac{1}{4}x - 5$ has slope $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ and $y$-intercept $-5$.\n- The second equation $y = dx + 9$ has slope $d$ and $y$-intercept $9$.\n\nSince the equations have different $y$-intercepts ($-5$ and $9$), the slopes must be equal for the system to have no solution.\n\nEquating the slopes, we get\n\n$$\nd = \\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221073 1069 "e-20831" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221073 1071 "One of the two equations in a linear system is $-8x - 4y = 12$. The system has no solution. Which of the following could be the other equation in the system?\n\n1. $2x + y = 5$\n2. $8x + 4y = -12$\n3. $-2x + y = 7$\n4. $x - 2y = 1$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221073 1072 #uuid "071c6562-beb9-5d82-bf4d-f26e6014a180" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221073 1074 "A system of linear equations has no solution if the equations represent parallel lines that never intersect. This occurs when both equations have the same slope but different $y$-intercepts.\n\nLet’s start by rewriting the given equation in slope-intercept form,\n\n$$\ny = mx + b{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-8x - 4y &= 12 \\\\\n- 4y &= 8x + 12 \\\\\ny &= -2x - 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the given equation has slope $-2$ and $y$-intercept $-3$.\n\nLet's now look for a second equation among the choices that has the same slope but a different $y$-intercept. We consider the choices in turn.\n\n- Consider the equation $8x + 4y = -12$ and rewrite it in slope-intercept form:\n$y = -2x - 3$\nThis equation has the same slope and the same $y$-intercept as the given equation. It is not valid.\n- Consider the equation $-2x + y = 7$ and rewrite it in slope-intercept form:\n$y = 2x + 7$\nThis equation has a different slope, $m = 2 \\ne -2$. Therefore, it is not valid.\n- Consider the equation $x - 2y = 1$ and rewrite it in slope-intercept form:\n$y = \\dfrac{1}{2}x - \\dfrac{1}{2}$\nThis equation has a different slope, $m = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\ne -2$. Therefore, it is not valid.\n- Consider the equation $2x + y = 5$ and rewrite it in slope-intercept form:\n$y = -2x + 5$.\nThis equation has the same slope, $m = -2$, and a different $y$-intercept, $b=5\\neq - 3$. Therefore, it is valid.\n\nConsequently, the second equation in the system could be\n\n$$\n2x + y = 5\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is **only I.**" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221074 1069 "e-20833" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221074 1071 "${-5x - 15y = 25; - x - 3y = 5$\n\nHow many solutions does the given system of equations have?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221074 1072 #uuid "0c5ba932-63b7-5e12-b23a-69a30d2a64fc" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221074 1074 "To determine how many solutions a system of linear equations has, we check whether the two equations represent:\n\n- The same line, which implies there are infinitely many solutions;\n- Parallel lines that never intersect, which implies there is no solution;\n- Two distinct lines that intersect once, which implies there is exactly one solution.\n\nLet’s analyze the system:\n\n$$\n{-5x - 15y = 25; - x - 3y = 5\n$$\n\nNote that if we multiply both sides of the second equation by $5$, we get exactly the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5(-x - 3y) &= 5(5) \\\\\n- 5x - 15y &= 25\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the two equations represent the same line, and any point that lies on one will also lie on the other.\n\nTherefore, the system has infinitely many solutions." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221075 1069 "e-20843" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221075 1071 "A line intersects two parallel lines, forming four acute angles and four obtuse angles. The measure of one of the obtuse angles is $(11m + 94)^\\circ$. The sum of one obtuse angle and three of the acute angles is $(k - 22m)^\\circ$. What is the value of $k$?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221075 1072 #uuid "e9179227-4bfb-5de7-b5ec-b3a3fb69df13" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221075 1074 "When a transversal intersects two parallel lines, it creates eight angles in total: four acute angles and four obtuse angles. All acute angles are congruent, and all obtuse angles are congruent, as shown below.\n\n![](images/aa/aa6d49cdf854be608c558173a22f2b2d41e28e9abc2c59b80a2837d03073fd5b.png)\n\nLet $\\angle A$ be an acute angle and $\\angle O$ be an obtuse angle.\n\nIn our case, the measure of one obtuse angle is\n\n$$\nm\\angle O = (11m + 94)^\\circ\n$$\n\nSince acute and obtuse angles formed in this configuration are supplementary, their measures sum to $180^\\circ{:}$\n\n$$\nm\\angle A + m\\angle O = 180^\\circ\n$$\n\nHence, the measure of each acute angle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠A &= 180^{∘} - m∠O \\\\\n&= 180^{∘} - (11m + 94)^{∘} \\\\\n&= 180^{∘} - 11m^{∘} - 94^{∘} \\\\\n&= (86 - 11m)^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that the sum of one obtuse angle and three acute angles is $(k - 22m)^\\circ$. Writing as an equation, we have\n\n$$\nm\\angle O + 3 \\cdot m\\angle A = (k - 22m)^\\circ\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our expressions for $m\\angle O$ and $m\\angle A$, we solve for $k$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(11m + 94)^{∘} + 3 \\cdot (86 - 11m)^{∘} &= (k - 22m)^{∘} \\\\\n11m + 94 + 258 - 33m &= k - 22m \\\\\n352 - 22m &= k - 22m \\\\\nk &= 352\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221076 1069 "e-20844" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221076 1071 "![](images/11/110fe5775bfc585b7a396975628eb1bbb2bb0cbb8c469e49355f9c89bfb6ef41.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, parallel lines $m$ and $n$ are intersected by the lines $p$ and $q$. If $x = 40$ and $y = 120$, what is the value of $z$?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221076 1072 #uuid "1c4bee4a-ef50-502d-861a-54d4cb3da91e" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221076 1074 "We begin by examining the triangle formed by lines $p$, $q$, and $m$. This triangle has three interior angles:\n\n- One angle is formed at the intersection of lines $p$ and $q$. This angle is vertical to the angle labeled $x^\\circ$, hence, they are congruent. So, the measure of this first angle is $40^\\circ$.\n- A second angle is formed at the intersection of lines $p$ and $m$. This angle lies adjacent to the angle labeled $y^\\circ$, forming a linear pair. Since linear pairs are supplementary, the measure of this second angle is\n$180^\\circ - y^\\circ = 180^\\circ - 120^\\circ = 60^\\circ$.\n- The third angle is formed at the intersection of lines $q$ and $m$. Since in any triangle the sum of the measures of its interior angles is $180^\\circ$, the measure of the third angle is\n$180^\\circ - 40^\\circ - 60^\\circ = 80^\\circ$.\n\nWe can summarize these angles by adding them to the diagram, shown below.\n\n![](images/4c/4cdbc6e543180e1ebc5220e5d0db4fef545c290bc84f59068bff820379c3530c.png)\n\nNow observe the interior angle at the point where line $q$ intersects line $n$. Since $m \\parallel n$, and the line $q$ acts as a transversal, this angle corresponds to the $80^\\circ$ angle at the intersection of $q$ and $m$. Since corresponding angles are congruent, the measure of this angle is also $80^\\circ$.\n\n![](images/64/64a6f180ea5cc365b8a0ed6e3cb15c34832da15299e648c07efa56c68ce980b6.png)\n\nAt the intersection of lines $q$ and $n$, the two angles labeled $z^\\circ$ form a straight angle on $q$ with the $80^\\circ$ angle we just found. Since a straight angle measures $180^\\circ$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz^{∘} + z^{∘} + 80^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n&= 2z^{∘} = \\mid 100^{∘} \\\\\nz^{∘} &= 50^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $z = 50$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221077 1069 "e-20846" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221077 1071 "![](images/5d/5d1614efbc39ee9e991fc27e6d4bf30edb9ff953734af86107436795fb072ec0.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, lines $u$ and $v$ are parallel, and the line $w$ intersects both. If $x = 2k + 14$ and $y = 6k - 10$, what is the value of $z$?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221077 1072 #uuid "0a52dce1-12f3-5e82-9669-98387bfa06a2" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221077 1074 "From the diagram, we see that\n\n- The angles labeled $x^\\circ$ and $y^\\circ$ are vertical angles, so they are congruent:\n$x^\\circ = y^\\circ$\n- The angles labeled $x^\\circ$ and $z^\\circ$ are consecutive interior angles, so they are supplementary:\n$x^\\circ + z^\\circ = 180^\\circ$\n\nWe are given expressions for $x$ and $y$. Since\n\n$$\nx^\\circ = y^\\circ\n$$\n\nwe can write\n\n$$\n(2k + 14)^\\circ = (6k - 10)^\\circ\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $k{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2k + 14 &= 6k - 10 \\\\\n14 + 10 &= 6k - 2k \\\\\n24 &= 4k \\\\\nk &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this value into the expression for $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= 2k + 14 \\\\\n&= 2(6) + 14 \\\\\n&= 12 + 14 \\\\\n&= 26\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x^\\circ$ and $z^\\circ$ are supplementary, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz^{∘} &= 180^{∘} - x^{∘} \\\\\n&= 180^{∘} - 26^{∘} \\\\\n&= 154^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $z = 154$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221078 1069 "e-20848" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221078 1071 "![](images/c5/c5e20e4c52036a642b647080f43e4ff8883b641a6f103a20e38e4ae5dd41c1ca.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, lines $p$ and $q$ are parallel, and the line $t$ intersects both. If $a > 105$, which of the following must be true?\n\n1. $b < 75$\n2. $a + b > 180$\n3. $b > 75$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221078 1072 #uuid "c40631c7-1f4d-5641-ba6b-43459aa7e465" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221078 1074 "Since lines $p$ and $q$ are parallel, and the line $t$ acts as a transversal, the angle labeled $a^\\circ$ and the angle adjacent to $b^\\circ$ outside the parallel lines are corresponding angles. Hence, they are congruent.\n\n![](images/ed/edbb26b99c0bafe9a3281749e2d70045c46c7e916bfba5066b9f5f57eaaadbfb.png)\n\nThe angle labeled $a^\\circ$ and the adjacent angle form a linear pair, creating a straight angle at the point of intersection of $t$ and $q$. The measure of a straight angle is $180^\\circ$, so we can write\n\n$$\nb + a = 180\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is false.\n\nNext, we are told that $a > 105$. Substituting this into the equation, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}a & > 105 \\\\ b + a & > b + 105 \\\\ 180 & > b + 105 \\\\ 75 & > b \\\\ b & < 75\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nSo, statement I is true while statement III is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221079 1069 "e-20849" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221079 1071 "![](images/0c/0ca868ba6d647578a2a1b58542b078c01ff0ac6286cf3264af561ec768bba784.png)\n\nIn the diagram, line $a$ is parallel to line $b$, and line $t$ intersects both lines. If $y = 4x + 3$, what is the value of $x$?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221079 1072 #uuid "1be0baa6-d20b-57a1-a612-841351fe5220" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221079 1074 "Since lines $a$ and $b$ are parallel, and the line $t$ acts as a transversal, the angle labeled $69^\\circ$ and the angle adjacent to $y^\\circ$ inside the parallel lines are corresponding angles. Hence, they are congruent.\n\n![](images/77/7784095a616304d3e833677641baf6716e1c17a16ea47b1fe7a3de76d40c937d.png)\n\nThe angle labeled $y^\\circ$ and the adjacent angle form a linear pair, creating a straight angle at the point of intersection of $b$ and $t$. The measure of a straight angle is $180^\\circ$, so we can write\n\n$$\ny^\\circ + 69^\\circ = 180^\\circ\n$$\n\nWe are also told that\n\n$$\ny = 4x + 3\n$$\n\nSubstituting this and solving for $x$, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(4x + 3)^{∘} + 69^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n4x + 3 + 69 &= 180 \\\\\n4x + 72 &= 180 \\\\\n4x &= 108 \\\\\nx &= 27\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221080 1069 "e-20852" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221080 1071 "![](images/53/53b01ddde61a599be6338ca4d4a66a0cf0f96d902bc58351c7a47323074627eb.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, three lines intersect at the point $R$. If $m = 60$ and $n = 70$, what is the value of $p$?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221080 1072 #uuid "0a576696-6efd-5576-bd9c-8733c0d68444" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221080 1074 "Since vertical angles are always congruent, the angle labeled $n^\\circ$ and its opposite angle have the same measure.\n\n![](images/21/21be3222072f3176448a7a85c380940fe90cc199cec3dcb6674f2bed062a1fcd.png)\n\nNow, the three angles labeled $m^\\circ$, $n^\\circ$, and $p^\\circ$ are adjacent and together form a straight line at point $R$. The measure of a straight angle is $180^\\circ$, so we can write\n\n$$\nm^\\circ + n^\\circ + p^\\circ = 180^\\circ\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known values, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n60^{∘} + 70^{∘} + p^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n130^{∘} + p^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\np^{∘} &= 50^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $p$ is $50$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221081 1069 "e-20855" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221081 1071 "![](images/2b/2ba32a5d42811942e359de14e6e2c47327b459049e0b3de5229761968e7ac6d4.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, two lines intersect at a point. If the measure of $\\angle 1$ is $125^\\circ$, what is the measure of angle $\\angle 2$?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221081 1072 #uuid "be50a283-b3b6-5358-ae13-af99e443a76b" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221081 1074 "When two lines meet at a point, they create two pairs of opposite angles. The angles in each pair are called vertical angles. Vertical angles are always congruent.\n\nIn this case, angle $\\angle 1$ and angle $\\angle 2$ are vertical angles, so they have the same measure.\n\nTherefore, the measure of $\\angle 2$ is $125^\\circ$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221082 1069 "e-20856" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221082 1071 "![](images/d2/d2efb7179f2d314b9d3dc5b4a03802c8dae2c48ec15f849ffecc31fc517cfdef.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, lines $m$ and $n$ are parallel. What is the value of $y$?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221082 1072 #uuid "c1608da8-49f3-5983-925f-8e182fd3392d" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221082 1074 "We are given that $m$ and $n$ are parallel. Since line $k$ intersects both lines $m$ and $n$, it's a transversal.\n\nThe angle labeled $129^\\circ$ and the angle labeled $x^\\circ$ are consecutive interior angles since they are on the same side of the transversal $k$ and inside the parallel lines $m$ and $n$.\n\nWhen a transversal intersects two parallel lines, the consecutive interior angles are supplementary. So, we can write\n\n$$\nx^\\circ + 129^\\circ = 180^\\circ\n$$\n\nSolving for $x$, we get\n\n$$\nx^\\circ = 180^\\circ - 129^\\circ = 51^\\circ\n$$\n\nNow, consider the triangle formed by the lines $j$, $m$, and $\\ell$, shown in blue below.\n\n![](images/49/496e172b71f4446aa24299222a2ed90e36616903e9b964fc68e84f88b76a532b.png)\n\nIn any triangle, the sum of the measures of its interior angles is $180^\\circ$. So, we have\n\n$$\nx^\\circ + 90^\\circ + y^\\circ = 180^\\circ\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known values, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n51^{∘} + 90^{∘} + y^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\ny^{∘} + 141^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\ny^{∘} &= 39^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can conclude that $y=39$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221083 1069 "e-20857" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221083 1071 "![](images/c7/c737c96ae3b98988910dd0a74f37fb5fa37da01ea861b4ffea37044eab79ad1a.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, lines $n$ and $p$ are parallel, and the line $z$ intersects both. What is the value of $z$?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221083 1072 #uuid "0ab08f7a-6943-523b-926a-724eea05d8d5" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221083 1074 "We are given that $n$ and $p$ are parallel. Since line $h$ intersects both lines $n$ and $p$, it's a transversal.\n\nThe angle labeled $72^\\circ$ and the angle labeled $z^\\circ$ are consecutive exterior angles since they are on the same side of the transversal $h$ and outside the parallel lines $n$ and $p$.\n\nWhen a transversal intersects two parallel lines, the consecutive exterior angles are supplementary. So, we can write\n\n$$\nz^\\circ + 72^\\circ = 180^\\circ\n$$\n\nSolving for $z$, we get\n\n$$\nz^\\circ = 180^\\circ - 72^\\circ = 108^\\circ\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can conclude that $z=108$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221084 1069 "e-20858" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221084 1071 "![](images/74/74920488cd117e59f8ca1c4646109ea4f0b404a08730aff54831f4eee212ac10.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, lines $m$ and $n$ are parallel, and the line $t$ intersects both. What is the value of $x$?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221084 1072 #uuid "033ae52e-79f0-576f-9893-d74d61286684" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221084 1074 "We are given that $m$ and $n$ are parallel. Since line $t$ intersects both lines $m$ and $n$, it is a transversal.\n\nThe angle labeled $60^\\circ$ and the angle labeled $x^\\circ$ are consecutive interior angles because they are on the same side of the transversal $t$ and between the parallel lines $m$ and $n$.\n\nWhen a transversal intersects two parallel lines, consecutive interior angles are supplementary. So, we can write\n\n$$\nx^\\circ + 60^\\circ = 180^\\circ\n$$\n\nSolving for $x$, we get\n\n$$\nx^\\circ = 180^\\circ - 60^\\circ = 120^\\circ\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can conclude that $x = 120$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221085 1069 "e-20991" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221085 1071 "![](images/0b/0b29b492b6dcb0178a0dc5f89a7f20c33e26d618becfb10acd5940a7a5fc44a3.png)\n\nThe graph shows the relationship between the books purchased, $x$, and the magazines purchased, $y$, that Chloe can buy. Find the equation representing this relationship." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221085 1072 #uuid "5e8d5354-a9de-55a5-8aa0-41af0e403a2b" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221085 1074 "We want to determine an equation that represents the relationship between the books purchased, $x$, and magazines purchased, $y$.\n\nThe graph is a line that can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny = mx + b\n$$\n\nwhere $m$ is the slope and $b$ is the $y$-intercept.\n\nLooking at the graph, we see that it crosses the $y$-axis at\n\n$$\n(x_1, y_1) = (0, 120)\n$$\n\nThus, the $y$-intercept is\n$b = 120$.\n\nThe line also crosses the $x$-axis at\n\n$$\n(x_2, y_2) = (120, 0)\n$$\n\nThus, the slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\frac{y_{2} - y_{1}}{x_{2} - x_{1}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{0 - 120}{120 - 0} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{120}{120} \\\\\n&=-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the equation of the line is\n\n$$\ny = -x + 120\n$$\n\nNow, to convert this to standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= -x + 120 \\\\\nx + y &= 120\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation that represents this relationship is\n\n$$\nx + y = 120\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221086 1069 "e-20992" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221086 1071 "![](images/bc/bc744cf78545f68a2ef3bda4c49be63a4d577d8985053c6606f9e4c084f8ddc9.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the amount saved $s$, in dollars, after working for $t$ minutes. What equation relates $s$ and $t$?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221086 1072 #uuid "c8758c2d-f4c1-5502-bc80-862305f01593" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221086 1074 "We want to determine the equation that relates the amount saved $s$ to the time $t$.\n\nThe graph is a line that can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ns = mt + b\n$$\n\nwhere $m$ is the slope and $b$ is the $y$-intercept.\n\nLooking at the graph, we see the line passes through the origin, $(0,0)$. Thus, the $y$-intercept is\n$b = 0$.\n\nNext, we determine the slope. Looking at the graph, we see that the line passes through the points\n\n$$\n(t_1, s_1) = (0, 0)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n(t_2, s_2) = (2, 4)\n$$\n\nThus, the slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\frac{s_{2} - s_{1}}{t_{2} - t_{1}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4 - 0}{2 - 0} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{2} \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation that relates the amount saved $s$ to the time $t$ is\n$s = 2t$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221087 1069 "e-20996" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221087 1071 "Which of the following inequalities is equivalent to the inequality below?\n$25p + 10q > 15$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221087 1072 #uuid "d47a1b0e-cb5e-5795-8eee-f21e9d3bcfbc" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221087 1074 "We simplify the inequality by dividing *all terms* by the greatest common factor of $5{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{25p + 10q}{5} & > \\frac{15}{5} \\\\ 5p + 2q & > 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the inequality $25p + 10q > 15$ is equivalent to\n$5p + 2q > 3$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221088 1069 "e-21009" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221088 1071 "![](images/4d/4da84e8814dbe8093b6ee7d27145531367bbd72c3e0324141d1f25d77f14ce71.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $s$, where $y = s(x)$, shows the total cost $y$, in dollars, for park entry and renting $x$ skateboards for a park event. What is the best interpretation of the slope of this graph in this situation?\n\n1. The total cost is $\\[math]25$ for all skateboards.\n2. Each skateboard costs $\\[/math]25$ to rent.\n3. The total cost for the trip is $\\[math]75$.\n4. Each skateboard costs $\\[/math]75$ to rent." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221088 1072 #uuid "eff00719-bbb9-5255-84ec-1894b1bf4ab1" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221088 1074 "First, we find the slope of the line. We identify the points\n\n$$\n(x_1,y_1) = (0,75)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n(x_2,y_2) = (6,225)\n$$\n\non the line. So, we use the slope formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\frac{y_{2} - y_{1}}{x_{2} - x_{1}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{225 - 75}{6 - 0} \\\\\n&= \\frac{150}{6} \\\\\n&= 25\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the $y$-intercept point $(0,75)$, we have that the equation of our line is\n\n$$\ny = 25x + 75\n$$\n\nThis equation shows that the total cost, $y$, grows linearly with the number of skateboards rented, $x$. The slope, $25$, is multiplied by the number of skateboards, so it represents the cost per skateboard.\n\nSo, the slope of the graph shows that each skateboard costs $\\[math]25$ to rent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is **only II.**" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221089 1069 "e-21010" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221089 1071 "After a drone begins descending, its height above the ground, in meters, is modeled by the function $h(t) = 120 - 15t$, where $t$ is the number of seconds since it started descending. If the graph of $y = h(t)$ is drawn in the $xy$-plane, which of the following best describes the meaning of the $x$-coordinate of the line’s $x$-intercept in this situation?\n\n1. The total vertical distance traveled by the drone\n2. The speed of the drone in meters per second as it descends\n3. The height the drone began descending from\n4. The number of seconds it takes for the drone to reach the ground" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221089 1072 #uuid "8977066f-03bd-5f39-9eb2-78a3ba35d9b8" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221089 1074 "The function\n\n$$\nh(t) = 120 - 15t\n$$\n\nshows how the height $h$ (in meters) decreases over time $t$ (in seconds).\n\nThe $x$-intercept of the graph $y = h(t)$ is the point where the graph intersects the $x$-axis. At this point, $y = 0$, and so it corresponds to the moment when the drone reaches the ground.\n\nTo find the $x$-intercept, we set $y = 0$ and solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0 &= h(t) \\\\\n0 &= 120 - 15t \\\\\n15t &= 120 \\\\\nt &= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means the drone takes $8$ seconds to reach the ground after it starts descending.\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinate of the $x$-intercept represents **the number of seconds it takes for the drone to reach the ground.**\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is **only IV.**" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221090 1069 "e-21016" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221090 1071 "$S = 100t + 50$\nThe formula above shows the relationship between the speed $S$, in kilometers per hour, and the throttle level $t$ of an electric scooter. When $t = 0$, the scooter is stationary. When $t = 1$, the scooter is at full throttle. It is recommended that $t$ be between $0.1$ and $0.7$ for safe riding. Find the inequality that describes the target speed $S$ while riding." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221090 1072 #uuid "db605587-1045-5f53-ab97-35506acf4315" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221090 1074 "We are asked to determine the speed range based on a given formula and a recommended throttle interval.\n\nThe formula is\n\n$$\nS = 100t + 50\n$$\n\nAt minimum recommended throttle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS &= 100(0.1) + 50 \\\\\n&= 10 + 50 \\\\\n&= 60\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt maximum recommended throttle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS &= 100(0.7) + 50 \\\\\n&= 70 + 50 \\\\\n&= 120\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the target speed $S$ must satisfy\n\n$$\n60 \\leq S \\leq 120\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inequality is\n\n$$\n60 \\leq S \\leq 120\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221091 1069 "e-21017" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221091 1071 "A chemical reaction begins at a temperature of $25^\\circ$C and the temperature rises by more than $0.2^\\circ$C but less than $0.4^\\circ$C per hour. Find the inequality that shows all possible temperatures $t$, in degrees Celsius, after $100$ hours." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221091 1072 #uuid "d23208fd-1995-535d-92ea-fe98d6a9520c" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221091 1074 "We are told that the reaction starts at $25^\\circ$C.\n\nSince the temperature rises by more than $0.2^\\circ$C but less than $0.4^\\circ$C per hour, the minimum and maximum possible temperatures after $100$ hours are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n25 + (0.2 \\cdot 100) &= 25 + 20 \\\\\n&= 45\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n25 + (0.4 \\cdot 100) &= 25 + 40 \\\\\n&= 65\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ndegrees Celsius, respectively.\n\nSo, the temperature $t$ after $100$ hours must satisfy\n$45 < t < 65$.\n\nTherefore, the inequality is $45 < t < 65$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221092 1069 "e-21019" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221092 1071 "A facilities team has less than $\\[math]414$ to prepare a cleaning solution. To qualify for a bulk shipping rate, it must mix more than $170$ liters in total. Base solvent costs $\\[/math]0.90$ per liter and disinfectant concentrate costs $\\[math]6.30$ per liter. Rounded to one decimal place, what is the maximum volume of disinfectant concentrate the team can include while staying within the budget and keeping the shipping rate?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221092 1072 #uuid "2040a9c6-c0ed-5680-8ae9-590f69f47e96" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221092 1074 "We start by writing down the inequalities that model the situation. Let $x$ denote the volume of base solvent and $y$ denote the volume of disinfectant concentrate the team purchases.\n\n- The total cost of the solution is $0.90x+6.30y$. Since the team wants to spend less than $\\[math]414$, we have\n$0.90x+6.30y < 414$.\n- The total volume of the solution is $x+y$. Since the team needs to mix more than $170$ liters to maintain the shipping rate, we have\n$x+y > 170$.\n\nAlso, notice that\n\n$$\nx \\geq 0\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny \\geq 0\n$$\n\nas the volumes must be non-negative.\nSo, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n{0.90x + 6.30y < 414; x + y > 170; x \\ge 0; y \\ge 0\n$$\n\nLet's now plot the corresponding regions in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/23/23a62cc94d24c625d809a3c70faa3137775b317363529a870156af836b9be5ec.png)\n\nNotice that the corner point on the top of the feasible region has the coordinates approximately $(121.667,48.333)$, rounded to three decimal places. So, rounded to one decimal place, the maximum volume of disinfectant concentrate that potentially could satisfy the given constraints is $y=48.3$.\n\nNow, if $y=48.3$, then\n\n$$\nx \\mid > 170 - y; = 170 - 48.3; = 121.7\n$$\n\nSince the inequality is strict we take $x=121.8$.\n\nSubstituting $x=121.8$ and $y=48.3$ into the first inequality, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.90x + 6.30y &= 0.90(121.8) + 6.30(48.3) \\\\\n&= 413.91 \\\\\n&< 414✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, all the constraints are indeed satisfied. Therefore, the team can include a maximum of $48.3$ liters of disinfectant concentrate." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221093 1069 "e-21021" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221093 1071 "A library moves books using only $25$-book boxes and $60$-book boxes. Each trip may include at most $20$ boxes in total, and the cart must carry at least $500$ books. What is the maximum number of $25$-book boxes the library can move per trip?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221093 1072 #uuid "7b2ce57d-5708-5089-9263-675f5099b956" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221093 1074 "Let $s$ represent the number of $25$-book boxes and $t$ represent the number of $60$-book boxes.\n\nWe are given two constraints:\n\n- The total number of boxes must be at most $20{:}$\n$s + t \\leq 20$\n- The total number of books must be at least $500{:}$\n$25s + 60t \\geq 500$\n\nWe want to *maximize* the number of $25$-book boxes, $s$, subject to these constraints.\n\nNow, we assume $t = 0$ to check the highest possible $s$ that satisfies both conditions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}25s + 60t & \\ge 500 \\\\ 25s + 60(0) & \\ge 500 \\\\ 25s & \\ge 500 \\\\ s & \\ge \\frac{500}{25} = 20\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the largest possible value is $s = 20$.\n\nWe check if this value satisfies the total number of books condition:\n\n$$\ns + t=20 + 0=20\\leq 20 \\quad{\\color{green}\\checkmark}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum number of $25$-book boxes is $20$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221094 1069 "e-21022" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221094 1071 "${(2x - 3) - 4(y + 4) = 38; (2x - 3) + 4(y + 4) = 82$\n\nThe equations form a system. The solution is $(x, y)$. What is the value of $5(2x - 3)$?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221094 1072 #uuid "3564a063-cea1-5227-a0e7-b1c4c7430d5b" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221094 1074 "First, we let $w=2x-3$ and\n\n$$\nz=y+4\n$$\n\nso that the system may be written as follows:\n\n$$\n{w - 4z = 38; w + 4z = 82\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using the elimination method.\n\nNotice that when we add the two equations, the $4z$ terms will cancel:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nw- 4z &= 38 \\\\\nw + 4z &= 82 \\\\\n(w + w) &= (38 + 82) \\\\\n2w &= 120 \\\\\nw &= 60\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $w=2x-3$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5(2x - 3) &= 5w \\\\\n&= 5(60) \\\\\n&= 300\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221095 1069 "e-21023" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221095 1071 "${-5p + 8q = 110; 8p - 5q =-50$\n\nIf the solution to the system above is $(p, q)$, what is the value of $p + q$?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221095 1072 #uuid "4781cfc7-482d-567b-8f67-0e6863831859" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221095 1074 "First, we add the two equations and group like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-5p + 8q &= 110 \\\\\n8p - 5q &= -50 \\\\\n(-5p + 8p) + (8q - 5q) &= 110 - 50 \\\\\n3p + 3q &= 60\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve the resulting equation for $p + q{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3p + 3q &= 60 \\\\\n3(p + q) &= 60 \\\\\np + q &= \\frac{60}{3} \\\\\np + q &= 20\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the value of $p+q$ is $20$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221096 1069 "e-21047" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221096 1071 "Consider the equation in the variable $y$, given by\n$4(r - 2)y = -8$\nwhere $r$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $r$?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221096 1072 #uuid "16de4dd9-550b-5077-a044-e32eac515cba" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221096 1074 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n$ax = b$\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and\n\n$$\nb\\neq 0\n$$\n\nIf $a = 0$, then the equation has no solution.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ay = b$. In this case,\n\n$$\na = 4(r - 2), \\qquad b = -8\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n4(r - 2) = 0\n$$\n\nSolving this for $r$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4(r - 2) &= 0 \\\\\n(4(r - 2))/(4) &= \\frac{0}{4} \\\\\nr - 2 &= 0 \\\\\nr &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221097 1069 "e-21048" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221097 1071 "Consider the equation in the variable $z$, given by\n$(r - 9)z + 2 = -1$\nwhere $r$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $r$?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221097 1072 #uuid "1dc82656-0ae9-55f9-8a8c-35f19215ec89" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221097 1074 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n$ax = b$\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and\n\n$$\nb\\neq 0\n$$\n\nIf $a = 0$, then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, let's write our equation in the form $az = b{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(r - 9)z + 2 &= -1 \\\\\n(r - 9)z + 2 - 2 &= -1 - 2 \\\\\n(r - 9)_(⏟)_(a)z &= -3_(⏟)_(b)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n$r - 9 = 0$.\n\nSolving this for $r$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr - 9 &= 0 \\\\\nr &= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221098 1069 "e-21049" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221098 1071 "Consider the equation in the variable $x$, given by\n$2nx + 7 = -4x + 5$\nwhere $n$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $n$?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221098 1072 #uuid "2a3e1ce5-8203-5f57-9647-7984bbd60cd4" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221098 1074 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n$ax = b$\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and\n\n$$\nb\\neq 0\n$$\n\nIf $a = 0$, then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation in the form $ax = b$.\n\nWe start by adding $4x$ to both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2nx + 7 &= -4x + 5 \\\\\n2nx + 4x + 7 &= -4x + 5 + 4x \\\\\n2nx + 4x + 7 &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we subtract $7$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2nx + 4x + 7 &= 5 \\\\\n2nx + 4x + 7 - 7 &= 5 - 7 \\\\\n2nx + 4x &= -2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor $x$ on the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n(2n + 4)x = -2\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation is now in the form $ax = b{:}$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{(2n + 4)}_{a}x = \\underbrace{-2}_{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n2n + 4= 0\n$$\n\nSolving this for $n$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2n + 4 &= 0 \\\\\n2n + 4 - 4 &= 0 - 4 \\\\\n2n &= -4 \\\\\n\\frac{2n}{2} &= \\frac{-4}{2} \\\\\nn &= -2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221099 1069 "e-21050" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221099 1071 "$4x + m(3 - 2x) = 5 - 6x$\n\nIn the equation above, $m$ is a constant. If the equation has no solution, what is the value of $m$?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221099 1072 #uuid "c1a36ddf-7810-58e9-80d7-c5030e55998a" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221099 1074 "We're given the equation\n\n$$\n4x + m(3 - 2x) = 5 - 6x\n$$\n\nand told that it has no solution. This means it must be false for all values of $x$.\n\nLet us expand the product on the left-hand side and collect the like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x + 3m - 2mx &= 5 - 6x \\\\\n4x - 2mx + 6x &= 5 - 3m \\\\\n10x - 2mx &= 5 - 3m \\\\\n(10 - 2m)x &= 5 - 3m\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has no solutions if the following two conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\n10 - 2m = 0and5 - 3m \\ne 0\n$$\n\nSolving the equality for $m$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n10 - 2m &= 0 \\\\\n2m &= 10 \\\\\nm &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n5 - 3(5) = -10 \\neq 0\n$$\n\n$m = 5$ indeed yields an equation with no solutions." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221100 1069 "e-21052" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221100 1071 "Which of the following systems of linear equations has no solution?\n\n${y =-3; y = x - 3$\n\n${y = 5x + 9; y = 5x - 4$\n\n${x = 6; y =-1$\n\n${y =-2x + 3; y = 2x - 5$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221100 1072 #uuid "ade44d40-1c6b-5464-8225-fc8fd5bd2bd8" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221100 1074 "A system of linear equations has no solution if the equations represent parallel lines that never intersect. This occurs when both equations have the same slope but different $y$-intercepts.\n\nLet’s analyze each system:\n\n- ${y =-3; y = x - 3$\nThe first equation is a horizontal line, so it has slope $0$; the second has slope $1$. The slopes are different, so the lines intersect at one point.\n\n$\\qquad$ Thus, this system has one solution.\n\n- ${y =-2x + 3; y = 2x - 5$\nThe slopes are different ($-2$ and $2$), so the lines intersect at one point.\n\n$\\qquad$ Thus, this system has one solution.\n\n- ${x = 6; y =-1$\nThis system contains a vertical line and a horizontal line. These lines intersect at exactly one point.\n\n$\\qquad$ Thus, this system has one solution.\n\n- ${y = 5x + 9; y = 5x - 4$\nBoth equations have slope $5$, but different $y$-intercepts ($9$ and $-4$). These lines are parallel and distinct, so they never intersect.\n\n$\\qquad$ Thus, this system has **no** solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is **only II.**" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221101 1069 "e-21054" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221101 1071 "![](images/f2/f2cb2efbc5525d2778537d1a84f74e232c26828e6061fbff95543b3aee8e22c1.png)\n\nThe graph above represents the possible number of museum tickets and train tickets a customer can purchase to spend exactly $\\[math]72.00$ total. The price of each museum ticket is the same, and the price of each train ticket is the same. What is the price of one train ticket?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221101 1072 #uuid "40063043-bfd3-52de-98f1-86f475be4a8c" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221101 1074 "Let $t$ be the number of train tickets and $m$ be the number of museum tickets bought for $\\[math]72$.\n\nWe see that the graph passes through the point $(9,0)$. This means that the cost of $9$ train tickets and $0$ museum tickets is $\\[/math]72$, which gives\n$9 d = 72$,\n\nSolving this for $d$, we get\n$d = 8$.\n\nTherefore, one train ticket costs $\\[math]8.00$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221102 1069 "e-21055" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221102 1071 "Given that the lines $-7x + y = 5$ and $-14x + 2y = 10$ intersect at an infinite number of points, which of the following describes the intersection points, where $t$ is a real number?\n\n1. $\\left(t,7t-5 \\right)$\n2. $\\left(t,-7t+5 \\right)$\n3. $\\left(t,7t+5 \\right)$\n4. $\\left(t,5t+7 \\right)$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221102 1072 #uuid "d29ed4f9-6813-5ec6-8869-2ddd238a60e1" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221102 1074 "We're given that the two lines intersect at an infinite number of points.\n\nWe want to find an expression that represents every point of intersection. Notice that we're required to write our solution in the form\n$\\big(t, f(t)\\big)$.\n\nTherefore, we can find our solution as follows:\n\n- First, we set $x=t$.\n- Then, we find the corresponding $y$-coordinate for each $x$-coordinate by solving one of the lines for $y$.\n- Finally, we write the final result in terms of $t$.\n\nSo, we pick the line\n\n$$\n-7x + y = 5\n$$\n\nsolve it for $y$, and then substitute $x = t$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-7x + y &= 5 \\\\\ny &= 7x + 5 \\\\\ny &= 7t + 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are\n\n$$\n\\left(t,7t+5 \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is any number.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is **only III.**" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221103 1069 "e-21056" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221103 1071 "Given that the lines $5x - 10y = 15$ and $x - 2y = 3$ intersect at an infinite number of points, which of the following describes the intersection points, where $t$ is a real number?\n\n1. $\\left(3t+3,t\\right)$\n2. $\\left(2t-3,t\\right)$\n3. $\\left(2t+2,t\\right)$\n4. $\\left(2t+3,t\\right)$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221103 1072 #uuid "d5949fcd-fb83-571d-866c-97795067afc3" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221103 1074 "We're given that the two lines intersect at an infinite number of points.\n\nWe want to find an expression that represents every point of intersection. Notice that we're required to write our solution in the form\n$\\big(f(t), t\\big)$.\n\nTherefore, we can find our solution as follows:\n\n- First, we set $y=t$.\n- Then, we find the corresponding $x$-coordinate for each $y$-coordinate by solving one of the lines for $x$.\n- Finally, we write the final result in terms of $t$.\n\nSo, we pick the line $x - 2y = 3$, solve it for $x$, and then substitute $y = t$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - 2y &= 3 \\\\\nx &= 2y + 3 \\\\\nx &= 2t + 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are\n\n$$\n\\left(2t+3,t\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is any number.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is **only IV.**" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221104 1069 "e-21057" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221104 1071 "Given that the lines $x - y = 4$ and $2x - 2y = 8$ intersect at an infinite number of points, fill in the blanks to describe these points in terms of the real parameter $t$ such that $y=6t$.\n\nThe intersection points are $\\Big(\\, \\bbox[3pt,border:solid black 1pt]{\\phantom{XXXX}},\\,6t\\Big)$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221104 1072 #uuid "95431c73-af09-5902-ad9b-069b0ee123a1" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221104 1074 "We're given that the two lines intersect at an infinite number of points.\n\nWe want to find an expression that represents every point of intersection. Notice that we're required to write our solution in the form\n$\\big(f(t), 6t\\big)$.\n\nTherefore, we can find our solution as follows:\n\n- First, we set $y=6t$.\n- Then, we find the corresponding $x$-coordinate for each $y$-coordinate by solving one of the lines for $x$.\n- Finally, we write the final result in terms of $t$.\n\nSo, we pick the line $x - y = 4$, solve it for $x$, and then substitute $y = 6t$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - y &= 4 \\\\\nx &= y + 4 \\\\\nx &= 6t + 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the intersection points are\n\n$$\n\\left(\\,\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{6t+4}, 6t\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is any number." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221105 1069 "e-21058" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221105 1071 "${6x - 3y = 9; 12x - 6y = 18$\nConsider the system of equations above. Given that this system has infinitely many solutions $(x,y)$, which of the following are valid parametrizations of the solutions?\n\n1. $(t,\\, 2t-3)$\n2. $(3t-9,\\, t)$\n3. $(4t,\\, 8t-3)$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221105 1072 #uuid "ed95a2ca-9616-5e4f-9a69-a62d897c0172" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221105 1074 "Since there are infinitely many solutions to the system, both equations have the same set of solutions $(x,y)$. Therefore, we only need to consider the solution set of one of the equations.\n\nLet's consider each answer choice in turn.\n\n- First, we must construct a parametrization of the form $(t, f(t))$. So, we set $x = t$ and solve the equation $6x-3y = 9$ for $y$, as follows:\n$6x - 3y|= 9; - 3y|=-6x + 9; 3y|= 6x - 9; y|= \\frac{6x - 9}{3}; y|= 2x - 3; y|= 2t - 3$\nHence, $(t,\\, 2t-3)$ is a valid parametrization. $\\color{green}{\\checkmark}$\n- Next, we must construct a parametrization of the form $(g(t), t)$. So, we set $y = t$ and solve the equation $6x-3y = 9$ for $x$, as follows:\n$6x - 3y|= 9; 6x|= 3y + 9; x|= \\frac{3y + 9}{6}; x|= \\frac{1}{2}y + \\frac{3}{2}; x|= \\frac{1}{2}t + \\frac{3}{2}$\nThis gives $\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}t + \\dfrac{3}{2},\\, t\\right)$, which is *not* the same as $(3t-9,\\, t)$. Hence, $(3t-9,\\, t)$ is **NOT** a valid parametrization. $\\color{red}{\\times}$\n- Finally, we must construct a parametrization of the form $(4t,h(t))$. So, we set $x=4t$ and solve the equation $6x-3y=9$ for $y$, as follows:\n$6x - 3y|= 9; - 3y|=-6x + 9; 3y|= 6x - 9; y|= \\frac{6x - 9}{3}; y|= 2x - 3; y|= 2(4t) - 3; y|= 8t - 3$\nwhich holds for all $t$. Hence, $(4t,\\, 8t-3)$ is a valid parametrization. $\\color{green}{\\checkmark}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is **only I and III.**" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221106 1069 "e-21060" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221106 1071 "A right triangle has legs with lengths of $4\\,\\textrm{cm}$ and $6\\sqrt{3}\\,\\textrm{cm}$. If the length of this triangle's hypotenuse can be written in the form $2\\sqrt{d}\\,\\textrm{cm}$, where $d$ is an integer, what is the value of $d$?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221106 1072 #uuid "8117aff7-5b15-5724-a89d-13df3dbd1ca9" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221106 1074 "We begin by sketching a right triangle with legs whose lengths are $4\\,\\textrm{cm}$ and $6\\sqrt{3}\\,\\textrm{cm}$, and a hypotenuse with length $2\\sqrt{d}\\,\\textrm{cm}$.\n\n![](images/b3/b335f2381ab4b63f3dbeb121224357edeb4b59a5eea9e10cc76b1a140cce6cbe.png)\n\nThe Pythagorean theorem states that for a right triangle,\n\n$$\nc^2 = a^2 + b^2\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ represent the lengths of the legs of the triangle, and $c$ represents the length of its hypotenuse.\n\nIn our case, we are given the lengths of the legs\n\n$$\na = 4\\,\\textrm{cm}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nb = 6\\sqrt{3}\\,\\textrm{cm}\n$$\n\nand the length of the hypotenuse\n\n$$\nc = 2\\sqrt{d}\\,\\textrm{cm}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula above and solving for $d$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(2\\sqrt{d})^{2} &= 4^{2} + (6\\sqrt{3})^{2} \\\\\n4d &= 16 + 108 \\\\\n4d &= 124 \\\\\nd &= \\frac{124}{4} \\\\\nd &= 31\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221107 1069 "e-21061" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221107 1071 "An isosceles right triangle has a side of length $\\sqrt{648}\\,\\textrm{cm}$ and another side of length $9\\sqrt{4}\\,\\textrm{cm}$. In square centimeters, what is the area of the triangle?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221107 1072 #uuid "dfa8e94b-5d00-5e47-beb0-c63b14da4b94" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221107 1074 "To determine which side is the hypotenuse (i.e., the longest side), we rewrite the radicals as single square roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n9\\sqrt{4} &= \\sqrt{81} \\cdot \\sqrt{4} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{81 \\cdot 4} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{324}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compare the square roots directly:\n$\\sqrt{648} > \\sqrt{324}$\n\nSo, the hypotenuse is $\\sqrt{648}$, and the legs are $9\\sqrt{4}$ and $9\\sqrt{4}$.\n\nNext, we apply the formula for the area of a $45^\\circ$-$45^\\circ$-$90^\\circ$ triangle\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A = \\dfrac{1}{2} s^2\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is a leg. Substituting the values, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot (9\\sqrt{4})^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 9^{2} \\cdot (\\sqrt{4})^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 81 \\cdot 4 \\\\\n&= 81 \\cdot \\frac{4}{2} \\\\\n&= 81 \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= 162\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the triangle is $162 \\,\\textrm{cm}^2$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221108 1069 "e-21062" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221108 1071 "A right triangle has side lengths $2\\sqrt{2}$, $5\\sqrt{2}$, and $\\sqrt{58}$ units. What is the area of the triangle, in square units?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221108 1072 #uuid "f47dc2ab-3ce6-5640-9d34-5a457fdc8bca" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221108 1074 "We are given that the triangle has side lengths:\n$2\\sqrt{2}, \\quad 5\\sqrt{2}, \\quad \\sqrt{58}$\n\nTo determine which side is the hypotenuse (i.e., the longest side), we rewrite the radicals as single square roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2\\sqrt{2} &= \\sqrt{2^{2}} \\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4} \\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4 \\cdot 2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{8} \\\\\n5\\sqrt{2} &= \\sqrt{5^{2}} \\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{25} \\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{25 \\cdot 2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{50}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compare the square roots directly:\n$\\sqrt{58} > \\sqrt{50} > \\sqrt{8}$\n\nSo, the hypotenuse is $\\sqrt{58}$, and the legs are $2\\sqrt{2}$ and $5\\sqrt{2}$.\n\nNext, we apply the formula for the area of a right triangle\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A = \\dfrac{1}{2} ab\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are the legs. Substituting the values, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 2\\sqrt{2} \\cdot 5\\sqrt{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 10 \\cdot (\\sqrt{2} \\cdot \\sqrt{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 10 \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= \\frac{20}{2} \\\\\n&= 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the triangle is $10$ square units." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221109 1069 "e-21063" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221109 1071 "A metal toolbox is shaped like a right rectangular prism. It has a length of $18~\\mathrm{cm}$, a width of $9~\\mathrm{cm}$, and a height of $7~\\mathrm{cm}$. What is the surface area, in square centimeters $(\\mathrm{cm}^2)$, of the toolbox?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221109 1072 #uuid "8fe1a57d-eda1-534b-8f22-74b5b7cb61b9" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221109 1074 "To find the surface area $A$ of a right rectangular prism, we use the formula\n\n$$\nA = 2(\\ell w + \\ell h + w h)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\ell$ is the length, $w$ is the width, and $h$ is the height.\n\nSubstituting the given values $\\ell = 18$, $w = 9$, and $h = 7$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= 2(18 \\cdot 9 + 18 \\cdot 7 + 9 \\cdot 7) \\\\\n&= 2(162 + 126 + 63) \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 351 \\\\\n&= 702\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area of the toolbox is $702 \\, \\mathrm{cm}^2$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221110 1069 "e-21064" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221110 1071 "A right rectangular prism has dimensions of $4$ inches by $6$ inches by $10$ inches. What is the surface area, in square inches, of the prism?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221110 1072 #uuid "ee4465b5-9eb7-56e0-be76-77e006f5c4c4" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221110 1074 "To find the surface area $A$ of a right rectangular prism, we use the formula\n\n$$\nA = 2(\\ell w + \\ell h + w h)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\ell$, $w$, and $h$ are the length, width, and height of the prism.\n\nSubstituting the given values $\\ell = 4$, $w = 6$, and $h = 10$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= 2(4 \\cdot 6 + 4 \\cdot 10 + 6 \\cdot 10) \\\\\n&= 2(24 + 40 + 60) \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 124 \\\\\n&= 248\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the surface area of the prism is $248$ square inches." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221111 1069 "e-21066" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221111 1071 "$V = \\pi \\cdot (2r + 6) \\cdot r^2$\nA cylindrical can has a height that is $6$ centimeters more than twice its radius $r$ of the base. The function $V$, given above, models the volume of the can, in cubic centimeters. Find the expression representing the height, in centimeters, of the can." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221111 1072 #uuid "81cef7cb-004c-53f7-b746-dde60a5f56e2" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221111 1074 "The volume of a cylinder is given by\n\n$$\nV = \\pi \\cdot \\text{height} \\cdot \\text{radius}^2\n$$\n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\nV = \\pi \\cdot (2r+6) \\cdot r^2\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the radius of the base.\n\nTherefore, comparing the two expressions, we conclude that the height must be\n$2r+6$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221112 1069 "e-21067" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221112 1071 "What is the radius of a sphere whose volume is $\\dfrac{32}{3}\\pi\\:\\textrm{m}^3$?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221112 1072 #uuid "f0c0e5d6-25a1-5320-be2a-5755e00f7151" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221112 1074 "We can find the volume of a sphere using the formula\n\n$$\nV=\\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi r^3\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the radius of the sphere.\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\nV=\\dfrac{32}{3}\\pi\\:\\textrm{m}^3\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= \\frac{4}{3}πr^{3} \\\\\n\\frac{32}{3}π &= \\frac{4}{3}πr^{3} \\\\\n\\frac{32π \\cdot 3}{3 \\cdot 4π} &= r^{3} \\\\\nr^{3} &= 8 \\\\\n&= 2^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nr = \\sqrt[3]{2^3} = 2\\:\\textrm{m}\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221113 1069 "e-21068" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221113 1071 "The equation $(x-3)^2+(y+2)^2=25$ represents circle $\\text{A}$. Circle $\\text{B}$ is obtained by shifting circle $\\text{A}$ down $4$ units in the $xy$-plane. The point $(a,b)$ lies on circle $\\text{B}$. Which of the following is a possible value of $b$?\n\n1. $-1$\n2. $-12$\n3. $0$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221113 1072 #uuid "f1c920a3-88ab-56b5-a766-d0fd8a05b151" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221113 1074 "We're given the equation of circle $\\text{A}$\n\n$$\n(x-3)^2+(y+2)^2=25\n$$\n\nand we wish to translate it by $4$ units down.\n\nFor each point on circle $\\text{A}$, we must subtract $4$ from each $y$-coordinate to get the corresponding point on circle $\\text{B}$. So, the new $y$-coordinates of the points on circle $\\text{B}$ are given by\n\n$$\ny_{\\textrm{new}} = y - 4\n$$\n\nThis equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\ny = y_{\\textrm{new}} + 4\n$$\n\nTo get the equation of circle $\\text{B}$, we substitute this into the equation of circle $\\text{A}$. Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x - 3)^{2} + (y + 2)^{2} &= 25 \\\\\n(x - 3)^{2} + ((y_{new} + 4) + 2)^{2} &= 25\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDropping the $\\textrm{new}$ suffix, we get\n\n$$\n(x-3)^2+\\big((y + 4)+2\\big)^2 = 25\n$$\n\nFinally, simplifying the expression, we have\n\n$$\n(x-3)^2+(y+6)^2 = 25\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of circle $\\text{B}$ is\n\n$$\n(x-3)^2+(y+6)^2 = 25\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the range of possible values of $b$, where $(a,b)$ is a point on the circle.\n\nThe center of circle $\\text{B}$ is $(3,-6)$, and its radius is $r = 5$. Therefore, the range of $y$-values on the circle is\n\n$$\n-6 - 5 \\leq y \\leq -6 + 5\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n-11 \\leq y \\leq -1\n$$\n\nWe can write this as $y\\in [-11,-1]$.\n\nLet's now consider each value in turn.\n\n- $b=-1$ is possible since $-1\\in [-11,-1]\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n- $b=-12$ is NOT possible since $-12\\notin [-11,-1]\\quad{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n- $b=0$ is NOT possible since $0\\notin [-11,-1]\\quad{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is, \"I only.\"" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221114 1069 "e-21071" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221114 1071 "A circle has center $O$, and points $A$ and $B$ lie on the circle. The measure of arc $\\overset{\\frown}{AB}$ is $84^\\circ$. What is the measure of $\\angle OAB$, in degrees?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221114 1072 #uuid "01986898-8882-5a6d-8169-785e4a7188f2" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221114 1074 "![](images/9d/9d29a77e0fcdc633d55ec14ab4e34689c3c09770d195fd337f350ed05e7d1e4a.png)\n\nSince $\\overline{OA}$ and $\\overline{OB}$ are both radii of the circle, triangle $OAB$ is isosceles with $OA = OB$. Thus, the base angles $\\angle OAB$ and $\\angle OBA$ are congruent.\n\nIn $\\triangle OAB$, the measures of the interior angles sum to $180^\\circ{:}$\n\n$$\nm\\angle OAB + m\\angle OBA + m\\angle AOB = 180^\\circ\n$$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\nm\\overset{\\frown}{AB} = 84^\\circ\n$$\n\nSince $\\angle AOB$ is the central angle corresponding to arc $\\overset{\\frown}{AB}$, we have\n\n$$\nm\\angle AOB = 84^\\circ\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nm\\angle OAB = m\\angle OBA\n$$\n\nwe let each equal $x$. Substituting, we solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + x + 84^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n2x + 84^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n2x &= 96^{∘} \\\\\nx &= 48^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the measure of $\\angle OAB$ is\n\n$$\nm\\angle OAB = 48^\\circ\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221115 1069 "e-21072" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221115 1071 "Points $M$ and $N$ lie on a circle with center $O$. The radius of the circle is $9\\,\\mathrm{cm}$. Triangle $OMN$ has a perimeter of $29 \\: \\text{cm}$. What is the length, in centimeters, of $MN$?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221115 1072 #uuid "4e9b084c-43f5-5e3a-b95f-e8a3cce736a3" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221115 1074 "![](images/c5/c5da6a07fae64f3becabed9cce9cb7b38d60081d947ca2210093ecbac3ba94d2.png)\n\nThe triangle $OMN$ is formed by connecting the center $O$ to two points on the circle, $M$ and $N$. In this triangle, the segments $\\overline{OM}$ and $\\overline{ON}$ are both radii of the circle, which means\n\n$$\nOM = ON = 9 \\: \\text{cm}\n$$\n\nNow, the perimeter of triangle $OMN$ is given by\n\n$$\np = OM + ON + MN\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the perimeter of $\\triangle OMN$ is $29$, we can solve for MN the follwoing eqaution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np &= OM + ON + MN \\\\\n29 &= 9 + 9 + MN \\\\\n29 &= 18 + MN \\\\\nMN &= 29 - 18 \\\\\n&= 11cm\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221116 1069 "e-21073" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221116 1071 "In the $xy$-plane, a circle has center $O$ with coordinates $(a, b)$. Points $P$ and $Q$ lie on the circle. Point $P$ has coordinates $(a+4, b+\\sqrt{18})$, and $\\angle{POQ}$ is a right angle. What is the length of $\\overline{PQ}$?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221116 1072 #uuid "1690ae85-dd32-5e73-a45c-b81568443b19" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221116 1074 "![](images/29/29fba0df3a849db787252e4eaf665c8c77e6aa6e929f4c88d238716886b7359d.png)\n\nWe are given that $O$ is the center of the circle, and $P$ and $Q$ lie on the circle. Therefore, $\\overline{OP}$ and $\\overline{OQ}$ are radii of the circle. Hence, $OP = OQ$.\n\nWe know that the coordinates of $O$ and $P$ are $(a, b)$ and $(a+4, b+\\sqrt{18})$, respectively. Hence, we can calculate $OP$ using the distance formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nOP &= \\sqrt{(x_{2} - x_{1})^{2} + (y_{2} - y_{1})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(a + 4 - a)^{2} + (b + \\sqrt{18} - b)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4^{2} + (\\sqrt{18})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{16 + 18} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{34}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\nOQ = OP = \\sqrt{34}\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\triangle{POQ}$ is a right triangle because $\\angle{POQ}$ is a right angle. Therefore, applying the Pythagorean theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nPQ^{2} &= OP^{2} + OQ^{2} \\\\\n&= OP^{2} + OP^{2} \\\\\n&= 2OP^{2} \\\\\n&= 2(\\sqrt{34})^{2} \\\\\n&= 2(34) \\\\\n&= 68\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nPQ &= \\sqrt{68} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4 \\cdot 17} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4} \\cdot \\sqrt{17} \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{17}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221117 1069 "e-21075" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221117 1071 "![](images/a9/a9bf59183914f35c9ce658505240065f6e28f74217b073b49167d8d1922810f5.png)\n\nPoint $O$ is the center of the circle above, and the measure of $\\angle OCD$ is $25^{\\circ}$. If the length of $\\overline{OE}$ is $18$, what is the length of arc $\\overset{\\frown}{CD}$?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221117 1072 #uuid "b3a84f1c-61c5-5685-b530-4e767a6a4a51" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221117 1074 "To find the length of a circular arc with central angle $\\theta$ and radius $r$, we use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{Arc Length} = \\dfrac{\\theta}{360^\\circ} \\cdot 2\\pi r\n$$\n\nSince $\\overline{OC}, \\overline{OD}$, and $\\overline{OE}$ are all radii of the circle, triangle $COD$ is isosceles with\n\n$$\n\\overline{OC} = \\overline{OD}\n$$\n\nThus, the base angles are equal:\n\n$$\nm\\angle ODC = m\\angle OCD = 25^\\circ\n$$\n\nUsing the triangle angle sum formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠ODC + m∠OCD + m∠COD &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nm∠COD &= 180^{∘} - (m∠OCD + m∠COD) \\\\\n&= 180^{∘} - (25^{∘} + 25^{∘}) \\\\\n&= 180^{∘} - 50^{∘} \\\\\n&= 130^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the central angle of arc $\\overset{\\frown}{CD}$ is\n\n$$\n\\theta = 130^\\circ\n$$\n\nand the radius of the circle is $r = 18$, the length of arc $\\overset{\\frown}{CD}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nLength(CD^(⌢)) &= \\frac{130^{∘}}{360^{∘}} \\cdot 2π \\cdot 18 \\\\\n&= \\frac{13}{36} \\cdot 36π \\\\\n&= 13π\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221118 1069 "e-21086" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221118 1071 "![](images/f2/f2b6eebb6823d3ca5206862a647b59cb76bf58623c067918afe106db177d3b43.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, lines $m$ and $n$ are parallel, $r = 50$, and $s = 60$. What is the value of $x$?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221118 1072 #uuid "beb4c507-8db4-5c84-9291-156c3717eac8" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221118 1074 "Consider the triangle, shown in blue below, formed by the two non-parallel lines and the line $m$. Let $t^\\circ$ be the measure of the third angle of this triangle.\n\n![](images/b4/b4dd63a056847dc2397a112b5f19c4119a97cd3ade5357f55d90660d0fb76699.png)\n\nNote that, except for the side that is contained in the line $m$, the sides of the triangle act as transversals to the lines $m$ and $n$. Therefore, the angles whose measures are $s^\\circ$ and $t^\\circ$ are corresponding.\n\nWe are given that $m$ and $n$ are parallel. When a transversal intersects two parallel lines, corresponding angles are congruent. Therefore,\n\n$$\nt^\\circ = s^\\circ\n$$\n\n![](images/2c/2cf685a6a2fa15aa6f810927192d7ce41250d147050e6b40d39b7aed0dfab9d7.png)\n\nIn any triangle, the sum of the measures of its interior angles is $180^\\circ$. So, we have\n\n$$\nx^\\circ + r^\\circ + t^\\circ = 180^\\circ\n$$\n\nSubstituting in the known values, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{∘} + 50^{∘} + 60^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nx^{∘} + 110^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nx^{∘} &= 70^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can conclude that $x = 70$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221119 1069 "e-21087" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221119 1071 "In convex pentagon $LMNOP$, segment $\\overline{LM}$ is parallel to segment $\\overline{OP}$. If the measure of $\\angle M$ is $132^\\circ$ and the measure of $\\angle O$ is $150^\\circ$, what is the measure, in degrees, of $\\angle N$?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221119 1072 #uuid "c2cc2072-4785-5fcc-b6b9-b83e4010879b" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221119 1074 "We proceed to represent the given information in a diagram.\n\n![](images/ca/ca8987602a8369e06e1f7f88ad204c429626015803b02d21b60b0f229426d571.png)\n\nWe are given that $\\overline{LM}$ is parallel to $\\overline{OP}$. When transversal $\\overline{LP}$ cuts both, the consecutive interior angles formed are supplementary. So,\n\n$$\nm\\angle{L} + m\\angle{P} = 180^\\circ\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the sum of the interior angles of a polygon with $n$ sides equals\n$180^\\circ \\cdot (n-2)$.\nEvery pentagon has $n = 5$ sides. Applying the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n180^{∘} \\cdot (n - 2) &= 180^{∘} \\cdot (5 - 2) \\\\\n&= 180^{∘} \\cdot 3 \\\\\n&= 540^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for our pentagon $LMNOP$, we have\n\n$$\nm\\angle{L} + m\\angle{M} + m\\angle{N} + m\\angle{O} + m\\angle{P} = 540^\\circ\n$$\n\nFinally, if we substitute the values given for $m\\angle{M}$ and $m\\angle{O}$ into the previous equality, take into account the relationship between $m\\angle{L}$ and $m\\angle{P}$, and then solve for $m\\angle{N}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠L + m∠M + m∠N + m∠O + m∠P &= 540^{∘} \\\\\nm∠N + m∠M + m∠O + (m∠L + m∠P) &= 540^{∘} \\\\\nm∠N + 132^{∘} + 150^{∘} + 180^{∘} &= 540^{∘} \\\\\nm∠N &= 78^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221120 1069 "e-21090" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221120 1071 "![](images/46/46b6d1e78bfe1a0018098b67cff20c8358d37f7ede7ee47e6425250fb4595537.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, $\\overline{AD}$, $\\overline{BE}$, and $\\overline{CF}$ are parallel. Points $B$ and $E$ lie on $\\overline{AC}$ and $\\overline{DF}$, respectively. If $AB = 10$, $BC = 20$, and $EF = 9$, what is the length of $\\overline{DE}$?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221120 1072 #uuid "9a4fb526-ceaa-5840-ae87-1b52a6e61cb1" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221120 1074 "Since $\\overline{AD}$, $\\overline{BE}$, and $\\overline{CF}$ are parallel, we have four pairs of corresponding angles formed by the transversals $\\overline{AC}$ and $\\overline{DF}{:}$\n\n- The angles $\\angle{DAB}$ and $\\angle{EB C}$ are corresponding, formed by the transveral $\\overline{AC}$.\n- The angles $\\angle{ABE}$ and $\\angle{BCF}$ are corresponding, formed by the transveral $\\overline{AC}$.\n- The angles $\\angle{BED}$ and $\\angle{CFE}$ are corresponding, formed by the transveral $\\overline{DF}$.\n- The angles $\\angle{E DA}$ and $\\angle{F EB}$ are corresponding, formed by the transveral $\\overline{DF}$.\n\nCorresponding angles are congruent.\n\n![](images/96/96b34e00420f201fb5f9d8809937d62ddd7c6e34a902ef74d883b44f3b5149ea.png)\n\nAs a result, trapezoids $DABE$ and $EBCF$ are similar polygons.\n\nIn similar polygons, corresponding sides are proportional. In particular, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{AB}{BC} = \\dfrac{DE}{EF}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known values and solving for $DE$, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{10}{20} &= \\frac{DE}{9} \\\\\nDE &= \\frac{9 \\cdot 10}{20} \\\\\nDE &= \\frac{90}{20} \\\\\nDE &= 4.5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221121 1069 "e-21091" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221121 1071 "| Triangle | Perimeter |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $\\triangle ABC$ | $36$ |\n| $\\triangle XYZ$ | $72$ |\n\nTriangles $XYZ$ and $ABC$ are similar, with $\\overline{BC}$ corresponding to $\\overline{YZ}$. The perimeters of the triangles are given in the table above. If $BC = 8$, what is the length of $\\overline{YZ}$?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221121 1072 #uuid "5d0f6ca1-4796-5c81-b592-867a939e0d07" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221121 1074 "Recall that similar triangles have proportional corresponding side lengths and perimeters.\n\nWe are given the following:\n\n- The perimeter of $\\triangle XYZ$ is $72$.\n- The perimeter of $\\triangle ABC$ is $36$.\n- Side $\\overline{BC}$ corresponds to side $\\overline{YZ}$ with $BC = 8$.\n\nThe scale factor from $\\triangle ABC$ to $\\triangle XYZ$ is\n\n$$\nk = \\frac{72}{36} = 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of $\\overline{YZ}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nYZ &= k \\cdot BC \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 8 \\\\\n&= 16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221122 1069 "e-21092" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221122 1071 "![](images/59/59964ea4fcd8758ffbd36f843a1344d079880dd1495dffcfb21763941f410965.png)\n\nNote: Figure not drawn to scale.\n\nIn the figure above, $\\overline{BF}$ intersects $\\overline{DE}$ at point $C$, and $\\overline{BD} \\parallel \\overline{EF}$. The lengths of $\\overline{DC}$, $\\overline{BC}$, and $\\overline{CE}$ are $3$, $16$, and $21$, respectively. What is the length of $\\overline{BF}$?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221122 1072 #uuid "2e324e12-1442-5c5a-835b-6dd76d954ff7" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221122 1074 "From the diagram, we can see that $\\overline{BF}$ is transversal to $\\overline{BD}$ and $\\overline{EF}$. Since we are given that $\\overline{BD}$ and $\\overline{EF}$ are parallel, the pair of alternate interior angles $\\angle{B}$ and $\\angle{F}$ are congruent.\n\nOn the other hand, we know that vertical angles are congruent. Thus, $\\angle{BCD}$ and $\\angle{FCE}$ are congruent.\n\nTherefore, $\\triangle{BCD} \\sim \\triangle{FCE}$ by the AA similarity criterion. As a consequence of the similarity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{BC}{CF} = \\dfrac{DC}{CE}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values for $DC$, $BC$, and $CE$ into the above equality, and then solving for $CF$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{BC}{CF} &= \\frac{DC}{CE} \\\\\n\\frac{16}{CF} &= \\frac{3}{21} \\\\\n\\frac{CF}{16} &= \\frac{21}{3} \\\\\n\\frac{CF}{16} &= 7 \\\\\nCF &= 112\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\nBF = BC + CF\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\nBF = 16 + 112 = 128\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of $\\overline{BF}$ is $128$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221123 1069 "e-21093" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221123 1071 "![](images/27/27c76c538d0e3d091b05c6cdd3abe30314c53dc797560d6ae36c25d9819af82e.png)\n\nIn the figure above, what is the length of $\\overline{BD}$?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221123 1072 #uuid "22c9dd05-71f3-504e-a703-730535d06f58" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221123 1074 "We are given a right triangle $\\triangle{ABC}$ with legs of length $AB = 5$ and $BC = 12$, and an altitude $\\overline{BD}$ dropped from $B$ to hypotenuse $\\overline{AC}$.\n\nFirst, we calculate the length of the hypotenuse $\\overline{AC}$. According to the Pythagorean Theorem,\n\n$$\nAC^2 = AB^2 + BC^2\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAC &= \\sqrt{AB^{2} + BC^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{5^{2} + 12^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{25 + 144} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{169} \\\\\n&= 13\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, consider the triangles $\\triangle ADB$ and $\\triangle ABC$. Both triangles share $\\angle A$, and both contain a right angle: $\\angle ADB$ and $\\angle ABC$, respectively.\n\n![](images/b2/b2cf3fbf63239bbe7c895bc618d2e46d8570f1d44861ec9098214769996ce097.png)\n\nSo, by the AA similarity criterion, the triangles are similar.\n\nSince $\\triangle ADB \\sim \\triangle ABC$, the ratios of corresponding sides are equal. In particular,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{DB}{BC} = \\dfrac{AB}{AC}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in the known values, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{BD}{12} = \\dfrac{5}{13}\n$$\n\nFinally, solving for $BD$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nBD &= \\frac{5}{13} \\cdot 12 \\\\\n&= \\frac{60}{13} \\\\\n&\\approx 4.62\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of $\\overline{BD}$ is $4.62$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221124 1069 "e-21095" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221124 1071 "The radius of circle $A$ is three times the radius of circle $B$. If the area of circle $B$ is $15\\,\\textrm{cm}^2$, what is the area of circle $A$?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221124 1072 #uuid "fc1316e8-22fd-5b4a-9444-8b9c4df73952" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221124 1074 "Recall that all circles are similar.\n\nIf two shapes are similar with scale factor $k$, then the ratio of the two areas is equal to $k^2$.\n\nSince circle $A$ has a radius that is $3$ times the radius of circle $B$, circle $A$ is larger than circle $B$, and we have $k=3$.\n\nTherefore, the ratio of the areas is\n\n$$\nk^2 = 3^2 = 9\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathcal{A}_A}{\\mathcal{A}_B} = 9\n$$\n\nSolving for $\\mathcal{A}_A$ and substituting in\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}_B = 15\\,\\textrm{cm}^2\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_{A} &= 9 \\cdot A_{B} \\\\\n&= 9 \\cdot 15cm^{2} \\\\\n&= 135cm^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221125 1069 "e-21096" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221125 1071 "A teacher builds a scale foam model of a classroom. The actual classroom has an area of $1{,}120\\,\\textrm{ft}^2$. The length of each side of the model classroom is $\\dfrac{1}{20}$ times the length of the corresponding side of the actual classroom. What is the area of the model classroom?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221125 1072 #uuid "e1bf54b4-c9b9-560c-9c0b-303f73d41fa8" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221125 1074 "If two shapes are similar with scale factor $k$, then the ratio of the two areas is equal to $k^2$.\n\nThe length of each side of the model classroom is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{20}\n$$\n\ntimes the length of the corresponding side of the actual classroom.\n\nSo, the length of each side of the actual classroom is $20$ times the length of the corresponding side of the model classroom, and we have $k=20$.\n\nTherefore, the ratio of the areas is\n\n$$\nk^2 = (20)^2=400\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathcal{A}_{\\text{actual}}}{\\mathcal{A}_{\\text{model}}}=400\n$$\n\nSolving for $\\mathcal{A}_{\\text{model}}$ and substituting in\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}_{\\text{actual}}=1{,}120\\,\\textrm{ft}^2\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_{model} &= \\frac{A_{actual}}{400} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1,120ft^{2}}{400} \\\\\n&= 2.8ft^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221126 1069 "e-21097" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221126 1071 "![](images/ba/ba038b224dad67190343e6b156d82d78597c0fccc1daa79b55f363f50da84f7c.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows two circles, where point $V$ and the center of each circle lie on $\\overline{UW}$. The ratio of $UV$ to $VW$ is $9:1$. If the area of the smaller circle is $81\\,\\textrm{cm}^2$, what is the area of the shaded region?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221126 1072 #uuid "98ae155c-5da6-5bcd-9699-2e01121ad134" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221126 1074 "Recall that all circles are similar.\n\nIf two shapes are similar with scale factor $k$, then the ratio of the two areas is equal to $k^2$.\n\nFirst, since $\\overline{UW}$ joins two points on the larger circle and passes through its center, it is a diameter.\nSimilarly, since $\\overline{UV}$ joins two points on the smaller circle and passes through its center, it is a diameter.\n\nGiven that\n\n$$\nUV: VW = 9: 1\n$$\n\nwe can let $UV = 9k$ and $VW = 1k$ for some value $k$. Then, the larger circle is similar to the smaller circle with a scale factor of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nk &= \\frac{UW}{UV} \\\\\n&= \\frac{UV + VW}{UV} \\\\\n&= \\frac{9k + 1k}{9k} \\\\\n&= \\frac{10k}{9k} \\\\\n&= \\frac{10}{9}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the ratio of the areas is\n\n$$\nk^2 = \\left(\\dfrac{10}{9}\\right)^2 = \\dfrac{100}{81}\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathcal{A}_{\\text{larger}}}{\\mathcal{A}_{\\text{smaller}}} = \\dfrac{100}{81}\n$$\n\nSolving for $\\mathcal{A}_{\\text{larger}}$ and substituting in\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}_{\\text{smaller}} = 81\\,\\textrm{cm}^2\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_{larger} &= \\frac{100}{81} \\cdot A_{smaller} \\\\\n&= \\frac{100}{81} \\cdot 81cm^{2} \\\\\n&= 100cm^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the shaded region is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_{shaded} &= A_{larger} - A_{smaller} \\\\\n&= 100 - 81 \\\\\n&= 19cm^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221127 1069 "e-21098" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221127 1071 "Cone $P$ has a height of $6\\,\\textrm{in}$ and is similar to cone $Q$, which has a height of $9\\,\\textrm{in}$. The volume of cone $P$ is $56\\,\\textrm{in}^3$. What is the volume of cone $Q$?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221127 1072 #uuid "da9a831a-4bde-5e9a-a1e4-ea2e4785c626" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221127 1074 "If two solids are similar with scale factor $k$, then the ratio of the corresponding volumes equals $k^3$.\n\nHere, the larger cone ($Q$) is similar to the smaller one ($P$) with a scale factor of\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{9\\,\\textrm{in}}{6\\,\\textrm{in}} = \\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nThus, the ratio of the volume is\n\n$$\nk^3 = \\left(\\dfrac32\\right)^3 = \\dfrac{27}{8}\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathcal{V}_Q}{\\mathcal{V}_P} = \\dfrac{27}{8}\n$$\n\nSolving for $\\mathcal{V}_Q$ and substituting\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{V}_P = 56\\,\\textrm{in}^3\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV_{Q} &= \\frac{27}{8} \\cdot V_{P} \\\\\n&= \\frac{27}{8} \\cdot 56 \\\\\n&= 189in^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221128 1069 "e-21099" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221128 1071 "The height of rectangular prism $P$ is $10\\,\\textrm{cm}$. Rectangular prism $Q$ is similar to prism $P$, and has a surface area that is $36$ times that of $P$. What is the height of prism $Q$?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221128 1072 #uuid "73d40e0d-a1cf-5b79-ba77-ee41271924e6" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221128 1074 "If two solids are similar with scale factor $k$, then the ratio of the corresponding surface areas equals $k^2$.\n\nSince prism $Q$ has a surface area that is $36$ times that of prism $P$, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathcal{A}_Q}{\\mathcal{A}_P} = 36\n$$\n\nThis means that the prisms are similar with a scale factor of\n\n$$\nk = \\sqrt{36} = 6\n$$\n\nThus, the ratio of the heights is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{h_Q}{h_P} = 6\n$$\n\nSolving for $h_Q$ and substituting in\n\n$$\nh_P = 10\\,\\textrm{cm}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nh_{Q} &= 6 \\cdot h_{P} \\\\\n&= 6 \\cdot 10cm \\\\\n&= 60cm\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221129 1069 "e-21100" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221129 1071 "Two triangular prisms, $P$ and $Q$, are similar. The surface area of prism $P$ is $36\\,\\textrm{in}^2$, and the surface area of prism $Q$ is $324\\,\\textrm{in}^2$. If the volume of prism $P$ is $50\\,\\textrm{in}^3$, what is the volume of prism $Q$?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221129 1072 #uuid "df2f2b78-fdf3-5156-a8bc-96663bc24fa1" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221129 1074 "If two solids are similar with scale factor $k$, then\n\n- the ratio of the corresponding surface areas equals $k^2$, and\n- the ratio of the corresponding volumes equals $k^3$.\n\nHere, prism $Q$ is larger than prism $P$. Computing the ratio of the surface areas, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathcal{A}_Q}{\\mathcal{A}_P} = \\dfrac{324\\,\\textrm{in}^2}{36\\,\\textrm{in}^2} = 9\n$$\n\nThis means that the solids are similar with a scale factor of\n\n$$\nk = \\sqrt{9} = 3\n$$\n\nThus, the ratio of the volumes is\n\n$$\nk^3 = 3^3 = 27\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathcal{V}_Q}{\\mathcal{V}_P} = 27\n$$\n\nSolving for $\\mathcal{V}_Q$ and substituting in\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{V}_P = 50\\,\\textrm{in}^3\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV_{Q} &= 27 \\cdot V_{P} \\\\\n&= 27 \\cdot 50in^{3} \\\\\n&= 1,350in^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221130 1069 "e-21101" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221130 1071 "Two square pyramids, $P$ and $Q$, are similar. The volume of pyramid $P$ is $16\\,\\textrm{ft}^3$, and the volume of pyramid $Q$ is $432\\,\\textrm{ft}^3$. If the surface area of pyramid $P$ is $36\\,\\textrm{ft}^2$, what is the surface area of pyramid $Q$?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221130 1072 #uuid "8ebfbdea-ac8c-5dce-b867-c4f5c0626e5f" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221130 1074 "If two solids are similar with scale factor $k$, then\n\n- the ratio of the corresponding volumes equals $k^3$, and\n- the ratio of the corresponding surface areas equals $k^2$.\n\nHere, pyramid $Q$ is larger than pyramid $P$. Computing the ratio of the volumes, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathcal{V}_Q}{\\mathcal{V}_P} = \\dfrac{432}{16} = 27\n$$\n\nThis means that the pyramids are similar with a scale factor of\n\n$$\nk = \\sqrt[3]{27} = 3\n$$\n\nThus, the ratio of the surface areas is\n\n$$\nk^2 = 3^2 = 9\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathcal{A}_Q}{\\mathcal{A}_P} = 9\n$$\n\nSolving for $\\mathcal{A}_Q$ and substituting in\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}_P = 36\\,\\textrm{ft}^2\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_{Q} &= 9 \\cdot A_{P} \\\\\n&= 9 \\cdot 36ft^{2} \\\\\n&= 243ft^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221131 1069 "e-21102" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221131 1071 "Circle $R$ has a radius of $4t$ and circle $S$ has a radius of $28t$, where $t$ is a positive constant. The area of circle $S$ is how many times the area of circle $R$?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221131 1072 #uuid "4c04713c-ec94-5a65-af8c-cddd0b3407e3" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221131 1074 "Recall that all circles are similar.\n\nIf two shapes are similar with scale factor $k$, then the ratio of the two areas is equal to $k^2$.\n\nHere, circle $S$ is similar to circle $R$ with a scale factor of\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{28t}{4t} = 7\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of $S$ is\n\n$$\nk^2 = 7^2 = 49\n$$\n\ntimes larger than the area of $R$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221132 1069 "e-21104" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221132 1071 "Two similar toy packaging boxes, $U$ and $W$, are in the shape of right rectangular prisms. The surface area of box $U$ is $25~\\mathrm{in}^2$, and the surface area of box $W$ is $49~\\mathrm{in}^2$. The volume of box $W$ is $686~\\mathrm{in}^3$. What is the total volume, in cubic inches $(\\mathrm{in}^3)$, of both boxes?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221132 1072 #uuid "c862ff73-43f1-5d16-a58d-27b7338bdf76" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221132 1074 "First, let’s introduce our notation:\n\n- Let $k$ be the scale factor of similarity between the two boxes.\n- Let $S_U = 25$ and $S_W = 49$ represent the surface areas of boxes $U$ and $W$, respectively.\n- Let $V_U$ and $V_W = 686$ represent the volumes of boxes $U$ and $W$, respectively.\n\nSince the ratio of surface areas equals the square of the scale factor, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nk &= \\sqrt{\\frac{S_{U}}{S_{W}}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\frac{25}{49}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{7}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that the volume of similar solids is proportional to the cube of their scale factor. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{V_{U}}{V_{W}} &= (\\frac{5}{7})^{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{125}{343}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $V_U$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV_{U} &= \\frac{125}{343} \\cdot V_{W} \\\\\n&= \\frac{125}{343} \\cdot 686 \\\\\n&= 250\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV_{U} + V_{W} &= 250 + 686 \\\\\n&= 936in^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221133 1069 "e-21113" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221133 1071 "Two identical cubes have a side length of $4$ meters. The cubes are joined together by gluing one of their square faces together. Find the surface area, in square meters, of the combined solid." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221133 1072 #uuid "2bdcf15d-eca7-5142-b0d1-0d0a45c54bde" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221133 1074 "If $s$ denotes the side length of a cube, then its surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS &= 6 \\cdot (face area) \\\\\n&= 6 \\cdot s^{2} \\\\\n&= 6s^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area of a single cube of side length\n\n$$\ns=4\\,\\textrm m\n$$\n\n(before joining them) is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS &= 6(4)^{2} \\\\\n&= 6 \\cdot 16 \\\\\n&= 96m^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that the surface areas of both cubes (before joining) is\n\n$$\n2S = 2\\cdot 96 = 192\\,\\textrm{m}^2\n$$\n\nWhen the two cubes are glued together at their square faces, one face from each is hidden, reducing the total surface area by\n\n$$\n2s^2=2(4)^2 = 32\\,\\textrm{m}^2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area of the combined solid is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2S - 2s^{2} &= 192 - 32 \\\\\n&= 160m^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221134 1069 "e-21114" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221134 1071 "A square-based right rectangular prism has a height of $h$ and a base side length of $s$. The second right rectangular prism has an identical square base but is nine times higher. The two prisms are joined together by gluing one of their square bases together. Construct an expression that gives the surface area of the combined solid." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221134 1072 #uuid "51dde99f-f0dd-5da8-aa02-2b84ab59950c" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221134 1074 "We are given that\n\n- the first prism has a base side length of $s$ and a height of $h$, while\n- the second prism has a base's side length of $s$ and a height of $9h$.\n\nSo, the surface area of the first prism is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{1} &= 2 \\cdot (base area) + 4 \\cdot (lateral surface area) \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot s^{2} + 4 \\cdot sh \\\\\n&= 2s^{2} + 4sh\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd the surface area of the second prism is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{2} &= 2 \\cdot (base area) + 4 \\cdot (lateral surface area) \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot s^{2} + 4 \\cdot s \\cdot 9h \\\\\n&= 2s^{2} + 36sh\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the two prisms are glued together at their square bases, one base from each is hidden, reducing the total surface area by $2s^2$. Therefore, the surface area of the combined solid is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{1} + S_{2} - 2s^{2} &= (2s^{2} + 4sh) + (2s^{2} + 36sh) - 2s^{2} \\\\\n&= 4s^{2} + 40sh - 2s^{2} \\\\\n&= 2s^{2} + 40sh\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221135 1069 "e-21116" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221135 1071 "Two identical wooden blocks are each in the shape of a cylinder with a height of $12$ centimeters. The surface area of each block is $M$ square centimeters. When the two blocks are joined together by gluing one of their bases together, the resulting solid has a surface area of $1.2M$ square centimeters. What is the radius, in centimeters, of each circular base?\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the surface area $S$ of a cylinder is given by*\n$S|= 2 \\cdot (base area) + (lateral surface area); = 2 \\cdot πr^{2} + 2πrh$\n*where $r$ is the base radius and $h$ is its height.*" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221135 1072 #uuid "8dc3a784-3d83-5e47-a809-11ddd63e6523" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221135 1074 "Let $r$ be the radius of the base. Since each block is a cylinder with height $12$ centimeters, its surface area can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= 2πr^{2} + 2πr \\cdot 12 \\\\\n&= 2πr^{2} + 24πr\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the two wooden cylinders are glued together at their bases, one base from each is hidden, reducing the total surface area by $2\\pi r^2$. So, the combined surface area is\n$2M - 2\\pi r^2$.\n\nWe are told this equals $1.2M$. So, we obtain the following equation:\n\n$$\n2M - 2\\pi r^2 = 1.2M\n$$\n\nRearranging terms and simplifying, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2M - 1.2M &= 2πr^{2} \\\\\n(2 - 1.2)M &= 2πr^{2} \\\\\n0.8M &= 2πr^{2} \\\\\n0.4M &= πr^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the expression\n\n$$\nM = 2\\pi r^2 + 24\\pi r\n$$\n\ninto\n\n$$\n0.4M = \\pi r^2\n$$\n\nand simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.4(2πr^{2} + 24πr) &= πr^{2} \\\\\n5 \\cdot 0.4(2πr^{2} + 24πr) &= 5 \\cdot πr^{2} \\\\\n2(2πr^{2} + 24πr) &= 5πr^{2} \\\\\n4πr^{2} + 48πr &= 5πr^{2} \\\\\n48πr &= πr^{2} \\\\\n48r &= r^{2} \\\\\nr^{2} - 48r &= 0 \\\\\nr(r - 48) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $r = 0$ or $r = 48$. We disregard $r = 0$ since the radius must be positive.\n\nTherefore, the radius of the base is $48 \\: \\mathrm{cm}$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221136 1069 "e-21117" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221136 1071 "![](images/68/680a4b3256f0c05d2e4fe9a1d5520847865647348fd2060e69c576b3866a564e.png)\n\nNote: Figure not drawn to scale.\n\nIn the figure shown, $\\overline{E Y}$ and $\\overline{F W}$ intersect at point $P$. $WP = 40$, $EP = 64$, $EF = 48$, and $FP = 96$. What is the length of $\\overline{W Y}$?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221136 1072 #uuid "01cd3ad3-41a9-5c38-b723-3ed33bd99e78" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221136 1074 "In the triangles $\\triangle EFP$ and $\\triangle WYP$, there are two pairs of congruent angles:\n\n- $m\\angle E = m\\angle W = b^\\circ$, and\n- $\\angle EPF$ and $\\angle WPY$ are vertical and so have equal measures.\n\nThus, $\\triangle EFP \\sim \\triangle WYP$ by the AA similarity criterion. As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{EF}{WY} = \\frac{EP}{WP}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $WP = 40$, $EP = 64$, and $EF = 48$, we substitute these values and solve for $WY{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{48}{WY} &= \\frac{64}{40} \\\\\n48 \\cdot 40 &= 64 \\cdot WY \\\\\n1920 &= 64 \\cdot WY \\\\\nWY &= \\frac{1920}{64} \\\\\n&= 30\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221137 1069 "e-21118" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221137 1071 "In a triangle $\\triangle{GRS}$, the angle $\\angle{R}$ is a right angle. Point $T$ lies on $\\overline{GS}$, and point $U$ lies on $\\overline{RS}$ such that $\\overline{TU}$ is parallel to $\\overline{GR}$. If $m\\angle{GSR} = 26^\\circ$, what is the measure, in degrees, of $\\angle GTU$?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221137 1072 #uuid "c0f10ef1-7c0c-528b-95c6-f3dae77eecb9" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221137 1074 "We proceed to represent the given information in a diagram.\n\n![](images/7c/7c5e5ea6195b23fe4bcd4a93c0335145847691812a9d971ebb7636ba1a174869.png)\n\nSince $\\overline{TU}$ is parallel to $\\overline{GR}$ and $\\angle{R}$ is a right angle, $\\angle{SUT}$ is also a right angle.\n\nWe consider the triangle $\\triangle{STU}$. In any triangle, the sum of the measures of its interior angles is $180^\\circ$. Therefore,\n\n$$\nm\\angle{UTS} + m\\angle{SUT} + m\\angle{TSU} = 180^\\circ\n$$\n\nTaking into account that\n\n$$\nm\\angle{SUT} = 90^\\circ\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nm\\angle{TSU} = m\\angle{GSR} = 26^\\circ\n$$\n\nwe can determine $m\\angle{UTS}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠UTS + m∠SUT + m∠TSU &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nm∠UTS + 90^{∘} + 26^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nm∠UTS + 116^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nm∠UTS &= 64^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, note that $\\angle{GTU}$ and $\\angle{UTS}$ form a linear pair, so they are supplementary. This means\n\n$$\nm\\angle{GTU} + m\\angle{UTS} = 180^\\circ\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting\n\n$$\nm\\angle{UTS} = 64^\\circ\n$$\n\nin the equality above and solving for $m\\angle{GTU}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠GTU + m∠UTS &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nm∠GTU + 64^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nm∠GTU &= 116^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221138 1069 "e-21119" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221138 1071 "A $300$-foot tall radio tower casts a shadow $120$ feet long. At the same time, a nearby antenna casts a shadow $20$ feet long. What is the height, in feet, of the antenna?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221138 1072 #uuid "ef7eb6a6-0095-59ed-b688-e26c5c2d5661" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221138 1074 "In the figure below, $AC$ represents the tower, which is $300$ feet tall, and $CB$ is the shadow cast by the tower, measuring $120$ feet. The antenna is shown as $DE$, with $BE$ as its shadow, which is $20$ feet long.\n\n![](images/1e/1ede13b27a38c27994ad346a4e7025b745c0649fa599e6de28beaf7c3a997dc6.png)\n\nNote that since the measurements are taken at the same time, both objects have the same angle of elevation $\\theta$, making the right triangles $\\triangle ACB$ and $\\triangle DEB$ similar by the AA similarity criterion.\n\nSince the triangles are similar, the ratio of height to shadow length is the same for both, so\n\n$$\n\\frac{AC}{CB} = \\frac{DE}{BE}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{300}{120} = \\frac{DE}{20}\n$$\n\nSolving for $DE$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nDE &= 20 \\cdot \\frac{300}{120} \\\\\n&= \\frac{6, 000}{120} \\\\\n&= 50feet\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the height of the antenna is $50$ feet." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221139 1069 "e-21122" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221139 1071 "A rectangular banner has an area of $280$ square feet. A new version of the banner is built in which both the length and width are increased by $25\\%$. What is the area, in square feet, of the new banner?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221139 1072 #uuid "3e13ed4f-c1a0-5a77-9ea5-5b11a5b4cee6" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221139 1074 "When an object is scaled by a factor of $k$ in all dimensions, its area is scaled by a factor of $k^2$.\n\nIn our case, the increase is $25\\%$, so the scale factor is\n\n$$\nk = 1 + \\dfrac{25}{100} = 1.25\n$$\n\nThus, the area of the new banner will be\n\n$$\nk^2 = \\left(1.25 \\right)^2 = 1.5625\n$$\n\ntimes the area of the old one.\n\nTherefore, the area of the new banner is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nArea &= k^{2} \\cdot 280 \\\\\n&= 1.5625 \\cdot 280 \\\\\n&= 437.5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nsquare feet." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221140 1069 "e-21125" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221140 1071 "![](images/b5/b52ac9762939d3b4e1b2c4e5052310a033874c50ec5b53b46aa057b1ce9d3d9a.png)\n\nTriangles $ABC$ and $RST$ each have a side measuring $4$ and a side measuring $9$, as shown above. Which of the following additional pieces of information is sufficient to prove that these triangles are congruent?\n\n1. The measure of $\\angle{T}$ is $20^\\circ$.\n2. The measures of $\\angle B$ and $\\angle S$ are equal.\n3. The measures of $\\angle A$ and $\\angle R$ are equal.\n4. No additional information is needed.\n\n*(Note: Images not drawn to scale)*" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221140 1072 #uuid "628ab161-022b-5a5d-9939-2ff90708c04a" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221140 1074 "To prove *congruence* between two triangles, we can use any of the following congruence criteria:\n\n- **SSS**: all three pairs of corresponding sides are equal\n- **SAS**: two pairs of corresponding sides and the included angle are equal\n- **ASA**: two pairs of corresponding angles and the included side are equal\n- **AAS**: two pairs of corresponding angles and a non-included side are equal\n- **HL**: In right triangles, the hypotenuse and one leg are equal\n\nIn the triangles $\\triangle ABC$ and $\\triangle RST$, there are two pairs of congruent sides:\n\n- $AB = RS = 4$\n- $AC = RT = 9$\n\nThus, we have two pairs of equal corresponding sides. This alone is not enough to prove congruency.\n\nWith that in mind, let’s analyze each piece of additional information in turn.\n\n- Consider option I (The measure of $\\angle{T}$ is $20^\\circ$). Knowing the measure of a single angle only in one triangle is insufficient to determine congruence between two triangles. ${\\color{red}\\times}$\n\n![](images/57/575ee2377f56b4ac4a24286359f4173569777cf860850f685ca61c2eb2dd33c1.png)\n\n- Consider option II (The measures of $\\angle B$ and $\\angle S$ are equal). Knowing that two sides and a non-included angle of two triangles have equal measures is insufficient to conclude that the triangles are congruent. In other words, there is no Side-Side-Angle criterion for congruent triangles. ${\\color{red}\\times}$\n\n![](images/1a/1ac260ba100e2a2bcc7ed7dea0ff4a7055dafc0cc3ae7d7d0ce05010f9986908.png)\n\n- Consider option III (The measures of $\\angle A$ and $\\angle R$ are equal). These are corresponding angles in the two triangles. If they are equal in measures, the triangles must be congruent by the Side-Angle-Side (SAS) criterion. ${\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\n![](images/47/471704db11736f1446836566baf03d493db3380db08ad3dfa1ecc0eea1ddd005.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221141 1069 "e-21126" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221141 1071 "![](images/09/095f432ac6e4a5d07dfbb374ee207414862764009fda630bbf275e0a8cb557be.png)\n\nConsider the triangles above. Which of the following additional pieces of information is sufficient to determine whether these triangles are similar?\n\n1. The length of the side $\\overline{AB}$.\n2. The measure of $\\angle{C}$.\n3. The measure of $\\angle{C}$ and the length of the side $\\overline{AB}$.\n4. No additional information is needed.\n\n*(Note: Images not drawn to scale)*" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221141 1072 #uuid "f78bdc42-e100-54d4-83c9-5243587f4c79" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221141 1074 "To prove similarity between two triangles, we can use any of the following similarity criteria:\n\n- **AA**: two pairs of corresponding angles are equal\n- **SAS**: two pairs of proportional sides with equal included angles\n- **SSS**: all three pairs of sides are proportional\n\nWe are given that two pairs of corresponding angles match:\n\n$$\nm\\angle A = m\\angle D = 80^\\circ, \\qquad m\\angle B = m\\angle E = 30^\\circ\n$$\n\nThis means we have two pairs of congruent corresponding angles. Therefore, the triangles are similar by the AA criterion. So, no further information is needed to deduce that the triangles are similar.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221142 1069 "e-21127" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221142 1071 "Triangles $PQR$ and $XYZ$ are such that\n$PQ=5,\\quad QR = 7, \\quad\\text{and}\\quad XY=15,\\quad YZ=21$.\nWhich of the following additional pieces of information is sufficient to determine whether these triangles are similar?\n\n1. The lengths of the sides $\\overline{PR}$ and $\\overline{XZ}$.\n2. $m\\angle{P} = m\\angle{X}$.\n3. The length of the side $\\overline{XZ}$.\n4. No additional information is needed." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221142 1072 #uuid "99c6e581-0a36-59c9-93c8-cf7a68b975c5" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221142 1074 "To prove **similarity** between two triangles, we can use any of the following similarity criteria:\n\n- **AA**: two pairs of corresponding angles are equal\n- **SAS**: two pairs of proportional sides with equal included angles\n- **SSS**: all three pairs of sides are proportional\n\nIn triangles $\\triangle PQR$ and $\\triangle XYZ$, we are given two side lengths from each triangle:\n\n$$\n\\frac{PQ}{XY} = \\dfrac{5}{15} = \\dfrac{1}{3}, \\qquad \\frac{QR}{YZ} = \\dfrac{7}{21}=\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/03/0301e2573b88b9dcb14f98c3b2385162148c7c1da9eef3c58ad1810a401a0cb7.png)\n\nSo, the known pairs of sides are already in proportion. However, this information by itself is not enough to conclude similarity.\n\nWith that in mind, let’s analyse each of the pieces of additional information in turn.\n\n- Consider option I (the lengths of the sides $\\overline{PR}$ and $\\overline{XZ}$). If we know $PR$ and $XZ$, then we can check all three pairs of side ratios. If all three match, then the triangles are similar by the SSS criterion. Hence, this information is sufficient. ${\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\n![](images/df/df99bd68171c7b9cfb2665f6af2d2dbfbd341eac29683f97d5ea0679c7948411.png)\n\n- Consider option II ($m\\angle{P} = m\\angle{X})$. If we only know the measures of one pair of corresponding angles that are not the included angles, then this gives an SSA configuration, which does not guarantee similarity. Hence, this information is not sufficient. ${\\color{red}\\times}$\n\n![](images/51/51244d15764eee9b364e9a9b84d9f3655c12b95e583db38ad34ed93ecfdb1056.png)\n\n- Consider option III (The length of the side $\\overline{XZ}$). If we only know one additional side from a single triangle, then we cannot form a pair of corresponding sides. Hence, this information is not sufficient. ${\\color{red}\\times}$\n\n![](images/31/31beeba9bffa9dad00e5496a3b78a5c97ac02a157ae27cb9012508673389e8f6.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221143 1069 "e-21128" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221143 1071 "In triangles $ABC$ and $GHI$, sides $\\overline{BC}$ and $\\overline{HI}$ each have length $6$, and angles $\\angle{A}$ and $\\angle{G}$ each have measure $90^\\circ$. Which additional piece of information is sufficient to prove that triangle $ABC$ is congruent to triangle $GHI$?\n\n1. The lengths of $\\overline{AB}$ and $\\overline{GH}$ are equal.\n2. The length of $\\overline{AB}$.\n3. The measure of $\\angle{I}$.\n4. No additional information is needed." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221143 1072 #uuid "59e07b0a-7582-54f0-9539-55453693c7df" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221143 1074 "To prove *congruence* between two triangles, we can use any of the following congruence criteria:\n\n- **SSS**: all three pairs of corresponding sides are equal\n- **SAS**: two pairs of corresponding sides and the included angle are equal\n- **ASA**: two pairs of corresponding angles and the included side are equal\n- **AAS**: two pairs of corresponding angles and a non-included side are equal\n- **HL**: In right triangles, the hypotenuse and one leg are equal\n\n![](images/67/673b76c372d6975a7ed3ea88bda32ade7d72ec21a03466473c057619b9c2f93b.png)\n\nIn the triangles $\\triangle ABC$ and $\\triangle GHI$, there is one pair of congruent angles and one pair of congruent sides:\n\n- $m\\angle A = m\\angle G = 90^\\circ$\n- $BC = HI = 25$\n\nThus, we have a pair of congruent corresponding angles and a pair of congruent corresponding sides. This alone is not enough to prove congruency.\n\nWith that in mind, let’s analyze each piece of additional information in turn.\n\n- Consider option I (The lengths of $\\overline{AB}$ and $\\overline{GH}$ are equal). Then the triangles *must* be congruent by the Hypotenuse-Leg (HL) criterion. ${\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\n![](images/00/004bd09736d9f813ce4c82e86acf18c9ec8f19c1106d4cde69df88c6550c8c7f.png)\n\n- Consider option II (The length of $\\overline{AB}$). Knowing the length of a single side only in one triangle is insufficient to determine congruence between two triangles. ${\\color{red}\\times}$\n\n![](images/f3/f3714a7c4a730cefeaff408cdba7bd69479bbc6b0a891364a2b67f007181a37b.png)\n\n- Consider option III (The measure of $\\angle{I}$). Knowing the measure of a single angle only in one triangle is insufficient to determine congruence between two triangles. ${\\color{red}\\times}$\n\n![](images/ce/ce2f9be52fc711e1b373ad0cbb734f67b0f27ce67098d657f90952caa66407db.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221144 1069 "e-21130" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221144 1071 "Triangles $\\triangle WXY$ and $\\triangle UVZ$ are plotted in the $xy$-plane. Triangle $\\triangle WXY$ has vertices $W$, $X$, and $Y$ at $(3,2)$, $(3,8)$, and $(11,2)$, respectively. Triangle $\\triangle UVZ$ has vertices $U$, $V$, and $Z$ at $(-2,-1)$, $(-2,-7)$, and $(-10,-1)$, respectively. If $m\\angle X = y^\\circ+11^\\circ$, what is the measure of $\\angle V$?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221144 1072 #uuid "a9f37798-c7ea-541e-8212-6eaf18d2495b" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221144 1074 "First, let's sketch the two triangles described.\n\n![](images/c3/c33e7ff6cf4c079c647b91b878dcd24bf7aaf814df088b98bdc4cffe9f6d4db7.png)\n\nNotice that\n\n- the segments $\\overline{WX}$ and $\\overline{UV}$ both lie on vertical lines, and\n- the segments $\\overline{WY}$ and $\\overline{UZ}$ both lie on horizontal lines.\n\nSo, the angles $\\angle W$ and $\\angle U$ in $\\triangle WXY$ and $\\triangle UVZ$, respectively, are both enclosed by these perpendicular lines; hence, they are right angles.\n\nWe find the measure of $\\angle Y$ using the fact that the sum of interior angles in a triangle equals $180^\\circ{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠W + m∠X + m∠Y &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n90^{∘} + (y^{∘} + 11^{∘}) + m∠Y &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n101^{∘} + y^{∘} + m∠Y &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nm∠Y &= 180^{∘} - 101^{∘} - y^{∘} \\\\\n&= 79^{∘} - y^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the measures of the legs of the triangle $\\triangle WXY$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nWX &= 8 - 2 = 6, \\\\\nWY &= 11 - 3 = 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the measures of the legs of the triangle $\\triangle UVZ$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nUV &= -1 - (-7) = 6, \\\\\nUZ &= -2 - (-10) = 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we have a pair of congruent angles,\n\n$$\nm\\angle W = m\\angle U = 90^\\circ\n$$\n\nand the corresponding sides enclosing the congruent angles are congruent:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nWX &= 6 = UV \\\\\nWY &= 8 = UZ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\triangle WXY \\cong \\triangle UVZ$ by the SAS congruence criterion, with the vertex correspondence\n\n$$\nW \\leftrightarrow U,\\qquad X \\leftrightarrow V,\\qquad Y \\leftrightarrow Z\n$$\n\nTherefore, the measure of angle $V$ is\n\n$$\nm\\angle V = m\\angle Y = 79^\\circ - y^\\circ\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221145 1069 "e-21131" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221145 1071 "Triangles $T U W$ and $D E G$ are congruent, where $T$, $U$, and $W$ correspond to $D$, $E$, and $G$, respectively. The measure of angle $T$ is $39^{\\circ}$ and the measure of angle $U$ is $28^{\\circ}$. What is the measure of angle $G$?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221145 1072 #uuid "19949038-aa02-5926-82e5-7911e613447a" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221145 1074 "Let's draw a sketch of the situation described.\n\n![](images/d6/d6a69fce82a99e11d9b6ea8ccf56bf1b45a4c874a6418f89b875d2f40e2fb3ea.png)\n\nFirst, we find the measure of $\\angle W$ using the fact that the sum of interior angles in a triangle equals $180^\\circ{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠T + m∠U + m∠W &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n39^{∘} + 28^{∘} + m∠W &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n67^{∘} + m∠W &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nm∠W &= 180^{∘} - 67^{∘} \\\\\n&= 113^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\triangle TUW \\cong \\triangle DEG$, with the vertex correspondence\n\n$$\nT \\leftrightarrow D, \\qquad U \\leftrightarrow E, \\qquad W \\leftrightarrow G\n$$\n\ntheir corresponding angles must also be congruent.\n\nTherefore, the measure of angle $G$ is\n\n$$\nm \\angle G = m \\angle W = 113^\\circ\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221146 1069 "e-21132" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221146 1071 "![](images/ab/aba647cf7a6546da0f6e3e25dcea2f4b817e52e9142a0c77def33c017c2603fe.png)\n\nIn the figure above, $\\triangle{XYZ}$ has an area between $70$ and $90$, inclusive, and $q$ is an integer. What is the largest possible value of $p$?\n\nNote: Figure not drawn to scale." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221146 1072 #uuid "bd5f4cda-54dc-52c9-830d-da0ee03957b3" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221146 1074 "The formula for the area of a triangle is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = \\dfrac{b \\cdot h}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere $b$ is a base and $h$ is the corresponding height.\n\nIn the case of $\\triangle{XYZ}$, from the diagram we can see that its base is\n\n$$\nb = 3 + 17 = 20\n$$\n\nand its corresponding height is\n$h = q$.\n\nTherefore, the area of $\\triangle{XYZ}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\frac{b \\cdot h}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{20 \\cdot q}{2} \\\\\n&= 10q\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that the area is between $70$ and $90$, inclusive. Thus, we have the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\n70 \\leq 10q \\leq 90\n$$\n\nDividing by $10$ to isolate $q$, we obtain\n\n$$\n7 \\leq q \\leq 9\n$$\n\nSince $q$ is an integer, its possible values are $7$, $8$, and $9$.\n\nLooking at the diagram again, we can see that the two right triangles are similar: each has a right angle, and they share the angle $\\angle{X}$. As a consequence of this similarity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{p}{q} = \\dfrac{3}{3 + 17}\n$$\n\nSolving for $p$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{p}{q} &= \\frac{3}{3 + 17} \\\\\n\\frac{p}{q} &= \\frac{3}{20} \\\\\np &= \\frac{3q}{20}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, from the possible values of $q$, we will find the possible values of $p$.\n\n- For $q = 7$, we obtain\n$p = \\dfrac{3(7)}{20} = \\dfrac{21}{20}$.\n- For $q = 8$, we obtain\n$p = \\dfrac{3(8)}{20} = \\dfrac{24}{20} = \\dfrac{6}{5}$.\n- For $q = 9$, we obtain\n$p = \\dfrac{3(9)}{20} = \\dfrac{27}{20}$.\n\nTherefore, the largest possible value of $p$ is\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\dfrac{27}{20}}\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221147 1069 "e-21139" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221147 1071 "![](images/72/727b0943a876269b481f9a433d63cc2639aacc7f120d2eb4ef8d8144df5fe068.png)\n\nIn the circle above, point $O$ is the center, and diameter $\\overline{MN}$ bisects chord $\\overline{PQ}$ at point $R$. Find $PQ$ if the radius of the circle is $13 \\: \\text{cm}$ and $OR=5 \\: \\text{cm}$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221147 1072 #uuid "59ed9f66-62f0-5d25-b38a-bf1fa0947eca" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221147 1074 "First, let's draw the radii $\\overline{OP}$ and $\\overline{OQ}$.\n\n![](images/50/503177c06c795741923f3d030590e98b21a73a7237d73c3bab3b3ba0f0e37a91.png)\n\nSince $OP = OQ$, we have that $\\triangle OPQ$ is isosceles. In this triangle, $\\overline{OR}$ is the median drawn to the base. Thus, $\\overline{OR}$ is also a height, and we get $\\overline{OR} \\perp \\overline{PQ}$.\n\nNow $\\triangle ORQ$ is right. So, using the Pythagorean theorem, we have the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nOR^{2} + RQ^{2} &= OQ^{2} \\\\\n(5)^{2} + RQ^{2} &= (13)^{2} \\\\\n25 + RQ^{2} &= 169 \\\\\nRQ^{2} &= 144 \\\\\nRQ &= 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nPQ &= 2RQ \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 12 \\\\\n&= 24cm\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221148 1069 "e-21140" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221148 1071 "![](images/c7/c77146df36aff3f6ed6466677d1b69f4a3a1faeb69124f59eb6361439bff608f.png)\n\nIn the circle above, point $O$ is the center, and diameter $\\overline{PR}$ is perpendicular to chord $\\overline{QS}$ at point $T$. Find $QS$ if the radius of the circle is $34 \\: \\text{cm}$ and $TR=18 \\: \\text{cm}$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221148 1072 #uuid "751209a4-b894-5635-aaba-edca32b2b24e" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221148 1074 "First, let's draw the radii $\\overline{OQ}$ and $\\overline{OS}$.\n\n![](images/4c/4c455108b450cfd3e6157096b03fe12eb05ee8b04bfdc0c09b9938e6ee4ed4e5.png)\n\nSince $OQ = OS$, we have that $\\triangle OSQ$ is isosceles. In this triangle, $\\overline{OT}$ is the height drawn to the base. Thus, $\\overline{OT}$ is also a median, and we get $TS = TQ$.\n\nNow, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nOT &= OR - TR \\\\\n&= 34 - 18 \\\\\n&= 16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\triangle OTS$ is right. So, using the Pythagorean theorem, we have the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nOT^{2} + TS^{2} &= OS^{2} \\\\\n(16)^{2} + TS^{2} &= (34)^{2} \\\\\n256 + TS^{2} &= 1156 \\\\\nTS^{2} &= 900 \\\\\nTS &= \\sqrt{900} \\\\\n&= 30\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQS &= 2TS \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 30 \\\\\n&= 60cm\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221149 1069 "e-21141" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221149 1071 "![](images/cd/cd194a071548c6ee2e2f63b8b77ab435d4d71073e757569c713616bda0af8fc7.png)\n\nA hemisphere and a cone have circular bases of equal circumference and have been connected at their bases, as shown above. The diameter of the base of the hemisphere is $14$ feet, and the total height of the combined solid is $20$ feet. What is the total volume, in cubic feet, of the combined solid?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221149 1072 #uuid "a33d07e7-c253-54e6-92db-09a7debf7f7a" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221149 1074 "We first calculate the volume of the hemisphere using the formula\n\n$$\nV_{\\text{hemisphere}} = \\dfrac{1}{2} V_{\\text{sphere}} = \\dfrac{2}{3} \\pi r^3\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the radius of the sphere.\n\nSince the diameter of the hemisphere is $14$, its radius is\n\n$$\nr=\\dfrac{14}{2}=7\n$$\n\nSubstituting $r = 7$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV_{hemisphere} &= \\frac{2}{3}π(7)^{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{3}π(343) \\\\\n&= \\frac{686}{3}π\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the volume of the cone using the formula\n\n$$\nV_{\\text{cone}} = \\dfrac{1}{3} \\pi r^2 h\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the radius of the base and $h$ is the height of the cone.\n\nSince the total height of the combined solid is $20$, the height of the cone is $h=20-7=13$.\nSubstituting $r = 7$ and $h=13$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV_{cone} &= \\frac{1}{3}π(7)^{2}(13) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}π(49)(13) \\\\\n&= \\frac{637}{3}π\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the volume of the combined solid, we add the volume of the hemisphere and the volume of the cone:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV_{combined} &= V_{hemisphere} + V_{cone} \\\\\n&= \\frac{686}{3}π + \\frac{637}{3}π \\\\\n&= \\frac{1323}{3}π \\\\\n&= 441π\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the combined solid volume is $441\\pi$ cubic feet." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221150 1069 "e-21142" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221150 1071 "A solid sphere with a radius of $6$ meters is placed exactly in the center of a cylinder with a base radius of $6$ meters and a height of $12$ meters so that it touches the center of each base of the cylinder. What is the volume of the space in the cylinder that is not occupied by the sphere?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221150 1072 #uuid "bca1d06f-6b60-5eb9-b6e1-32f91d754df3" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221150 1074 "We first calculate the volume of the cylinder using the formula\n\n$$\nV_{\\text{cylinder}} = \\pi r^2 h\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the radius of the base and $h$ is the height of the cylinder.\nSubstituting $r = 6$ and $h=12$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV_{cylinder} &= π(6)^{2}(12) \\\\\n&= π(36)(12) \\\\\n&= 432π\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the volume of the sphere using the formula\n\n$$\nV_{\\text{sphere}} = \\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi r^3\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the radius of the sphere.\nSubstituting $r = 6$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV_{sphere} &= \\frac{4}{3}π(6)^{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{3}π(216) \\\\\n&= 288π\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the volume of the unoccupied space, we subtract the volume of the sphere from the volume of the cylinder:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV_{empty} &= V_{cylinder} - V_{sphere} \\\\\n&= 432π - 288π \\\\\n&= 144π\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unoccupied volume is $144\\pi$ cubic meters." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221151 1069 "e-21154" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221151 1071 "A storage tank is shaped like a cube with an edge length of $12$ inches. A solid spherical sensor with a radius of $6$ inches is suspended exactly in the center so that it touches the center of each face of the cube. To the nearest cubic inch, what is the volume of the space in the tank that is not occupied by the sensor?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221151 1072 #uuid "c83260e9-5b1b-5dad-9558-f1096d01a306" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221151 1074 "We are given that the cube has an edge length of $12$ inches, and that the sphere inside it has a radius of $6$ inches.\n\nWe first calculate the volume of the cube using the formula for the volume of a cube\n\n$$\nV_{\\text{cube}} = s^{3}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $s = 12$, we have\n\n$$\nV_{\\text{cube}} = 12^{3} = 1\\,728\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the volume of the sphere using the formula\n\n$$\nV_{\\text{sphere}} = \\frac{4}{3}\\pi r^{3}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $r = 6$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV_{sphere} &= \\frac{4}{3}π(6)^{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{3}π(216) \\\\\n&\\approx 904.78\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\nTo find the volume of the unoccupied space, we subtract the volume of the sphere from the volume of the cube:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV_{empty} &= V_{cube} - V_{sphere} \\\\\n&\\approx 1728 - 904.78 \\\\\n&\\approx 823.22\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to the nearest cubic inch, the unoccupied volume is $823$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221152 1069 "e-21179" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221152 1071 "$\\begin{bmatrix}t^{3} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nFind the Wronskian $W(\\mathbf{x}_1, \\mathbf{x}_2)$ of the above vector-valued functions." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221152 1072 #uuid "e9855de8-af52-5bc0-aae2-1dbc5906a659" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221152 1074 "The Wronskian $W(\\mathbf{x}_1,\\mathbf{x}_2)$ of two vector-valued functions\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x_{11}(t) \\\\ x_{21}(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\nis defined as\n$\\begin{vmatrix}x_{11}(t) & x_{12}(t) \\\\ x_{21}(t) & x_{22}(t)\\end{vmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, our Wronskian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nW(x_{1}, x_{2}) &= \\begin{vmatrix}t^{3} & t^{3} - 5 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= t^{3} \\cdot 4 - (t^{3} - 5) \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= 4t^{3} - 2t^{3} + 10 \\\\\n&= 2t^{3} + 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221153 1069 "e-21180" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221153 1071 "$\\begin{bmatrix}-4e^{2t} \\\\ e^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the vector-valued functions $\\mathbf{x}_1(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2(t)$ above. Given that $\\mathbf x_1$ and $\\mathbf x_2$ are solutions of the same system of linear ODEs, compute the Wronskian is $W(\\mathbf{x}_1, \\mathbf{x}_2)$ and determine if the set $\\left\\{\\mathbf{x}_1(t), \\mathbf{x}_2(t) \\right\\}$ is linearly dependent on $\\mathbb R$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221153 1072 #uuid "9ef2af54-fc18-5908-9adb-fb3e2d92c098" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221153 1074 "The Wronskian $W(\\mathbf{x}_1,\\mathbf{x}_2)$ of two vector-valued functions\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x_{11}(t) \\\\ x_{21}(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\nis defined as\n$\\begin{vmatrix}x_{11}(t) & x_{12}(t) \\\\ x_{21}(t) & x_{22}(t)\\end{vmatrix}$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- If the Wronskian is not identically zero, then $\\mathbf{x}_1$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2$ are linearly independent.\n- If the Wronskian is identically zero, and it's known that $\\mathbf{x}_1$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2$ are solutions to the same system of linear ODEs, then $\\mathbf{x}_1$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, our Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nW(x_{1}, x_{2}) &= \\begin{vmatrix}-4e^{2t} & e^{t} \\\\ e^{2t} & 2e^{t}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= (-4e^{2t}) \\cdot (2e^{t}) - e^{t} \\cdot (e^{2t}) \\\\\n&=-8e^{3t} - e^{3t} \\\\\n&=-9e^{3t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is nonzero for at least one $t\\in \\mathbb R$.\n\nTherefore, the set\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\mathbf{x}_1(t), \\mathbf{x}_2(t) \\right\\}\n$$\n\nis linearly independent on $\\mathbb R$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221154 1069 "e-21181" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221154 1071 "${x_{1}^{′}(t) = 2x_{1}(t) + x_{2}(t); x_{2}^{′}(t) =-4x_{1}(t) - 2x_{2}(t)$.\nConsider the system of linear ODEs above, where $t\\in\\mathbb R$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The function $\\begin{bmatrix}t^{2} + 1 \\\\ -4t\\end{bmatrix}$ is a solution of the system.\n2. The function $\\begin{bmatrix}2t + 1 \\\\ -4t\\end{bmatrix}$ is a solution of the system.\n3. The function $\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 1 - 2t\\end{bmatrix}$ is a solution of the system.\n\nAlso, determine which pair of solutions forms a set of fundamental solutions to the system." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221154 1072 #uuid "79080694-1e56-57e5-8249-9aadbcea4346" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221154 1074 "The Wronskian $W(\\mathbf{x}_1,\\mathbf{x}_2)$ of two vector-valued functions\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x_{11}(t) \\\\ x_{21}(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\nis defined as\n$\\begin{vmatrix}x_{11}(t) & x_{12}(t) \\\\ x_{21}(t) & x_{22}(t)\\end{vmatrix}$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- If the Wronskian is not identically zero, then $\\mathbf{x}_1$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2$ are linearly independent.\n- If the Wronskian is identically zero, and it's known that $\\mathbf{x}_1$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2$ are solutions to the same system of linear ODEs, then $\\mathbf{x}_1$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2$ are linearly dependent.\n\nFirst, we write our system in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute the given vectors into the matrix differential equation:\n\n- $\\begin{bmatrix}t^{2} + 1 \\\\ -4t\\end{bmatrix}$ $\\text{isn't}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}(t^{2} + 1)^{′} \\\\ (-4t)^{′}\\end{bmatrix}|= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}t^{2} + 1 \\\\ -4t\\end{bmatrix}; \\begin{bmatrix}2t \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}|= \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\cdot (t^{2} + 1) + 1 \\cdot (-4t) \\\\ -4 \\cdot (t^{2} + 1) + (-2) \\cdot (-4t)\\end{bmatrix}; \\begin{bmatrix}2t \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}|= \\begin{bmatrix}2t^{2} - 4t + 2 \\\\ -4t^{2} + 8t - 4\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot$\n\n- $\\begin{bmatrix}2t + 1 \\\\ -4t\\end{bmatrix}$ $\\text{is}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}(2t + 1)^{′} \\\\ (-4t)^{′}\\end{bmatrix}|= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2t + 1 \\\\ -4t\\end{bmatrix}; \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}|= \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\cdot (2t + 1) + 1 \\cdot (-4t) \\\\ -4 \\cdot (2t + 1) + (-2) \\cdot (-4t)\\end{bmatrix}; \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}|= \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}✓$\n- $\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 1 - 2t\\end{bmatrix}$ $\\text{is}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}(t)^{′} \\\\ (1 - 2t)^{′}\\end{bmatrix}|= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 1 - 2t\\end{bmatrix}; \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}|= \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\cdot t + 1 \\cdot (1 - 2t) \\\\ -4 \\cdot t + (-2) \\cdot (1 - 2t)\\end{bmatrix}; \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}|= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}✓$\n\nNow, consider the set of solutions $\\big\\{\\mathbf{b},\\mathbf{c} \\big\\}$. Its Wronskian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nW(b, c) &= \\begin{vmatrix}2t + 1 & t \\\\ -4t & 1 - 2t\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= (2t + 1) \\cdot (1 - 2t) - t \\cdot (-4t) \\\\\n&= (2t + 1)(1 - 2t) + 4t^{2} \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is nonzero for at least one $t \\in \\mathbb R$. Thus, since $\\mathbf b$ and $\\mathbf c$ are both solutions of the same linear system, and their Wronskian is nonzero for at least one $t\\in\\mathbb R$, $\\big\\{\\mathbf{b},\\mathbf{c} \\big\\}$ is a set of fundamental solutions of the system." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221155 1069 "e-21195" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221155 1071 "The line $ax + by = -6$ is perpendicular to the line $9y + 2x = 15$. What is the value of $\\dfrac{b}{a}$?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221155 1072 #uuid "7ff7eadc-73b6-56ff-a24a-d68ff20cf936" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221155 1074 "First, we rewrite both lines in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\nby = -ax - 6 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad y = -\\dfrac{a}{b}x - \\dfrac{6}{b}\n$$\n\n$$\n9y = -2x + 15 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad y = -\\dfrac{2}{9}x + \\dfrac{5}{3}\n$$\n\nSo, the slopes of the lines are\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{a}{b}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{2}{9}\n$$\n\nrespectively.\n\nThe slopes of perpendicular lines are negative reciprocals. The reciprocal of\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{2}{9}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{9}{2}\n$$\n\nand changing the sign gives:\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{a}{b} = \\dfrac{9}{2} \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad \\dfrac{b}{a} = -\\dfrac{2}{9}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{b}{a} = -\\dfrac{2}{9}\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221156 1069 "e-21271" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221156 1071 "Find $e^{At}$ for $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of $A$ are $1$ and $4$.*" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221156 1072 #uuid "35a158c3-37b6-5123-ba90-1fd05218ce43" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221156 1074 "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2$. Thus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ne^{At} &= α_{1}At + α_{0}I \\\\\n&= α_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}t + α_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}α_{1}t + α_{0} & 0 \\\\ -3α_{1}t & 4α_{1}t + α_{0}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial\n\n$$\nr(\\lambda) = \\alpha_1 \\lambda + \\alpha_0\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of $At$ are\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=t, \\qquad \\lambda_2=4t\n$$\n\nboth of multiplicity one. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{r(t) = e^{t}; r(4t) = e^{4t}⇒{tα_{1} + α_{0} = e^{t}; 4tα_{1} + α_{0} = e^{4t}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0{:}$\n\n- Subtracting the first equation from the second one, we get\n\n$$\n3t\\alpha_1 = e^{4t} - e^{t} \\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad \\alpha_1 = \\dfrac{1}{3t}\\bigl(e^{4t}-e^{t}\\bigr)\n$$\n\n- Multiplying the first equation by $4$ and subtracting the second one, we get\n\n$$\n3\\alpha_0 = 4e^{t} - e^{4t} \\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad \\alpha_0 = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\bigl(4e^{t}-e^{4t}\\bigr)\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ into the expressions for the entries of $e^{At}$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nα_{1}t + α_{0} &= (\\frac{1}{3t}(e^{4t} - e^{t}))t + \\frac{1}{3}(4e^{t} - e^{4t}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}(e^{4t} - e^{t} + 4e^{t} - e^{4t}) \\\\\n&= e^{t} \\\\\n- 3α_{1}t &= -3(\\frac{1}{3t}(e^{4t} - e^{t}))t \\\\\n&= e^{t} - e^{4t} \\\\\n4α_{1}t + α_{0} &= 4(\\frac{1}{3t}(e^{4t} - e^{t}))t + \\frac{1}{3}(4e^{t} - e^{4t}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}((4e^{4t} - 4e^{t}) + (4e^{t} - e^{4t})) \\\\\n&= e^{4t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n$\\begin{bmatrix}e^{t} & 0 \\\\ e^{t} - e^{4t} & e^{4t}\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221157 1069 "e-21272" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221157 1071 "Find $e^{At}$ for $\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 4 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of $A$ are $3 \\pm 2\\textrm{i}$.*" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221157 1072 #uuid "18064c11-63d3-599a-b504-7b589f46c3dc" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221157 1074 "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2$. Thus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ne^{At} &= α_{1}At + α_{0}I \\\\\n&= α_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 4 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}t + α_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}7α_{1}t + α_{0} & 4α_{1}t \\\\ -5α_{1}t & - α_{1}t + α_{0}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial\n\n$$\nr(\\lambda) = \\alpha_1 \\lambda + \\alpha_0\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues or $At$ are\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1= (3+2\\textrm{i}) t, \\qquad \\lambda_2= (3-2\\textrm{i}) t\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr((3 + 2\\textrm{i})t) &= e^{(3 + 2\\textrm{i})t} \\\\\nα_{1}(3 + 2\\textrm{i})t + α_{0} &= e^{3t}(\\cos (2t) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (2t)) \\\\\n(3α_{1}t + α_{0}) + 2α_{1}\\textrm{i}t &= e^{3t}\\cos (2t) + \\textrm{i}e^{3t}\\sin (2t)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0{:}$\n\n$$\n{3α_{1}t + α_{0} = e^{3t}\\cos (2t); 2tα_{1} = e^{3t}\\sin (2t)⇒{α_{1} = \\frac{1}{2t}e^{3t}\\sin (2t); α_{0} = e^{3t}(\\cos (2t) - \\frac{3}{2}\\sin (2t))\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ into the expressions for the entries of $e^{At}$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n7α_{1}t + α_{0} &= 7 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2t}e^{3t}\\sin (2t) \\cdot t + e^{3t}(\\cos (2t) - \\frac{3}{2}\\sin (2t)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{7}{2}e^{3t}\\sin (2t) + e^{3t}\\cos (2t) - \\frac{3}{2}e^{3t}\\sin (2t) \\\\\n&= e^{3t}(\\cos (2t) + 2\\sin (2t)), \\\\\n4α_{1}t &= 4 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2t}e^{3t}\\sin (2t) \\cdot t \\\\\n&= 2e^{3t}\\sin (2t), \\\\\n- 5α_{1}t &= -5 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2t}e^{3t}\\sin (2t) \\cdot t \\\\\n&=-\\frac{5}{2}e^{3t}\\sin (2t), \\\\\n- α_{1}t + α_{0} &= -\\frac{1}{2t}e^{3t}\\sin (2t) \\cdot t + e^{3t}(\\cos (2t) - \\frac{3}{2}\\sin (2t)) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{2}e^{3t}\\sin (2t) + e^{3t}\\cos (2t) - \\frac{3}{2}e^{3t}\\sin (2t) \\\\\n&= e^{3t}(\\cos (2t) - 2\\sin (2t))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos (2t) + 2\\sin (2t) & 2\\sin (2t) \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2}\\sin (2t) & \\cos (2t) - 2\\sin (2t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221158 1069 "e-21273" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221158 1071 "Find $e^{At}$ for $\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of $A$ is $6$, and it has multiplicity two.*" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221158 1072 #uuid "40a6318a-a1c9-55f3-afdb-02b43891d7b5" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221158 1074 "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2$. Thus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ne^{At} &= α_{1}At + α_{0}I \\\\\n&= α_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}t + α_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}8α_{1}t + α_{0} & -2α_{1}t \\\\ 2α_{1}t & 4α_{1}t + α_{0}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial\n\n$$\nr(\\lambda) = \\alpha_1 \\lambda + \\alpha_0\n$$\n\nThe only eigenvalue of $At$ is\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 = 6t\n$$\n\nof multiplicity two. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{r(6t) = e^{6t}; \\frac{d}{dλ}r(6t) = e^{6t}⇒{6tα_{1} + α_{0} = e^{6t}; α_{1} = e^{6t}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0{:}$\n\n- From the second equation, we get\n$\\alpha_1 = e^{6t}$.\n- Substituting this into the first one, we get\n$6t e^{6t} + \\alpha_0 = e^{6t} \\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad \\alpha_0 = e^{6t}(1-6t)$.\n\nSubstituting $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ into the expressions for the required entries of $e^{At}$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n8α_{1}t + α_{0} &= 8e^{6t}t + e^{6t}(1 - 6t) \\\\\n&= e^{6t}(1 + 2t), \\\\\n- 2α_{1}t &= -2e^{6t}t \\\\\n&= e^{6t}(-2t), \\\\\n2α_{1}t &= 2e^{6t}t \\\\\n&= e^{6t}(2t), \\\\\n4α_{1}t + α_{0} &= 4e^{6t}t + e^{6t}(1 - 6t) \\\\\n&= e^{6t}(1 - 2t)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 + 2t & -2t \\\\ 2t & 1 - 2t\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221159 1069 "e-21312" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221159 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y' = 2x^2+3xy, \\qquad y(0)=-1$\nThe **implicit** Euler method is applied with step size $\\Delta x = 1$.\nFind the general formula for the new $y$ value, $y_\\text{new}$, in terms of the new value $x_\\text{new}$ and the current value $y$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221159 1072 #uuid "c3550cab-110a-5344-be19-d183b55bc098" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221159 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y_0 = y(x_0)\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = y'_\\text{new} \\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\ny'_\\text{new} = f(x_\\text{new},y_\\text{new})\n$$\n\nevaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn general, $\\Delta y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy &= y_{new}^{′} \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= (2x_{new}^{2} + 3x_{new}y_{new}) \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 2x_{new}^{2} + 3x_{new}y_{new}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y\n$$\n\nand solve for the general formula for $y_\\text{new}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{new} &= y + Δy \\\\\ny_{new} &= y + 2x_{new}^{2} + 3x_{new}y_{new} \\\\\ny_{new} - 3x_{new}y_{new} &= y + 2x_{new}^{2} \\\\\ny_{new}(1 - 3x_{new}) &= y + 2x_{new}^{2} \\\\\ny_{new} &= \\frac{y + 2x_{new}^{2}}{1 - 3x_{new}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221160 1069 "e-21318" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221160 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y' = x^2y + e^x, \\qquad y(0)=-1$\nUse the **implicit** Euler method with one step to approximate $y\\left(\\dfrac12\\right)$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221160 1072 #uuid "503c762b-0313-598a-a80c-c53d14ec250a" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221160 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y_0 = y(x_0)\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = y'_\\text{new} \\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\ny'_\\text{new} = f(x_\\text{new},y_\\text{new})\n$$\n\nevaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac12\n$$\n\nwe will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac12 - 0 = \\dfrac12\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In our case,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy &= y_{new}^{′} \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= (x_{new}^{2}y_{new} + e^{x_{new}}) \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(x_{new}^{2}y_{new} + e^{x_{new}})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y\n$$\n\nand solve for the general formula for $y_\\text{new}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{new} &= y + Δy \\\\\ny_{new} &= y + \\frac{1}{2}(x_{new}^{2}y_{new} + e^{x_{new}})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{new} - \\frac{1}{2}x_{new}^{2}y_{new} &= y + \\frac{1}{2}e^{x_{new}} \\\\\n(1 - \\frac{1}{2}x_{new}^{2})y_{new} &= y + \\frac{1}{2}e^{x_{new}} \\\\\ny_{new} &= \\frac{y + \\frac{1}{2}e^{x_{new}}}{1 - \\frac{1}{2}x_{new}^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is\n\n$$\nx_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x = 0 + \\dfrac12 = \\dfrac12\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{new} &= \\frac{y + \\frac{1}{2}e^{x_{new}}}{1 - \\frac{1}{2}x_{new}^{2}} \\\\\n&= (-1 + \\frac{1}{2}e^{1/2})/(1 - \\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{2})^{2}) \\\\\n&= (4(\\sqrt{e} - 2))/(7)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny\\left(\\dfrac12\\right) = \\dfrac{4}{7} \\left(\\sqrt{e} - 2\\right)\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221161 1069 "e-21424" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221161 1071 "Given that $\\mathcal{L} \\left\\{t\\sin(\\omega t) \\right\\}=\\dfrac{2\\omega s}{(s^2+\\omega^2)^2}$, find the Laplace transform $\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{e^{-5t}\\cdot t\\sin(2t)\\right\\}$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221161 1072 #uuid "fe6b394f-a130-5746-a5c9-0fbbee020554" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221161 1074 "The first shifting theorem states that if\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{f(t)\\right\\} = F(s)\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{e^{at}f(t)\\right\\} = F(s-a)\n$$\n\nUsing the given result, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{t\\sin(2t) \\right\\} = \\frac{2(2)s}{(s^{2}+2^2)^{2}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the first shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{e^{-5t} \\cdot t\\sin (2t)} &= (2(2)(s + 5))/(((s + 5)^{2} + 2^{2})^{2}) \\\\\n&= (4(s + 5))/(((s + 5)^{2} + 4)^{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221162 1069 "e-21445" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221162 1071 "Given that $\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{t^n\\big\\}=\\dfrac{n!}{s^{n+1}}$ for $s>0$, find $\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{u(t-2)\\big(t^2+3t+2\\big) \\right\\}$, where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221162 1072 #uuid "41b1a655-6092-57b6-8f95-396c566e7a83" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221162 1074 "The second shifting theorem states that if\n\n$$\na \\ge 0\n$$\n\nand $\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\big\\}$ exists for $s > s_0$, then\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\big\\} = e^{-as}\\,\\mathcal{L}\\big\\{g(t+a)\\big\\}, \\qquad \\text{for} \\; s > s_0\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{u(t-2)\\big(t^2+3t+2\\big) \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=2$, and we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{u(t-2)\\,g(t)\\big\\} = e^{-2s}\\,\\mathcal{L}\\big\\{g(t+2)\\big\\}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\ng(t)=t^2+3t+2\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(t + 2) &= (t + 2)^{2} + 3(t + 2) + 2 \\\\\n&= t^{2} + 4t + 4 + 3t + 6 + 2 \\\\\n&= t^{2} + 7t + 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{t^n\\big\\}=\\dfrac{n!}{s^{n+1}}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L} \\{1\\} = \\dfrac{1}{s}\n$$\n\nfor $s>0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{g(t + 2)} &= L{t^{2} + 7t + 12} \\\\\n&= L{t^{2}} + 7L{t} + 12L{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{s^{3}} + 7 \\cdot \\frac{1}{s^{2}} + 12 \\cdot \\frac{1}{s} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{s^{3}} + \\frac{7}{s^{2}} + \\frac{12}{s}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{u(t - 2)g(t)} &= e^{-2s}L{g(t + 2)} \\\\\n&= e^{-2s}(\\frac{2}{s^{3}} + \\frac{7}{s^{2}} + \\frac{12}{s})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221163 1069 "e-21503" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221163 1071 "Given that $\\mathcal{L}\\{\\cos(\\omega t)\\} = \\dfrac{s}{s^2+\\omega^2}$ for $s>0$, find the Laplace transform of\n$\\displaystyle g(t)= \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{t} \\cos\\big(\\pi\\tau\\big) \\, \\textrm{d}\\tau, \\quad s>0$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221163 1072 #uuid "11b5421d-e98b-5e37-9bdd-bec13f010118" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221163 1074 "We know that if\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\} = F(s)\n$$\n\n, then\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{\\int_{0}^{t} f(\\tau) \\, \\text{d}\\tau \\right\\} = \\frac{F(s)}{s}\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nf(t) = \\cos(\\pi t)\n$$\n\nThus, for $s > 0$,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(s) &= L{f(t)} \\\\\n&= L{\\cos (πt)} \\\\\n&= \\frac{s}{s^{2} + π^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the integral identity above, we have that for $s > 0$,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{g(t)} &= L{\\int_{0}^{t}\\cos (πτ)dτ} \\\\\n&= (F(s))/(s) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{s^{2} + π^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221164 1069 "e-21527" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221164 1071 "For the function\n$G(s) = \\dfrac{1}{s(s-1)}, \\qquad s > 1$\nfind the inverse Laplace transform of $G(s)$, given that\n$\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{at}\\}=\\dfrac{1}{s-a},\\ s>a$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221164 1072 #uuid "58ed1920-cd4f-5993-b3bf-1fa4e43492af" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221164 1074 "Notice that we can write $G(s)$ as\n\n$$\nG(s) = \\frac{1}{s(s-1)} = \\frac{F(s)}{s}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\dfrac{1}{s-1}\n$$\n\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{at}\\}=\\frac{1}{s-a}\n$$\n\nThus, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL^{-1}{F(s)} &= L^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s - 1}} \\\\\n&= e^{t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we know that if\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\} = F(s)\n$$\n\n, then\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{\\int_{0}^{t} f(\\tau)\\, \\text{d}\\tau \\right\\} = \\frac{F(s)}{s}\n$$\n\nUsing the property above in reverse, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL^{-1}{G(s)} &= L^{-1}{(F(s))/(s)} \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{t}f(τ)dτ \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{t}e^{τ}dτ \\\\\n&= \\left.e^{τ}\\right|_{0}^{t} \\\\\n&= e^{t} - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221165 1069 "e-21531" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221165 1071 "Consider a function $f(t)$ such that its Laplace transform is $\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\} = F(s)$ for $s>s_0$. Suppose we wish to find $\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{-7t} f(t)\\}$. Show the steps to compute this transform." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221165 1072 #uuid "04233a8a-069f-5649-b198-c6bbf5b96742" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221165 1074 "Starting from the definition of the Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{e^{-7t}f(t)} &= \\int_{0}^{∞}e^{-st} \\cdot e^{-7t}f(t)dt \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{∞}e^{- (s + 7)t}f(t)dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle F(s) = \\int_0^{\\infty} e^{-st} \\, f(t)\\, \\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nwhere $s > s_0$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{\\infty} e^{-(s+7)t} \\, f(t)\\, \\textrm{d}t = F(s+7)\n$$\n\nwhere\n$s + 7 > s_0 \\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad s > s_0 - 7$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221166 1069 "e-21538" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221166 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y' = -x - y, \\qquad y\\left(-1\\right) = 0$\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. Given that the step size is $\\Delta x = 1$, and that one preliminary step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, complete the next rows of the table below.\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n$\\Delta y = \\dfrac1{12}(5y'_p + 8y' - y'_{-1}) \\cdot \\Delta x$\n*where $y_p$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and $y'_{-1}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.*\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{}$ |\n| $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{}$ |  |  |  |  |  |" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221166 1072 #uuid "14669099-db40-5dd2-b4d5-4db016374643" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221166 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) = c\n$$\n\nthe $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM2) method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}Predictor &: & Δy_{p} &= \\frac{1}{2}(3y^{′} - y_{-1}^{′}) \\cdot Δx, \\\\ Corrector &: & Δy|= \\frac{1}{12}(5y_{p}^{′} + 8y^{′} - y_{-1}^{′}) \\cdot Δx\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $y' = f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y)$,\n- $y'_{-1} = f(x_{-1}, y_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point ($x_{-1}, y_{-1})$, and\n- $y'_p = f(x_\\text{new}, y_p)$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $(x_\\text{new}, y_p) = (x + \\Delta x, y + \\Delta y_p)$.\n\nWe're told that the first step is calculated using the Runge-Kutta Method, resulting in\n\n$$\ny\\left(0\\right) \\approx \\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nNow, we'll continue from this point, using the $2$-step ABM method.\n\nFirst, we compute the value of $y'$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= -x - y \\\\\n&=-0 - \\frac{1}{4} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{1}{4}}$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nFrom the first row of the table, we see that the slope at the previous point\n\n$$\n(x_{-1}, y_{-1}) = \\left(-1, 0\\right)\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\ny'_{-1} = 1\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $\\Delta y_p$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy_{p} &= \\frac{1}{2}(3y^{′} - y_{-1}^{′}) \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(3 \\cdot (-\\frac{1}{4}) - 1) \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&=-\\frac{7}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{1}{4}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{7}{8}}$ |  |  |  |\n\nSo, the coordinates of the predicted new point are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{new} &= x + Δx = 0 + 1 = 1, \\\\\ny_{p} &= y + Δy_{p} = \\frac{1}{4} + (-\\frac{7}{8}) =-\\frac{5}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{1}{4}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{7}{8}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{5}{8}}$ |  |  |\n\nHence, the slope at this predicted point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{p}^{′} &= -x_{new} - y_{p} \\\\\n&=-1 - (-\\frac{5}{8}) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{3}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{1}{4}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{7}{8}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{5}{8}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{3}{8}}$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $\\Delta y$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy &= \\frac{1}{12}(5y_{p}^{′} + 8y^{′} - y_{-1}^{′}) \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{12}(5 \\cdot (-\\frac{3}{8}) + 8 \\cdot (-\\frac{1}{4}) - 1) \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{12} \\cdot (-\\frac{39}{8}) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{13}{32}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{1}{4}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{7}{8}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{5}{8}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{3}{8}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{13}{32}}$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is\n\n$$\nx_\\text{new} = 1\n$$\n\nTo get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $\\Delta y$ to $y{:}$\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y = \\dfrac{1}{4} + \\left(-\\dfrac{13}{32}\\right) = -\\dfrac{5}{32}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{1}{4}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{7}{8}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{5}{8}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{3}{8}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{13}{32}}$ |\n| $1$ | $-\\dfrac{5}{32}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(1) \\approx \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{5}{32}}\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221167 1069 "e-21539" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221167 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y' = 2x - 2y + 1, \\qquad y(0) = 0$\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. Given that the step size is $\\Delta x = \\dfrac14$, and that the first step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, fill the missing entries in the table below, where $y_p$ denotes the predictor at each step.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |  |  |  |" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221167 1072 #uuid "c736760f-414e-5ab2-adfa-c09acce10d5e" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221167 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) = c\n$$\n\nthe predictor equation of the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method (ABM2) with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y_p = \\dfrac12(3y' - y'_{-1}) \\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $y' = f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y)$, and\n- $y'_{-1} = f(x_{-1}, y_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point $(x_{-1}, y_{-1})$.\n\nThe initially predicted new $y$-value is then given by\n\n$$\ny_p = y + \\Delta y_p\n$$\n\nWe're told that the first step is calculated using the Runge-Kutta Method, resulting in\n\n$$\ny\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right) \\approx \\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nNow, we'll continue from this point, using the ABM $2$-step method.\n\nFirst, we compute the value of $y'$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= 2x - 2y + 1 \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot (\\frac{1}{4}) - 2 \\cdot (\\frac{1}{4}) + 1 \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{1}$ |  |  |\n\nFrom the table, we see that the slope at the previous point\n\n$$\n(x_{-1}, y_{-1}) = \\left(0, 0\\right)\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\ny'_{-1} = 1\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $\\Delta y_p$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy_{p} &= \\frac{1}{2}(3y^{′} - y_{-1}^{′}) \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(3 \\cdot 1 - 1) \\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{8} \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this value to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\dfrac14$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{1}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{\\dfrac{1}{4}}$ |  |\n\nHence, according to the predictor equation, the initially predicted new $y$-value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{p} &= y + Δy_{p} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4} + \\frac{1}{4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\dfrac14$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{1}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{\\dfrac{1}{4}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{\\dfrac{1}{2}}$ |" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221168 1069 "e-21549" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221168 1071 "Consider a function $f(t)$ for which the Laplace transform is $\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\} = F(s)$. How can we obtain the graph of $\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{-6t} f(t)\\}$ from the graph of $F(s)$?" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221168 1072 #uuid "1e5e6e3a-306b-5e8c-a8ba-baa0d63a1813" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221168 1074 "The first shifting theorem states that if\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{f(t)\\right\\} = F(s)\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{e^{at}f(t)\\right\\} = F(s-a)\n$$\n\nThis implies that multiplying a function $f(t)$ by $e^{at}$ shifts the corresponding Laplace transform horizontally along the $s$-axis.\n\n- If $a > 0$, the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the right.\n- If $a < 0$, the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the left.\n\nIn our case, to get the graph of $\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{-6t} f(t)\\}$, we must shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $6$ units to the left." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221169 1069 "e-21551" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221169 1071 "Prove that if $\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\} = F(s)$, then\n$\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{t} f(\\tau)\\, d\\tau \\right\\} = \\frac{F(s)}{s}$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221169 1072 #uuid "602b037e-28bf-5afd-a466-652668bf08c6" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221169 1074 "Let $A(t)$ be the anti-derivative of $f(t)$ such that\n\n$$\nA'(t) = f(t)\n$$\n\nand $A(0) ={0}$. Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{t}f(τ)dτ &= \\left.A(τ)\\right|_{0}^{t} \\\\\n&= A(t) - A(0) \\\\\n&= A(t)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy definition of the Laplace transform, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{\\int_{0}^{t}f(τ)dτ} &= L{A(t)} = \\int_{0}^{∞}e^{-st}A(t)dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's use integration by parts:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}u = A(t) & ⇒ & du = A^{′}(t)dt \\\\ dv = e^{-st}dt & ⇒ & v =-\\frac{1}{s}e^{-st}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{∞}e^{-st}A(t)dt &= \\left.A(t)(-\\frac{1}{s}e^{-st})\\right|_{0}^{∞} - \\int_{0}^{∞}(-\\frac{1}{s}e^{-st})A^{′}(t)dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A(0)=0$ and\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{s}e^{-st} \\to 0\n$$\n\nas $t \\to \\infty$, we have\n\n$$\n\\left.-\\dfrac{1}{s}A(t)e^{-st}\\bigg\\right|_0^{\\infty} = {0}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{\\infty} e^{-st}A(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t = \\dfrac{1}{s}\\int_0^{\\infty}{e^{-st} A'(t)} \\,\\mathrm{d}t\n$$\n\nFinally, using that\n\n$$\nA'(t) = f(t)\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{s}\\int_{0}^{∞}e^{-st}A^{′}(t)dt &= \\frac{1}{s}\\int_{0}^{∞}e^{-st}f(t)dt \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{s} \\cdot F(s) \\\\\n&= (F(s))/(s)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221170 1069 "e-21565" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221170 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y' = x + y + 1, \\qquad y\\left(-2\\right) = 0$\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. Given that the step size is $\\Delta x = \\dfrac14$, and that one preliminary step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, complete the second row of the table below.\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n$\\Delta y = \\dfrac1{12}(5y'_p + 8y' - y'_{-1}) \\cdot \\Delta x$\n*where $y_p$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and $y'_{-1}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.*\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $-2$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $-\\dfrac74$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $-1$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{2}$ | $-1$ |  |" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221170 1072 #uuid "a185d6d7-e351-5f24-891a-aeb318cef584" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221170 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) = c\n$$\n\nthe $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM2) method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}Predictor &: & Δy_{p} &= \\frac{1}{2}(3y^{′} - y_{-1}^{′}) \\cdot Δx, \\\\ Corrector &: & Δy|= \\frac{1}{12}(5y_{p}^{′} + 8y^{′} - y_{-1}^{′}) \\cdot Δx\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $y' = f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y)$,\n- $y'_{-1} = f(x_{-1}, y_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point ($x_{-1}, y_{-1})$, and\n- $y'_p = f(x_\\text{new}, y_p)$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $(x_\\text{new}, y_p) = (x + \\Delta x, y + \\Delta y_p)$.\n\nLooking at the partially filled table, we note the following:\n\n- From the second row, the slope at the current point $(x, y) = \\left(-\\dfrac74, -\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)$ is $y' = -1$.\n- From the first row, the slope at the previous point $(x_{-1}, y_{-1}) = \\left(-2, 0\\right)$ is $y'_{-1} = -1$.\n- From the second row, the slope at the predicted new point $(x_\\text{new}, y_p)$ is $y'_p = -1$.\n\nNow, we compute $\\Delta y$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy &= \\frac{1}{12}(5y_{p}^{′} + 8y^{′} - y_{-1}^{′}) \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{12}(5 \\cdot (-1) + 8 \\cdot (-1) - (-1)) \\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{48} \\cdot (-12) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $-2$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $-\\dfrac74$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $-1$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{2}$ | $-1$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{1}{4}}$ |" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221171 1069 "e-21566" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221171 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y' = x - 2y + 6, \\qquad y(0) = 2$\nTo approximate the solution, one step of the Runge-Kutta method has already been computed, resulting in the following estimate:\n$y(1) \\approx 3$\nUse the $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton Method with one step to approximate $y(2)$.\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n$\\Delta y = \\dfrac1{12}(5y'_p + 8y' - y'_{-1}) \\cdot \\Delta x$\n*where $y_p$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and $y'_{-1}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.*" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221171 1072 #uuid "ab9a026d-ddb2-52e8-9f52-4fbcf9b482a6" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221171 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) = c\n$$\n\nthe $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM $2$-step) Method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}Predictor &: & Δy_{p} &= \\frac{1}{2}(3y^{′} - y_{-1}^{′}) \\cdot Δx, \\\\ Corrector &: & Δy|= \\frac{1}{12}(5y_{p}^{′} + 8y^{′} - y_{-1}^{′}) \\cdot Δx\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $y' = f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y)$,\n- $y'_{-1} = f(x_{-1}, y_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point ($x_{-1}, y_{-1})$, and\n- $y'_p = f(x_\\text{new}, y_p)$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $(x_\\text{new}, y_p) = (x + \\Delta x, y + \\Delta y_p)$.\n\nBefore we start, let's add the initial condition data and Runge-Kutta step data to a table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $2$ |  | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $3$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2$, we will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = 2 - 1 = 1\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the values of $y'$ at each point according to the given rule:\n\n- The slope at the previous point $(x_{-1}, y_{-1}) = (0,2)$ is\n$y_{-1}^{′}|= x_{-1} - 2y_{-1} + 6; = 0 - 2 \\cdot 2 + 6; = 2$.\n- The slope at the current point $(x, y) = (1,3)$ is\n$y^{′}|= x - 2y + 6; = 1 - 2 \\cdot 3 + 6; = 1$.\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $1$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $\\Delta y_p$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy_{p} &= \\frac{1}{2}(3y^{′} - y_{-1}^{′}) \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(3 \\cdot 1 - 2) \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac12$ |  |  |  |\n\nSo, the coordinates of the predicted new point are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{new} &= x + Δx = 1 + 1 = 2, \\\\\ny_{p} &= y + Δy_{p} = 3 + \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{7}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac72$ |  |  |\n\nHence, the slope at this predicted point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{p}^{′} &= x_{new} - 2y_{p} + 6 \\\\\n&= 2 - 2 \\cdot \\frac{7}{2} + 6 \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac72$ | $1$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $\\Delta y$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy &= \\frac{1}{12}(5y_{p}^{′} + 8y^{′} - y_{-1}^{′}) \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{12}(5 \\cdot 1 + 8 \\cdot 1 - 2) \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{12} \\cdot 11 \\\\\n&= \\frac{11}{12}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac72$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac{11}{12}$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is\n\n$$\nx_\\text{new}=2\n$$\n\nTo get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $\\Delta y$ to $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{new} &= y + Δy \\\\\n&= 3 + \\frac{11}{12} \\\\\n&= \\frac{47}{12}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac72$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac{11}{12}$ |\n| $2$ | $\\dfrac{47}{12}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(2)\\approx\\dfrac{47}{12}\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221172 1069 "e-21582" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221172 1071 "Given that\n$\\begin{bmatrix}L{\\sin ωt}|= \\frac{ω}{s^{2} + ω^{2}}, & s > 0, \\\\ L{\\cos ωt}|= \\frac{s}{s^{2} + ω^{2}}, & s > 0\\end{bmatrix}$,\nfind $\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{u(t-4)\\,\\cos(9\\pi t) \\right\\}$, where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221172 1072 #uuid "88ee94b3-1f6e-587a-a63f-736f9df7186e" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221172 1074 "The second shifting theorem states that if\n\n$$\na \\ge 0\n$$\n\nand $\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\big\\}$ exists for $s > s_0$, then\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\big\\} = e^{-as}\\,\\mathcal{L}\\big\\{g(t+a)\\big\\}, \\qquad \\text{for} \\; s > s_0\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{u(t -4)\\,\\cos(9\\pi t) \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=4$, and we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{u(t-4)\\,g(t)\\big\\} = e^{-4s}\\,\\mathcal{L}\\big\\{g(t+4)\\big\\}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\ng(t)= \\cos(9\\pi t)\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(t + 4) &= \\cos(9π(t + 4)) \\\\\n&= \\cos (9πt + 36π) \\\\\n&= \\cos 9πt\\cos 36π - \\sin 9πt\\sin 36π \\\\\n&= \\cos 9πt \\cdot 1 - \\sin 9πt \\cdot 0 \\\\\n&= \\cos 9πt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L} \\left\\{\\cos(\\omega t)\\right\\}=\\dfrac{s}{s^2 + \\omega^2}\n$$\n\nfor $s>0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{g(t + 4)} &= L{\\cos (9πt)} \\\\\n&= \\frac{s}{s^{2} + 81π^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{u(t - 4)g(t)} &= e^{-4s}L{g(t + 4)} \\\\\n&= e^{-4s} \\cdot \\frac{s}{s^{2} + 81π^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221173 1069 "e-21590" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221173 1071 "Given that\n$L{t^{n}}|= \\frac{n!}{s^{n + 1}}, \\mid s > 0$,\nuse linearity of Laplace transforms and the first shifting theorem to find the Laplace transform of $g(t) = 2t^2 + 5te^{4t}$ for $s > 4$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221173 1072 #uuid "05394517-ee39-5b77-9f84-b439d907dd3e" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221173 1074 "First, we apply the linearity properties of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{g(t)} &= L{2t^{2} + 5te^{4t}} \\\\\n&= L{2t^{2}} + L{5te^{4t}} \\\\\n&= 2L{t^{2}} + 5L{te^{4t}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that, for $s > 0$,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{t^{n}} &= \\frac{n!}{s^{n + 1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{t} &= \\frac{1}{s^{2}} \\\\\nL{t^{2}} &= \\frac{2}{s^{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first shifting theorem states that if\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{f(t)\\right\\} = F(s)\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{e^{at}f(t)\\right\\} = F(s-a)\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{te^{4t} \\right\\} = \\dfrac{1}{(s-4)^2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $g(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{g(t)} &= 2L{t^{2}} + 5L{te^{4t}} \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot \\frac{2}{s^{3}} + 5 \\cdot (1)/((s - 4)^{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{s^{3}} + (5)/((s - 4)^{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis transform is valid for $s>4$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221174 1069 "e-21646" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221174 1071 "Suppose that $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty)$ and its Laplace transform\n$F(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\}$\nexists for $s > a$. Then, $F(s)$ is twice differentiable at every point $s > a$, and we have\n$F''(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\{t^2\\cdot f(t) \\}$.\n\nA proof of this statement is given below.\n\n> $\\textrm{L1}{:}\\:$ $F(s) = \\displaystyle \\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} f(t)\\,\\textrm{d}t$ exists for $s > a$.\n> $\\textrm{L2}{:}\\:$ $F''(s) = \\displaystyle \\frac{\\textrm{d}^2}{\\textrm{d}s^2}\\displaystyle \\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} f(t)\\,\\textrm{d}t$\n> $\\textrm{L3}{:}\\:$ $F''(s) = \\displaystyle \\int_0^\\infty \\frac{\\partial^2}{\\partial s^2}\\big(e^{-st} f(t)\\big)\\,\\textrm{d}t$\n> $\\textrm{L4}{:}\\:$ $\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial^2}{\\partial s^2}\\big(e^{-st} f(t)\\big) = t^2 e^{-st} f(t)$\n> $\\textrm{L5}{:}\\:$ $F''(s) = \\displaystyle \\int_0^\\infty t^2 e^{-st} f(t)\\,\\textrm{d}t$\n> $\\textrm{L6}{:}\\:$ $F''(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\{t^2\\cdot f(t)\\}$\n\nExplain the reasoning behind:\n\n- How line $\\textrm{L2}$ follows from line $\\textrm{L1}$.\n- How line $\\textrm{L4}$ follows from its preceding line.\n- How line $\\textrm{L6}$ follows from $\\textrm{L5}$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221174 1072 #uuid "f0be4adc-84af-538c-a6b6-ae0716e66cb3" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221174 1074 "If a function $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty)$ and has exponential order $a$, then its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\}\n$$\n\nexists for every real $s > a$, and $F$ is infinitely differentiable in this region of convergence.\n\nThe smoothness property states that, for each integer\n\n$$\nn\\geq 0\n$$\n\nand $s > a$, we have\n\n$$\nF^{(n)}(s) = (-1)^n\\, \\mathcal{L}\\{t^n f(t) \\}\n$$\n\nWe're asked to prove the smoothness property in the case $n = 2$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- We first consider line $\\textrm{L2}$. From line $\\textrm{L1}$ we know that\n$F(s) = \\displaystyle \\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} f(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t$.\nBy the definition of the second derivative with respect to $s$, we get\n$F'(s) = \\frac{\\mathrm{d}^2}{\\mathrm{d}s^2} F(s) = \\frac{\\mathrm{d}^2}{\\mathrm{d}s^2}\\displaystyle \\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} f(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t$,\nwhich is exactly the statement in line $\\textrm{L2}$.\n- We now consider line $\\textrm{L4}$. The function $f(t)$ does not depend on $s$, so\n$\\frac{\\partial^2}{\\partial s^2}\\big(e^{-st} f(t)\\big) = f(t)\\,\\frac{\\partial^2}{\\partial s^2}(e^{-st}) = f(t)\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial s}(-te^{-st}) = f(t)\\,(t^2 e^{-st}) = t^2 e^{-st} f(t)$.\nThus line $\\textrm{L4}$ follows from the chain rule.\n- Finally, we consider line $\\textrm{L6}$. By the definition of the Laplace transform,\n$\\mathcal{L}\\{t^2\\cdot f(t)\\} = \\displaystyle \\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} \\big(t^2 f(t)\\big)\\,\\mathrm{d}t$.\nComparing this with the integral in line $\\textrm{L5}$, we see that\n$F''(s) = \\displaystyle \\int_0^\\infty t^2 e^{-st} f(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t = \\displaystyle \\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} \\big(t^2 f(t)\\big)\\,\\mathrm{d}t = \\mathcal{L}\\{t^2\\cdot f(t)\\}$,\nwhich is exactly the expression in line $\\textrm{L6}$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221175 1069 "e-21650" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221175 1071 "${x^{′}(t) =-2x(t) + 6y(t); y^{′}(t) = x(t) - 3y(t)$\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_1=0$ and $\\lambda_2=-5$, with corresponding eigenvectors are $\\mathbf{v}_1 = [3, \\ 1]^T$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2 = [-2, \\ 1]^T$, respectively. Using this information, sketch the phase portrait of the system." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221175 1072 #uuid "6649e376-1865-5cb9-8892-687f80082d22" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221175 1074 "The general solution of the system is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nLet's now sketch the phase portrait.\n\n- First, notice that $\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ are the equilibrium solutions of the system since $\\mathbf{x}'_E(t) = \\mathbf{0}$. These solutions form the line with the equation $y=\\dfrac13x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/c3/c39f92243806a8682701374244fa2f7def32ab56ae509c920a31e75abfacb88f.png)\n\n- Now, the line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation\n$y=-\\dfrac12x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line. Since $e^{-5t} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty$, these solutions approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty$.\n\n![](images/96/96a198c7fe3d98d3775ffc176b6659022b18676caf849cbc8d5c66833113bb19.png)\n\nAll other non-equilibrium solutions are parallel to the vector $\\mathbf{v}_2$. So, the phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/74/74b573da705aafba25c74aa789dc3a51464683dd5a47a999ba9c557d35ed1d02.png)" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221176 1069 "e-21651" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221176 1071 "${x^{′}(t) =-3x(t) + 9y(t); y^{′}(t) =-x(t) + 3y(t)$\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue $\\lambda=0$ of the system's matrix $A$ is $\\mathbf{v} = [3, \\ 1]^T$. The vector $\\mathbf{w} = [-1, \\ 0]^T$ is such that $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}$. Sketch the phase portrait of the system." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221176 1072 #uuid "e89c64cb-e046-592c-929b-9269cb7b2758" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221176 1074 "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda=0$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $\\mathbf{v}$, the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1\\mathbf{v} e^{\\lambda t} + c_2 (\\mathbf{v} te^{\\lambda t} + \\mathbf{w}e^{\\lambda t} \\,)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{w}$ is a solution to the equation\n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} &= c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} + c_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t + \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\\n&= c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}3t - 1 \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix}, c_{1}, c_{2} ∈ R\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ are equilibrium solutions of the system since\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'_E(t) = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nThese solutions form the line with the equation\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac13x\n$$\n\nin the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/bf/bfc7b00f7f63aec120179da3f89f72f0aa53f13d299c95ed8f53e6238d329e51.png)\n\nNow, we consider some general solutions of the form\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\nFor example:\n\n- When $c_1=1$ and $c_2=1$, we get the following curve:\n${x = 3t + 2; y = t + 1$\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/55/55dfdfce480deba74fe46c1634e4c7ffb711fbd617525cdb5387bdabfc83ae75.png)\n\n- When $c_1=-1$ and $c_2=-1$, we get the following curve:\n${x =-3t - 2; y =-t - 1$\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/2f/2fb2297c741ea20ca729dcacb9934d7f8d1cca58af93c2b75db177890934b2b6.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/9e/9e7970ba2d6bbe4745ee498cdbc72f2b7d8689219291847644bd6483696cc456.png)" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221177 1069 "e-21654" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221177 1071 "${x^{′}(t) = 2x(t) + 2y(t); y^{′}(t) = 5x(t) + 5y(t)$\nFind an equation of the line in the phase space that contains all equilibrium points of the system above." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221177 1072 #uuid "edb1633f-44e1-5324-8048-80252c65afe5" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221177 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$\nwhere $\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe equilibrium (fixed) points of a system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = \\mathbf{f}(x,y)\n$$\n\nare the points for which\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f}(x,y)=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{2x + 2y = 0; 5x + 5y = 0\n$$\n\nNotice that the second equation is a multiple of the first one. So, we can disregard the second equation.\n\nFrom the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n2x+2y=0 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad 2y=-2x \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad y=-x\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThis determines the line with the equation $y=-x$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221178 1069 "e-21661" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221178 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y' = 4x^2 - y, \\qquad y(0)=0$\nUse the **implicit** Euler method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y\\left(\\dfrac12\\right)$ and $y(1)$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221178 1072 #uuid "15820b47-eda7-55a7-9027-3a41831c71dc" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221178 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y_0 = y(x_0)\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = y'_\\text{new} \\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\ny'_\\text{new} = f(x_\\text{new},y_\\text{new})\n$$\n\nevaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(1)$. The distance from $x=0$ to $x=1$ is $1$, and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{1-0}2 = \\dfrac12\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $\\Delta y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy &= y_{new}^{′} \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= (4(x_{new})^{2} - y_{new}) \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n&= 2(x_{new})^{2} - \\frac{1}{2}y_{new}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y\n$$\n\nand solve for the general formula for $y_\\text{new}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{new} &= y + Δy \\\\\ny_{new} &= y + 2(x_{new})^{2} - \\frac{1}{2}y_{new} \\\\\n\\frac{3}{2}y_{new} &= y + 2(x_{new})^{2} \\\\\ny_{new} &= (2(y + 2(x_{new})^{2}))/(3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of\n\n$$\ny\\left(\\dfrac12\\right)\n$$\n\nand $y(1)$.\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx = 0,\\qquad y = 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is\n\n$$\nx_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x = 0 + \\dfrac12 = \\dfrac12\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{new} &= (2(y + 2(x_{new})^{2}))/(3) \\\\\n&= (2(0 + 2(\\frac{1}{2})^{2}))/(3) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac12, \\qquad y = \\dfrac13\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is\n\n$$\nx_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x = \\dfrac12 + \\dfrac12 = 1\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{new} &= (2(y + 2(x_{new})^{2}))/(3) \\\\\n&= (2(\\frac{1}{3} + 2 \\cdot 1^{2}))/(3) \\\\\n&= \\frac{14}{9}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny\\left(\\dfrac12\\right)\\approx\\dfrac{1}{3}, \\qquad y(1)\\approx\\dfrac{14}{9}\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221179 1069 "e-21664" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221179 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y'' + 4xy' - 3y = 2x, \\qquad y(0) = 1, \\quad y'(0) = -2$\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method by introducing $z = y'$ and reducing the equation to a system of first-order ODEs. What entries should be placed in the columns for $\\Delta y$ and $\\Delta z$ in the table below if the step size is $\\Delta x = 1$?\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nUse the information above to approximate the value of $y(1)$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221179 1072 #uuid "81a8347f-7852-5413-8d96-946ec658929e" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221179 1074 "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\ny'' = f(x,y,y'), \\qquad y(a) = c, \\quad y'(a) = d\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{y^{′} = z,\\begin{vmatrix}y(a) = c, \\\\ z^{′} = f(x, y, z)\\end{vmatrix}z(a) = d\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate $y''$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{″} + 4xy^{′} - 3y &= 2x \\\\\ny^{″} &= -4xy^{′} + 3y + 2x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable\n\n$$\nz = y', z' = y''\n$$\n\nin the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\nz' = -4xz + 3y + 2x\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is\n\n$$\nz(0) = y'(0) = -2\n$$\n\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{y^{′} = z,\\begin{vmatrix}y(0) = 1, \\\\ z^{′} =-4xz + 3y + 2x,\\end{vmatrix}z(0) =-2\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nFirst, because the initial conditions are $y(0)=1$ and $z(0)=-2$, we place $y=1$ and $z=-2$ in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  | $-2$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both $y'$ and $z'$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= z =-2 \\\\\nz^{′} &= -4xz + 3y + 2x \\\\\n&=-4 \\cdot 0 \\cdot (-2) + 3 \\cdot 1 + 2 \\cdot 0 \\\\\n&= 0 + 3 + 0 \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ |  | $-2$ | $3$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $\\Delta y$ and $\\Delta z$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy &= y^{′} \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= (-2) \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&=-2 \\\\\nΔz &= z^{′} \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $3$ | $3$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x$, $y$, and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $\\Delta x$ to $x$, $\\Delta y$ to $y$, and $\\Delta z$ to $z$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{new} &= x + Δx = 0 + 1 = 1 \\\\\ny_{new} &= y + Δy = 1 + (-2) =-1 \\\\\nz_{new} &= z + Δz =-2 + 3 = 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $3$ | $3$ |\n| $1$ | $-1$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(1) \\approx -1\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221180 1069 "e-21665" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221180 1071 "${x^{′}(t) = x(t) - 2y(t); y^{′}(t) = y^{2}(t) - x(t)$\nFind all equilibrium points of the system of differential equations above." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221180 1072 #uuid "c953f344-77dd-5eaa-9f57-33626a392041" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221180 1074 "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = \\mathbf{f}(x,y)\n$$\n\nare the points for which\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f}(x,y)=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{x - 2y = 0; y^{2} - x = 0\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first equation for $y{:}$\n\n$$\nx - 2y = 0⇒y = \\frac{x}{2}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $y$ into the second equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{2} - x &= 0 \\\\\n(\\frac{x}{2})^{2} - x &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{x^{2}}{4} - x &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} - 4x &= 0 \\\\\nx(x - 4) &= 0 \\\\\nx &= 0, 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $y{:}$\n\n- For $x=0$, we get $y = \\dfrac{0}{2} = 0$.\n- For $x=4$, we get $y = \\dfrac{4}{2} = 2$.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221181 1069 "e-21666" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221181 1071 "Which statement correctly describes an **asymptotically stable** equilibrium point?\n\n1. Some solutions starting arbitrarily close to the equilibrium eventually move away from it.\n2. Solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t \\to \\infty$.\n3. Solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close but do not necessarily converge to the equilibrium.\n4. Solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and sometimes converge to the equilibrium as $t \\to \\infty$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221181 1072 #uuid "e7405495-e774-5508-b22b-e72d82eb4346" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221181 1074 "Let's recall the definitions of stability:\n\n- An equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ is called *asymptotically stable* if there exists a neighborhood $O$ of $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$, such that every orbit, which begins inside $O$, approaches $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ as $t \\to \\infty$.In plain words, this means that solutions which start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t \\to \\infty$.\n- An equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ is called *stable* if for any neighborhood $O$ of $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$, there exists a neighborhood $O_1 \\subseteq O$, such that every orbit, which begins inside $O_1$ at some point in time $t=t_0$, remains inside $O$ for all $t > t_0$.In plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close, but may not converge to the equilibrium.\n- An equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ is called *unstable* if it's not stable.In plain words, this means that solutions eventually move away from the equilibrium for some initial conditions arbitrarily close to it.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is II only:\n\n> Solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t \\to \\infty$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221182 1069 "e-21668" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221182 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$x^2y'' + 2y' = 4x^2, \\qquad y(1) = -1, \\qquad y'(1) = -1$\nUse Euler's Method with one step to approximate $y\\left(\\dfrac32\\right)$ and $y'\\left(\\dfrac32\\right)$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221182 1072 #uuid "62004d73-b9ca-5133-b016-e2694a55211c" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221182 1074 "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\ny'' = f(x,y,y'), \\qquad y(a) = c, \\quad y'(a) = d\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{y^{′} = z,\\begin{vmatrix}y(a) = c, \\\\ z^{′} = f(x, y, z)\\end{vmatrix}z(a) = d\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate $y''$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2}y^{″} + 2y^{′} &= 4x^{2} \\\\\nx^{2}y^{″} &= 4x^{2} - 2y^{′} \\\\\ny^{″} &= \\frac{4x^{2} - 2y^{′}}{x^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable\n\n$$\nz = y', z' = y''\n$$\n\nin the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\nz' = \\dfrac{4x^2 - 2z}{x^2}\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is\n\n$$\nz(1) = y'(1) = -1\n$$\n\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{y^{′} = z,\\begin{vmatrix}y(1) =-1, \\\\ z^{′} = \\frac{4x^{2} - 2z}{x^{2}},\\end{vmatrix}z(1) =-1\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nSince we want to find the values of $y$ and $y'=z$ at\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac32\n$$\n\nwe will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac32 - 1 = \\dfrac12\n$$\n\nFirst, we place the initial condition data in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $-1$ |  |  | $-1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both $y'$ and $z'$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= z =-1 \\\\\nz^{′} &= \\frac{4x^{2} - 2z}{x^{2}} \\\\\n&= (4 \\cdot 1^{2} - 2 \\cdot (-1))/(1^{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{4 + 2}{1} \\\\\n&= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ |  | $-1$ | $6$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $\\Delta y$ and $\\Delta z$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy &= y^{′} \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= (-1) \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\\nΔz &= z^{′} \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= 6 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $-\\dfrac12$ | $-1$ | $6$ | $3$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x$, $y$, and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $\\Delta x$ to $x$, $\\Delta y$ to $y$, and $\\Delta z$ to $z$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{new} &= x + Δx = 1 + \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{3}{2} \\\\\ny_{new} &= y + Δy =-1 + (-\\frac{1}{2}) =-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\\nz_{new} &= z + Δz =-1 + 3 = 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $-\\dfrac12$ | $-1$ | $6$ | $3$ |\n| $\\dfrac32$ | $-\\dfrac32$ |  |  | $2$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny\\left(\\dfrac32\\right) \\approx -\\dfrac32\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny'\\left(\\dfrac32\\right) = z\\left(\\dfrac32\\right) \\approx 2\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221183 1069 "e-21669" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221183 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$2x y'' - (y')^2 + 4y = 0, \\qquad y(1) = -1, \\quad y'(1) = 2$\nUse Euler's Method with two steps to approximate $y(3)$ and $y'(3)$." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221183 1072 #uuid "25d6bf3b-282e-5c8b-b665-923dd602849d" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221183 1074 "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\ny'' = f(x,y,y'), \\qquad y(a) = c, \\quad y'(a) = d\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{y^{′} = z,\\begin{vmatrix}y(a) = c, \\\\ z^{′} = f(x, y, z)\\end{vmatrix}z(a) = d\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate $y''$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2xy^{″} - (y^{′})^{2} + 4y &= 0 \\\\\n2xy^{″} &= (y^{′})^{2} - 4y \\\\\ny^{″} &= ((y^{′})^{2} - 4y)/(2x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z = y'$, $z' = y''$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\nz' = \\dfrac{z^2 - 4y}{2x}\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is\n\n$$\nz(1) = y'(1) = 2\n$$\n\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{y^{′} = z,\\begin{vmatrix}y(1) =-1, \\\\ z^{′} = \\frac{z^{2} - 4y}{2x},\\end{vmatrix}z(1) = 2\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nFirst, note that we are given the values of $y$ and $z=y'$ at $x=1$ and asked to find their values at $x=3$. The distance from $x=1$ to $x=3$ is $2$, and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{3 - 1}2 = 1\n$$\n\nNow, we'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=-1$ and $z(1)=2$.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $-1$ |  |  | $2$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nWe compute $y'$ and $\\Delta y$, $z'$ and $\\Delta z$ (using the values of $x$, $y$, and $z$ in the first row), and the values of $x_\\text{new}$, $y_\\text{new}$, and $z_\\text{new}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= z = 2 \\\\\nz^{′} &= \\frac{z^{2} - 4y}{2x} = (2^{2} - 4(-1))/(2 \\cdot 1) = 4 \\\\\nΔy &= y^{′} \\cdot Δx = 2 \\cdot 1 = 2 \\\\\nΔz &= z^{′} \\cdot Δx = 4 \\cdot 1 = 4 \\\\\nx_{new} &= x + Δx = 1 + 1 = 2 \\\\\ny_{new} &= y + Δy =-1 + 2 = 1 \\\\\nz_{new} &= z + Δz = 2 + 4 = 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ | $4$ | $4$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ |  |  | $6$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nWe compute $y'$ and $\\Delta y$, $z'$ and $\\Delta z$ (using the values of $x$, $y$, and $z$ in the second row), and the values of $x_\\text{new}$, $y_\\text{new}$, and $z_\\text{new}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= z = 6 \\\\\nz^{′} &= \\frac{z^{2} - 4y}{2x} = \\frac{6^{2} - 4 \\cdot 1}{2 \\cdot 2} = 8 \\\\\nΔy &= y^{′} \\cdot Δx = 6 \\cdot 1 = 6 \\\\\nΔz &= z^{′} \\cdot Δx = 8 \\cdot 1 = 8 \\\\\nx_{new} &= x + Δx = 2 + 1 = 3 \\\\\ny_{new} &= y + Δy = 1 + 6 = 7 \\\\\nz_{new} &= z + Δz = 6 + 8 = 14\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ | $4$ | $4$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $6$ | $6$ | $6$ | $8$ | $8$ |\n| $3$ | $7$ |  |  | $14$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(3) \\approx 7\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny'(3) = z(3) \\approx 14\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221184 1069 "e-21671" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221184 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y' = 2x - y, \\qquad y(1) = 0$\nUse the trapezoidal method with one step to approximate $y\\!\\left(\\dfrac43\\right)$, rounded to two decimal places." 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221184 1072 #uuid "5399454b-0691-5cb3-884f-26af4ca52f62" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221184 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) = c\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = \\dfrac12(y' + y'_\\text{new}) \\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\ny'_\\text{new} = f(x_\\text{new}, y_\\text{new})\n$$\n\nevaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac43\n$$\n\nwe will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac43 - 1 = \\dfrac13\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $\\Delta y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy &= \\frac{1}{2}(y^{′} + y_{new}^{′}) \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(2x - y + (2x_{new} - y_{new})) \\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6}(2x - y + 2x_{new} - y_{new}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6}(2x - y + 2x_{new}) - \\frac{1}{6}y_{new}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{new} &= y + Δy \\\\\ny_{new} &= y + \\frac{1}{6}(2x - y + 2x_{new}) - \\frac{1}{6}y_{new} \\\\\ny_{new} &= \\frac{1}{6}(2x + 2x_{new} + 5y) - \\frac{1}{6}y_{new}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for $y_\\text{new}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{7}{6}y_{new} &= \\frac{1}{6}(2x + 2x_{new} + 5y) \\\\\ny_{new} &= \\frac{1}{7}(2x + 2x_{new} + 5y)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is\n\n$$\nx_\\text{new} = 1 + \\dfrac13 = \\dfrac43\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{new} &= \\frac{1}{7}(2x + 2x_{new} + 5y) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{7}(2 \\cdot 1 + 2 \\cdot \\frac{4}{3} + 5 \\cdot 0) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{3} \\\\\n&\\approx 0.67\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny\\!\\left(\\dfrac43\\right) \\approx 0.67\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045585 true] [17592186221185 106